var tafsirData_37 = {
  "1": "“By those angels lined up in ranks.” This verse opens a new chapter with an oath, a solemn swearing by the angels who are lined up in ranks. In the Quranic style, an oath draws attention to what follows and affirms its importance. By swearing by the angels arranged in ordered ranks, God elevates our attention to the unseen realm and prepares us for a significant declaration about to be made.\n\nThe image of angels “lined up in ranks” is powerful. It conveys order, discipline, and readiness. The angels, God’s unseen servants, are not a disordered crowd but an organized host, arranged in ranks like a disciplined army or a solemn assembly. This order reflects the nature of their service: they carry out God’s commands with precision, without confusion or hesitation. The ranks suggest both the multitude of the angels and the perfect coordination of their service.\n\nThe mention of angels at the opening of this chapter is significant. The chapter that follows contains accounts of the prophets, the unseen realms, and the realities of the Hereafter. By opening with an oath by the angels, the chapter directs our attention to the unseen world that lies behind the visible, the realm where God’s will is carried out by His angelic servants. The visible world, with its human affairs, is set within the larger context of an unseen order in which the angels play their appointed roles.\n\nThe oath itself, the act of swearing by the angels, indicates their importance and their honorable station. God does not swear by trivial things. To swear by the angels is to affirm their significance in the divine order and to invite the listener to reflect on the unseen realm they inhabit and serve. The oath lends weight to the message that follows, binding the listener’s attention to the declaration about to be made.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to lift our eyes to the unseen realm and to recognize the order and discipline of God’s angelic servants. It invites us to reflect on the ranks of angels who carry out God’s will in the unseen world, to approach the message of this chapter with the reverence that an oath demands, and to recognize that the visible world of our experience is surrounded and upheld by an unseen order in which the angels, lined up in their ranks, serve the Lord of all things with perfect obedience and coordination.",
  "2": "“And those who diligently drive the clouds.” This verse continues the oath, swearing by another group of angels: those who drive the clouds. The image is vivid and dynamic. Angels are described as actively driving the clouds across the sky, guiding them to their appointed destinations. Behind the familiar phenomenon of moving clouds lies, according to this verse, the unseen activity of angelic agents carrying out God’s command.\n\nThe significance of this image lies in what it reveals about the relationship between the seen and the unseen. The clouds, which we observe with our physical eyes, are driven, according to the verse, by beings we cannot see. The natural world, in the Quranic view, is not a self-operating machine but a realm in which unseen agents carry out the will of the Creator. The movement of the clouds, which science describes in terms of winds and atmospheric pressure, is presented here as the work of angels acting on God’s command.\n\nThe word “diligently” emphasizes the care and attention of the angelic drivers. They do not move the clouds carelessly or randomly but with diligence, guiding them precisely where God wills. This diligence ensures that the rain falls where it is needed, that the clouds serve their purpose in the divine plan, that the provision of water reaches the lands and peoples for whom it is intended. The unseen activity of the angels serves the seen needs of creation.\n\nThis verse, following the oath by the angels in ranks, extends our vision of the unseen realm. Not only are there angels arranged in orderly ranks, but there are angels actively at work in the natural world, driving the clouds, and presumably carrying out many other tasks in the service of God’s will. The unseen realm is not static but active, populated by beings who continuously do the work assigned to them by their Lord.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to see the natural world as a realm of unseen activity and to recognize the angelic agents at work behind familiar phenomena. It invites us to look at the moving clouds and to remember the diligent angels who, unseen, drive them according to God’s command, and to recognize in this unseen activity the care and precision with which God governs His creation. The verse calls us to a vision of the world that includes both the seen and the unseen, acknowledging that the visible phenomena of nature are upheld and guided by an unseen order of obedient service.",
  "3": "“And those who recite the Reminder!” This verse completes the threefold oath that opens the chapter, swearing by a third group: those who recite the Reminder. The Reminder refers to the revelation, the word of God, and those who recite it are understood to be the angels who convey the revelation, or alternatively, in some interpretations, the reciters among humanity. The oath honors the recitation of God’s word as a significant act worthy of being sworn by.\n\nThe identification of the reciters as angels connects this verse with the previous two. The angels in ranks, the angels who drive the clouds, and the angels who recite the Reminder form a threefold picture of angelic service: order, activity in nature, and the conveying of revelation. The angels are involved in every level of God’s governance: the organization of the unseen realm, the operation of the natural world, and the communication of divine truth to humanity.\n\nThe recitation of the Reminder is a profoundly important function. The revelation, the word of God, is the means by which humanity is guided, warned, and called to the worship of the One God. The angels who recite or convey this Reminder serve as the link between the divine source and the human recipients. Their role in the transmission of revelation underscores the honor in which God’s word is held: it is conveyed by His noblest servants, the angels.\n\nThe word “Reminder” is significant. The revelation is not new information about an unknown God but a reminder of truths that human beings tend to forget: the truth of God’s oneness, the reality of accountability, the path of righteousness. The angels recite this Reminder, calling humanity back to what it knows but neglects. The recitation is an act of mercy, alerting forgetful human beings to the realities that matter most.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to honor the recitation of God’s word and to recognize the unseen agents involved in its conveyance. It invites us to receive the Reminder, the revelation, with the reverence that befits a word conveyed by angels and originating from God, and to allow the Reminder to do its work of calling us back to the truths we have forgotten. The verse calls us to be among those who not only hear the Reminder but take it to heart, allowing the recited word, whether conveyed by angels or by human reciters, to remind us of our Lord and to guide us back to the path that leads to Him.",
  "4": "“Surely your God is One!” This verse, coming immediately after the oath by the angels, makes the central declaration of the chapter and of the whole Quran: your God is One. The affirmation of divine oneness, known in Arabic as “tawhid,” is the foundation of the Islamic message. There is only one God, one Creator, one Lord, one object of worship. The certainty is marked by the word “surely,” and the direct address, “your God,” makes the truth personal to every listener.\n\nThe significance of this declaration cannot be overstated. The whole of the Quran’s teaching rests on the oneness of God. If God is one, then worship belongs to Him alone, guidance comes from Him alone, and accountability is to Him alone. Every other truth flows from this foundational one. The angels, sworn by in the previous verses, serve this one God. The clouds are driven by His command. The Reminder is His word. All of reality is unified under the lordship of the One God.\n\nThe declaration stands against the polytheism that was prevalent in the Prophet’s time and that recurs in every age. The polytheists spread their devotion among many gods; the Quran insists on the oneness of the true God. The contrast is not merely numerical, one versus many, but qualitative: the one God is the Creator and Lord of all, while the many gods are creations or inventions with no real divinity. To worship the many is to mistake the creature for the Creator; to worship the One is to align devotion with reality.\n\nThe placement of this declaration after the oath by the angels is significant. The angels, noble and powerful as they are, are servants of the One God. They do not share His divinity; they carry out His will. By swearing by the angels and then declaring God’s oneness, the chapter establishes both the reality of the unseen realm and the supremacy of the One who rules it. The angels serve; God alone is served.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to affirm and live by the oneness of God. It invites us to recognise that our God is one, to direct all worship, trust, and devotion to Him alone, and to allow the truth of His oneness to unify our lives under His lordship. The verse challenges every form of divided devotion, every tendency to spread our worship or reliance among many objects, and calls us to the wholeness that comes from directing all that we are to the One who alone is God.",
  "5": "“He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, and the Lord of all points of sunrise.” This verse expands on the declaration of God’s oneness by describing the scope of His lordship. He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, which is to say, of the entire cosmos, all that exists. And He is the Lord of all points of sunrise, a phrase that encompasses the whole earth, from which the sun is seen to rise at different points throughout the year.\n\nThe comprehensiveness of this lordship is the point. God is not the Lord of one realm or one people but of everything. The heavens, the earth, and all that lies between them, the vast reaches of space and the intricate details of earthly life, all fall under His lordship. There is nothing in existence that is outside His dominion. This universal lordship is the basis for the oneness affirmed in the previous verse: because God is Lord of all, He alone deserves worship.\n\nThe phrase “all points of sunrise” is a beautiful way of expressing universality. The sun rises at different points on the horizon throughout the year, and from different vantage points on the earth, the sunrise appears in different directions. By declaring God the Lord of all points of sunrise, the verse encompasses the whole earth, every place from which the sun is seen to rise. No corner of the earth lies outside His lordship.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the previous one is direct. The oneness of God is demonstrated by the universality of His lordship. If He is Lord of the heavens, the earth, and all points of sunrise, then there is no room for other lords. The polytheists who assigned different gods to different realms, one for the sky, one for the earth, one for this people or that, were dividing a lordship that belongs wholly to the One God. The verse reunifies what they divided.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to recognise the universal lordship of God and to live under it. It invites us to see the heavens, the earth, and every sunrise as domains of the one Lord’s rule, to acknowledge His authority over every aspect of existence, and to direct our worship to Him alone because He alone is Lord of all. The verse challenges every attempt to divide God’s lordship or to assign parts of our lives or our world to other allegiances, calling us instead to the unified recognition that the One God is Lord of everything, from the highest heaven to the farthest point of sunrise.",
  "6": "“Indeed, We have adorned the lowest heaven with the stars for decoration.” This verse points to the stars as a sign of God’s creative artistry. The lowest heaven, the sky that we see above us, has been adorned with stars. The word “adorned” is significant: the stars are not merely functional but beautiful, placed in the sky as a decoration, a display of the Creator’s sense of beauty and His desire to fill His creation with splendor.\n\nThe stars, seen on a clear night, are one of the most awe-inspiring sights available to human beings. The vast number, the brightness, the patterns, the sense of immense depth and distance, all combine to produce wonder. The verse attributes this beauty to God’s deliberate adornment. He placed the stars in the sky as a decoration, turning the night from a mere absence of sun into a canvas of glittering lights.\n\nThe idea that the stars are “for decoration” reveals something about God’s character. A purely utilitarian creator might have made a functional sky without beauty. But God adorned the heaven with stars, adding splendor that serves no merely practical purpose but exists for the delight and wonder of those who behold it. The beauty of the night sky is a gift, a gratuitous display of the Creator’s generosity and artistry.\n\nThis verse follows the declaration of God’s oneness and universal lordship. The same God who is Lord of the heavens and the earth is the One who adorned the lowest heaven with stars. His lordship is not only powerful but beautiful. He governs the cosmos with wisdom and adorns it with splendor. The stars are both a sign of His power, in their vast number and precise arrangement, and of His artistry, in their beauty.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to look at the night sky with new eyes and to recognise in the stars the adornment of the Creator. It invites us to behold the stars not merely as astronomical objects but as a decoration placed by God for our wonder, to let their beauty stir our gratitude and awe, and to recognize in the adorned heaven the character of a God who fills His creation with splendor because He delights in beauty and desires to share that delight with the creatures made in His image.",
  "7": "“And for protection from every rebellious devil.” This verse adds a second purpose to the stars of the lowest heaven: they serve as a protection against every rebellious devil. The stars, besides being a decoration, have a defensive function in the unseen realm, guarding the heavens from the attempts of the rebellious spirits, the devils, to intrude or to eavesdrop on the affairs of the higher realms.\n\nThe concept of rebellious devils refers to the jinn and spirits who have defied God, following Satan in his rebellion. These beings, though unseen, are active, and according to the Quran, some of them attempt to ascend to the heavens to listen to the conversations of the angels, perhaps to steal information or to interfere. The stars serve as a protection against such attempts, a defensive arrangement that keeps the rebellious from accessing what is not theirs.\n\nThe combination of the two purposes of the stars, decoration and protection, reflects the dual nature of God’s creation. The same stars that beautify the sky for human beings also serve a defensive function in the unseen realm. Creation is not only beautiful but orderly and protected. God has arranged the cosmos not only for delight but for security, guarding the realms that need guarding while adorning the sky that human beings behold.\n\nThe mention of “every rebellious devil” emphasizes the comprehensiveness of the protection. No rebellious spirit, however determined, can breach the defense that God has established. The stars guard against all of them, ensuring that the heavens remain protected from intrusion. The arrangement reflects God’s complete control over both the seen and the unseen realms.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a glimpse into the unseen dimensions of creation and a reminder of God’s comprehensive governance. It invites us to recognize that the world is more complex than what we see, that there are unseen beings and unseen conflicts, and that God has established protections, using even the stars, to maintain the order of His creation. The verse calls us to trust the God who guards the heavens with the stars to also guard our lives, confident that the same Lord who protects the higher realms from every rebellious devil protects His faithful servants from the harms, seen and unseen, that would seek to reach them.",
  "8": "“They cannot listen to the highest assembly of angels for they are pelted from every side.” This verse describes the effectiveness of the protection mentioned in the previous verse. The rebellious devils cannot listen to the highest assembly, the gathering of the angels in the highest heavens. Their attempts to eavesdrop are thwarted because they are pelted from every side. The image is of beings trying to ascend and listen, only to be met with a barrage from all directions that drives them back.\n\nThe “highest assembly” refers to the angelic gathering in the presence of God, where the affairs of creation are discussed and God’s commands are conveyed. The devils, in their rebellion and curiosity, seek to listen in on this assembly, perhaps to gain forbidden knowledge or to interfere with the communication between God and His angels. But their attempts fail, because the protection established by God, represented by the stars, prevents them from listening.\n\nThe image of being “pelted from every side” conveys the totality of the defense. The devils are not merely blocked from one direction; they are struck from every direction, leaving them no avenue of approach. Whatever way they try to ascend or listen, they are met with the same defensive barrage. The comprehensiveness of the pelting reflects the comprehensiveness of God’s protection: there is no gap, no weakness, no unprotected side through which the rebellious can slip.\n\nThis verse reveals the ongoing conflict in the unseen realm between the obedient angels, who serve God, and the rebellious devils, who oppose Him. The conflict is real, but it is not equal. The devils are continually thwarted, unable to achieve their aims, driven back by the defenses God has established. The obedience of the angels and the rebellion of the devils are met with very different outcomes: the angels serve in freedom and honor, while the devils strive in futility and frustration.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the reality of the unseen conflict and of the absolute supremacy of God’s order over all rebellion. It invites us to recognize that the rebellious spirits, however active, are ultimately powerless, unable even to listen to the highest assembly because of the defenses God has established. The verse calls us to trust the God whose protection is so comprehensive that even the devils cannot breach it, and to align ourselves with the obedient rather than the rebellious, knowing that the side of obedience is the side of a defense that cannot fail and a sovereignty that cannot be opposed.",
  "9": "“Fiercely driven away. And they will suffer an everlasting torment.” This verse continues the description of the fate of the rebellious devils. They are fiercely driven away, expelled with force from the realms they sought to penetrate. The word “fiercely” emphasizes the violence and completeness of their expulsion. They are not merely turned back politely but driven away with overwhelming force, leaving no doubt about the futility of their attempts.\n\nThe image of being driven away, combined with the pelting from every side described in the previous verse, presents a picture of total defeat. The devils who sought to ascend and listen are instead driven down and away, their ambitions frustrated, their dignity stripped. The very heavens they sought to invade become the arena of their humiliation. The rebellion that lifted them up in pride is met with a force that casts them down.\n\n“And they will suffer an everlasting torment.” The verse then adds the ultimate consequence: an everlasting torment. The driving away is not the final punishment but a present experience of a fate that will culminate in eternal suffering. The devils, having rebelled against God and sought to interfere with His order, will face a torment that has no end. The word “everlasting” underscores the finality and permanence of their punishment.\n\nThe connection between the present driving away and the future everlasting torment is significant. The expulsion the devils experience now, when they try to listen, is a foretaste of the permanent driving away that awaits them in the torment to come. Their present frustration anticipates their future ruin. The rebellion that brings them temporary humiliation in the present will bring them eternal suffering in the future.\n\nThis verse serves as a warning, not only about the fate of the devils but about the consequences of rebellion against God in general. The devils are the extreme case, the beings who have most thoroughly rejected God’s authority, and their fate reveals the end toward which all rebellion tends. Those who, like the devils, set themselves against God, will find themselves driven away and ultimately subjected to a torment that does not end.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering reminder of the consequences of rebellion against God and of the absolute futility of opposing His order. It invites us to recognize that rebellion, however bold it may seem, ends in being driven away and in everlasting torment, and to choose instead the path of obedience. The verse calls us to align ourselves with the obedient angels rather than the rebellious devils, knowing that the one path leads to honor and the other to a fierce driving away and a torment that lasts forever, a fate so terrible that even its present foretaste, in the frustrated attempts of the devils, should warn every soul away from the rebellion that earns it.",
  "10": "“But whoever manages to stealthily eavesdrop is instantly pursued by a piercing flare.” This verse adds a detail to the description of the devils’ attempts to listen to the highest assembly. Some of them, it seems, manage by stealth to get close enough to hear a little, slipping through the defenses for a moment. But even these are not allowed to escape with what they have heard. They are instantly pursued by a piercing flare, a shooting star or meteor that strikes them and drives them away.\n\nThe image of the piercing flare is vivid. The traditional understanding connects this with the shooting stars that humans see streaking across the night sky: these, according to the verse, are the flares sent in pursuit of eavesdropping devils. The natural phenomenon, beautiful and striking to the human observer, has, in the unseen realm, a defensive purpose, striking the spirits who try to steal a hearing of the heavenly assembly.\n\nThe word “instantly” emphasizes the speed of the response. The moment a devil manages to eavesdrop, the pursuit begins. There is no delay, no opportunity to escape with the stolen information, no chance to use what was overheard. The defense is as swift as it is certain. The piercing flare is on the eavesdropper before he can profit from his theft.\n\nThe word “piercing” conveys the effectiveness of the flare. It does not merely frighten or warn; it pierces, striking and harming the eavesdropper. The devils who try to listen do not escape unscathed. Their attempted theft brings them injury, adding to the frustration and humiliation of their failed rebellion. The heavens are not only defended but defended with consequences for those who try to breach the defense.\n\nThis verse completes the picture of the unseen conflict that began with the stars as protection. The stars adorn the sky, guard against the devils, and serve as the flares that pursue any who manage to eavesdrop. The whole arrangement reveals the comprehensiveness and the effectiveness of God’s defense of the heavens. The rebellious spirits are met at every stage, from their initial attempt to ascend to their stealthy efforts to steal a hearing, with defenses they cannot overcome.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a further glimpse into the unseen order and a reminder of the thoroughness of God’s governance. It invites us to recognize that even the stealthiest opposition to God’s order is met with swift and piercing response, and that the natural phenomena we observe, like the shooting stars, may have unseen dimensions and purposes of which we are unaware. The verse calls us to trust the God whose defenses are so complete that even the most stealthy intrusion is instantly repelled, and to take warning from the fate of those who, like the eavesdropping devils, seek to oppose or interfere with an order they cannot breach.",
  "11": "“So ask them, O Prophet, which is harder to create: them or other marvels of Our creation? Indeed, We created them from a sticky clay.” This verse poses a question to the disbelievers that exposes the smallness of their self-regard and the greatness of God’s power. The Prophet is told to ask them which is harder to create: they themselves, or the other marvels of God’s creation, such as the heavens, the angels, the vast cosmos? The question expects the answer that the marvels are far greater, and that the creation of human beings, by comparison, is a small thing.\n\nThe question is humbling. The disbelievers, who doubted the resurrection and challenged God, are asked to consider their own creation in the context of the whole cosmos. They were created from clay, a humble material, while the heavens and the earth, the stars and the angels, are vastly more magnificent. If God could create these marvels, the creation and re-creation of human beings is a simple matter. The question strips away the disbelievers’ inflated sense of their own importance.\n\n“Indeed, We created them from a sticky clay.” The verse then states the material from which human beings were created: sticky clay. The clay that sticks together, that can be molded and shaped, is the humble origin of the human being. From this earthy substance, God fashioned the creature who now has the audacity to challenge his Maker. The reminder of the humble origin is meant to produce humility and to expose the absurdity of the challenge.\n\nThe contrast between the humble origin and the arrogant challenge is the point. A being made from clay, fashioned by God’s power from the dust of the earth, has no ground for pride or defiance. The disbelievers who doubt the resurrection are, in effect, doubting whether the God who made them from clay can remake them after death. The question and the reminder together expose the weakness of their position and the strength of God’s power.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to humility grounded in the recognition of our origins and a strengthening of faith in God’s power. It invites us to remember that we were created from clay, that our existence is wholly the gift of God’s creative power, and that the same power can easily raise us after death. The verse challenges the pride that would make us think ourselves too significant to have been easily created or too important to be re-created, calling us instead to the humility that recognizes our smallness before the marvels of God’s creation and the greatness of the power that made us all.",
  "12": "“In fact, you are astonished by their denial, while they ridicule you.” This verse captures the asymmetry between the Prophet and the disbelievers in their responses to each other. The Prophet is astonished by their denial, genuinely amazed that people could reject such clear truth. The disbelievers, for their part, ridicule him, treating his message with contempt. The two reactions reveal the different conditions of the hearts involved.\n\nThe Prophet’s astonishment is understandable. He had brought clear guidance, supported by evident signs, offered with sincerity and concern. The truth of his message was plain to any honest observer. That people could deny such truth amazed him. His astonishment was not the shock of someone who expected rejection but the genuine surprise of someone who could not comprehend how the evident could be refused.\n\nThe disbelievers’ ridicule stands in stark contrast. Rather than being astonished by the truth, they ridiculed the one who brought it. Mockery, as we have seen throughout the Quran, is a common response of those who cannot answer the truth: rather than engage it seriously, they laugh at it. The ridicule was a way of dismissing the message without dealing with its content, a substitute for the thoughtful consideration the truth deserved.\n\nThe contrast between astonishment and ridicule reveals the spiritual condition of each party. The Prophet’s astonishment reflects his love of truth and his sincere desire for the people’s good; he could not understand their refusal because truth and goodness were so precious to him. The disbelievers’ ridicule reflects their hardened hearts and their unwillingness to consider anything that challenged their preferences; they laughed because serious engagement would have required change.\n\nThis verse offers a measure of comfort to the Prophet by acknowledging his experience. His astonishment at their denial was real and understandable, and the verse validates it. At the same time, the verse places the disbelievers’ ridicule in its proper light, as the response of hardened hearts rather than a reasonable reply to his message. The contrast itself is a form of vindication: the Prophet’s astonishment honors the truth, while the disbelievers’ ridicule exposes their condition.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the different ways people respond to the truth and a comfort for those who are amazed by the denial they encounter. It invites us to share the Prophet’s astonishment at the refusal of clear truth, to refuse the easy contempt of ridicule, and to recognize that mockery of the truth reveals more about the mockers than about the message. The verse calls us to honor the truth with serious engagement, to be patient with those who ridicule, and to find comfort in the knowledge that the asymmetry between sincere astonishment and contemptuous ridicule has been the experience of truth-bearers throughout history.",
  "13": "“When they are reminded, they are never mindful.” This verse describes a defining characteristic of the disbelievers: their refusal to become mindful when reminded. The reminder, in the Quranic sense, is the call to remember God, the truth, and the accountability that awaits. The disbelievers received such reminders continually, through the Prophet’s preaching and through the signs scattered throughout creation. Yet, despite being reminded, they never became mindful.\n\nThe word “never” is emphatic. It is not that they sometimes failed to be mindful, as even the best believers do, but that they never became mindful. Their response to every reminder was the same: heedlessness. The reminder, however clear or compelling, produced no change in their awareness or conduct. This consistent failure reveals a settled condition of the heart, a stubborn resistance to the awakening the reminder was meant to produce.\n\nThe connection between being reminded and being mindful is significant. A reminder is meant to produce mindfulness, that alert, aware, God-conscious state in which a person lives in the recognition of divine reality. The disbelievers received the first, the reminder, but never attained the second, the mindfulness. The gap between the two reveals the failure of their response: the reminder was offered, but it did not accomplish its purpose, because their hearts refused to receive it.\n\nThe word “mindful” in the Quran carries a rich meaning. It refers to the state of “taqwa,” the reverent awareness of God that guards a person’s conduct. To be mindful is to live in the consciousness of God’s presence, His knowledge, and His judgment, allowing that consciousness to shape one’s choices. The disbelievers, despite being reminded of all this, never attained such mindfulness. Their lives remained governed by heedlessness rather than by the awareness the reminders were meant to awaken.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the danger of being reminded without becoming mindful and a call to ensure that the reminders we receive actually produce their intended effect. It invites us to examine our own response to the reminders that come to us, through revelation, through conscience, through the signs of creation, and to ask whether they are making us mindful or whether, like the disbelievers, we hear them without being changed. The verse challenges us to be people in whom the reminder produces its fruit, allowing the call to remember to awaken in us the living mindfulness that honors God and orders our lives according to the truth we have been reminded of.",
  "14": "“And whenever they see a sign, they make fun of it.” This verse describes another aspect of the disbelievers’ response: their mockery of the signs. A sign, in the Quran, is any event, phenomenon, or message that points to God and to the truth. The disbelievers encountered many signs, in the natural world, in the revelation, and in the Prophet’s message. Yet their consistent response to these signs was mockery. They did not consider them, reflect on them, or allow them to guide their thinking. They made fun of them.\n\nThe word “whenever” emphasizes the consistency of the response. It was not occasional mockery, reserved for some signs, but a uniform reaction to every sign. Whatever form the sign took, the disbelievers met it with the same contemptuous laughter. The consistency reveals a settled disposition: they had decided in advance to reject whatever pointed to God, and mockery was their chosen method of dismissal.\n\nMockery as a response to signs is revealing. A person who seriously engaged with a sign might be convinced or might raise objections, but either way they would treat the sign as something worth considering. The mocker, by contrast, refuses to engage. By laughing at the sign, the mocker avoids the need to deal with its content. Mockery is a defense mechanism, a way of dismissing something threatening without confronting it. The disbelievers used mockery to protect themselves from the challenge the signs presented.\n\nThe tragedy of this response is that the signs were given for the disbelievers’ benefit. The signs were meant to guide them, to awaken them, to lead them to God. By mocking the signs, the disbelievers rejected the very means of their own salvation. They turned away from the lights that could have illuminated their path, preferring the darkness of unbelief to the illumination the signs offered.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against the response of mockery and a call to take the signs seriously. It invites us to examine our own response to the signs that come to us, whether in revelation, in creation, or in the events of our lives, and to ensure that we meet them with thoughtful consideration rather than with contemptuous laughter. The verse challenges us to be people who allow the signs to do their work of guiding and awakening, rather than people who, like the disbelievers, make fun of the very things given to lead us to the truth, and who thereby cut ourselves off from the guidance the signs were meant to provide.",
  "15": "“Saying, This is nothing but pure magic.” This verse records the specific words with which the disbelievers dismissed the signs. They called them “pure magic.” The label of magic was a way of acknowledging the power of the signs while denying their divine origin. The disbelievers could not deny that something remarkable was happening, but rather than attributing it to God, they explained it as sorcery, a human trick or a supernatural feat with no divine authority.\n\nThe accusation of magic was a convenient evasion. By labeling the signs as magic, the disbelievers placed them in a category that could be dismissed without engagement. Magic, in their understanding, was a human or demonic craft, impressive but fraudulent, powerful but illegitimate. To call the signs magic was to deny that they came from God and to justify their rejection. The label allowed the disbelievers to refuse the signs while appearing to have a reason for their refusal.\n\nThe word “pure” emphasizes the totality of the dismissal. The signs were not partly magic and partly something else; they were, according to the disbelievers, “pure magic,” nothing but sorcery. The absoluteness of the label reflects the absoluteness of their refusal. They had decided to reject, and they chose a label that would justify complete rejection.\n\nThe irony of the accusation is profound. The signs, which pointed to the power and truth of God, were attributed to magic, a counterfeit of true power. The disbelievers mistook the divine for the demonic, the genuine for the fraudulent. Their accusation said more about their own confusion than about the nature of the signs. Unable to recognize the hand of God, they explained His works as the tricks of a magician.\n\nThis verse connects to the broader pattern of the disbelievers’ responses: they are reminded but never mindful, they see signs but make fun of them, and they explain the signs away as magic. The pattern reveals a settled determination to reject, expressed through various evasions. Each evasion, whether heedlessness, mockery, or the label of magic, serves the same underlying purpose: to avoid the engagement with the truth that would require change.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against the evasions we may use to dismiss the truth and a call to honest engagement with the signs of God. It invites us to examine whether we, like the disbelievers, use labels and explanations to avoid dealing with what the signs actually are, and to approach the signs with the openness and honesty that allow them to be recognized for what they are: pointers to the power and truth of the God who gave them. The verse challenges us to resist the convenient evasions that protect us from the truth, and to let the signs speak to us as they were meant to, leading us to the One they reveal.",
  "16": "“When we are dead and reduced to dust and bones, will we really be resurrected?” This verse records the specific objection of the disbelievers to the doctrine of resurrection. They pointed to the complete decomposition of the dead, the reduction of the body to dust and bones, and asked whether such thoroughly decayed remains could really be brought back to life. The question was meant as a refutation: since the dead become dust and bones, resurrection is impossible.\n\nThe objection rests on a materialist assumption: that the only forces at work are the natural processes of decay, and that what has been broken down cannot be reassembled. The disbelievers looked at the undeniable fact of decomposition and concluded that re-creation was beyond possibility. Their reasoning ignored the power of the One who created from nothing, the power that could surely re-create from decay.\n\nThe form of the question, “will we really be resurrected?” conveys incredulity. The disbelievers found the idea absurd, a fantasy that defied the evident reality of death and decay. Their tone was dismissive, treating the resurrection as too ridiculous to merit serious consideration. The incredulity reflects their failure to grasp the power of God and their fixation on the visible processes of the natural world.\n\nThe phrase “reduced to dust and bones” emphasizes the completeness of the decay the disbelievers pointed to. They were not talking about a body recently dead but about remains that had fully decomposed, scattered, absorbed into the earth. From such total dissolution, they insisted, reassembly was impossible. The more complete the decay, the more impossible the resurrection seemed to their materialist reasoning.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the objection that has been raised against resurrection in every age and of the flawed reasoning behind it. It invites us to recognize that the objection rests on a forgetfulness of God’s creative power, that the God who made us from dust can surely reassemble us after we return to dust, and that the incredulity of the materialist is grounded in a too-small view of what is possible. The verse challenges us to ground our certainty about the resurrection not in the visible processes of decay but in the proven power of the Creator, who made us once from nothing and who will raise us again, however completely our bodies may have returned to the dust and bones from which they came.",
  "17": "“And our forefathers as well?” This verse records the continuation of the disbelievers’ objection. Having questioned whether they themselves would be resurrected, they added, with evident scorn, the question of whether their forefathers would also be raised. The mention of the forefathers was meant to heighten the absurdity of the resurrection, in the disbelievers’ eyes. The forefathers had been dead far longer, their remains far more completely decayed, scattered, and lost. To raise them seemed even more impossible than raising the recently dead.\n\nThe mention of the forefathers also touched on the disbelievers’ reverence for their ancestors. They honored their forefathers and followed their ways. The idea that these honored ancestors would be raised, along with everyone else, to face judgment, was perhaps unsettling as well as seemingly absurd. It implied that the forefathers, far from being beyond accountability in the peace of death, would be brought back to answer for their lives, just like everyone else.\n\nThe cumulative force of the objection is clear. The disbelievers pointed first to their own future decomposition and then to the even more complete decay of their forefathers, building a case that resurrection, of anyone, at any stage of decomposition, was impossible. The case relied entirely on the assumption that decay is stronger than the power of God, an assumption the Quran repeatedly overturns.\n\nThe scornful tone of the question reflects the disbelievers’ confidence in their objection. They believed that by pointing to the thoroughly decayed remains of their forefathers, they had decisively refuted the doctrine of resurrection. Their confidence was misplaced, as the following verses will show, because their reasoning ignored the power and knowledge of the One who created all things from nothing.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a further exposure of the materialist objection to resurrection and of the flawed confidence behind it. It invites us to recognize that the objection, however it may be strengthened by pointing to longer-dead ancestors, rests on the same error: the assumption that decay defeats the Creator. The verse challenges us to see beyond the visible processes of death and decomposition to the power of the God who is Lord over both life and death, confident that the same power that will raise us will raise all who have died, including the forefathers of long ago, on the Day when every grave gives up its dead.",
  "18": "“Yes! And you will be fully humbled.” This verse gives the Prophet the answer to the disbelievers’ scornful questions about resurrection. Will they be raised? Will their forefathers be raised? The answer is a single, emphatic word: Yes! The resurrection is certain. The dead, however thoroughly decayed, will be raised. The forefathers, however long dead, will be brought back. The affirmation is absolute, leaving no room for the disbelievers’ doubt.\n\nThe word “Yes” is striking in its simplicity. Against the elaborate, scornful objections of the disbelievers, with their pointed questions about dust and bones and forefathers, the answer is a single word. The simplicity conveys confidence: the resurrection is so certain that it needs no elaborate defense, only a plain affirmation. The God who created all things has declared it, and His declaration settles the matter.\n\n“And you will be fully humbled.” The verse then adds a detail about the manner of the resurrection: those who denied it will be raised in a state of humiliation. The word “humbled” or “abased” conveys the reversal that the resurrection will bring. The disbelievers who strutted through life in pride, who mocked the messengers and ridiculed the doctrine of resurrection, will be raised low, their pride stripped away, their scorn silenced by the overwhelming reality they now face.\n\nThe combination of certainty and humiliation is significant. The resurrection is certain: yes, it will happen. And it will be humiliating for those who denied it: they will be fully humbled. The very doctrine they mocked will be the means of their abasement. The Day they said would never come will arrive, and they will face it not in the strength of their defiance but in the weakness of their exposed error.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a plain affirmation of the certainty of resurrection and a sober warning to those who deny it. It invites us to recognise that the resurrection is as certain as the word of God, that every human being will be raised, and that those who denied this truth will face it in humiliation rather than in defiance. The verse challenges us to believe the resurrection now, while belief can still shape our lives, so that when the Day arrives, we face it not among the humbled deniers but among those who believed and are raised to honor, having trusted the word that the disbelievers scorned but that the verse confirms with a simple, emphatic Yes.",
  "19": "“It will only take one Blast, then at once they will see it all.” This verse describes the suddenness of the resurrection. It will take only a single Blast, and at once the disbelievers will see everything. The resurrection, which they had spent their lives denying and mocking, will arrive in an instant, without warning, without preparation. The suddenness strips away every illusion of time and opportunity.\n\nThe image of a single Blast accomplishing the resurrection echoes similar statements elsewhere in the Quran. The means is minimal, a single sound, but the effect is maximal: the dead are raised, the Day begins, the reality that was denied becomes undeniable. The disproportion between the means and the effect underscores the overwhelming power of God. A single command accomplishes what the disbelievers considered impossible.\n\nThe phrase “at once they will see it all” emphasizes the immediacy and completeness of the revelation. The disbelievers will not gradually come to understand the truth; they will see it all at once, in a single moment of overwhelming clarity. Every doubt will be resolved, every objection answered, every denial exposed, in the instant when the Blast sounds and the reality they refused becomes the reality they must face.\n\nThe suddenness is both a mercy and a warning. It is a mercy in that the waiting, for those who dread the Day, will not be prolonged; it will come suddenly and be over. It is a warning in that the suddenness leaves no time for last-minute repentance or preparation. The time for preparation is now, before the Blast. Once it sounds, the opportunity is gone, and what remains is the seeing of it all, the sudden, total confrontation with the truth.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to preparedness grounded in the recognition of the resurrection’s suddenness. It invites us to recognise that the Day will arrive without warning, in a single Blast, and that the time to prepare is now, while preparation is still possible. The verse challenges us not to count on having time at the end, because the end will come suddenly, bringing with it the full revelation of everything we denied or neglected, and finding us either prepared or unprepared, with no middle ground and no second chance once the Blast has sounded and we see it all.",
  "20": "“Oh, woe to us! This is the Day of Judgment!” This verse records the cry of the disbelievers when the resurrection arrives and they see the reality they had denied. Their cry is one of despair: “Oh, woe to us!” The exclamation conveys the depth of their distress, the anguish of souls who realise, too late, that the truth they mocked is real and that they are utterly unprepared to face it.\n\nThe recognition “This is the Day of Judgment!” shows that the disbelievers now understand what they had denied. The Day they said would never come is here. The judgment they refused to believe in is upon them. Their recognition is complete and undeniable, forced by the overwhelming reality of the resurrection. They can no longer deny what they once scorned; the truth stands before them, undeniable and terrible.\n\nThe cry of woe is the sound of regret that comes too late. The disbelievers had every opportunity, in this life, to believe and prepare. They chose instead to mock and deny. Now, when the Day has arrived, they realise their error, but the realisation cannot help them. The time for the kind of belief that saves has passed. Their recognition of the Day is not a saving faith but a despairing acknowledgment of a reality they can no longer avoid.\n\nThe contrast between their former mockery and their present despair is complete. They had asked, scornfully, when the threat would come. Now it has come, and their scorn has turned to woe. They had laughed at the messengers and ridiculed the doctrine of resurrection. Now they cry out in anguish, victims of the very reality they laughed at. The reversal is total, and it is tragic.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering preview of the despair that awaits those who deny the truth and a call to avoid that fate by believing now. It invites us to recognise the Day before it arrives, to believe while belief can still save, and to prepare while preparation is still possible. The verse challenges us not to wait until the cry of woe is forced from our lips by the undeniable reality of the Day, but to acknowledge the Day now, in faith, so that when it arrives, we face it not with the despair of the deniers but with the hope of those who believed and prepared.",
  "21": "“This is the Day of Final Decision which you used to deny.” This verse records the words addressed to the disbelievers on the Day of Judgment. They are told, plainly, that this is the Day of Decision, the day on which all accounts are settled and all destinies determined. And the address adds the piercing reminder: which you used to deny. The very Day they had spent their lives refusing to believe in is now the Day they must face.\n\nThe title “Day of Decision” is significant. It is the day of final, decisive judgment, the day on which every question is resolved, every account settled, every destiny fixed. There is no further deliberation, no further opportunity, no appeal. The decisions made on this Day are final, determining the eternal fate of every soul. The title conveys the gravity and the finality of the occasion.\n\nThe phrase “which you used to deny” is the heart of the verse. It connects the present reality to the past refusal. The disbelievers had denied this Day throughout their lives, treating it as a fiction, mocking those who warned of it. Now they are confronted with the undeniable reality of what they denied, and the confrontation includes the reminder of their denial. The reminder strips away every excuse: they cannot claim ignorance, because they were warned; they cannot claim surprise, because the Day was clearly foretold.\n\nThe address serves a judicial purpose. In a court, the reading of the charges and the reminder of the defendant’s actions establish the basis for the verdict. Here, the reminder that the disbelievers used to deny this Day establishes the justice of whatever judgment follows. They were warned; they denied; the Day has come; the judgment is just. Every step of the process is fair, and the disbelievers have brought the outcome upon themselves through their own choices.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sober reminder that the Day of Decision is real and that denial does not make it go away. It invites us to recognise the Day now, before it arrives, and to cease any denial that may linger in our hearts. The verse challenges us to live as those who believe in the Day of Decision, ordering our lives by its reality, so that when it arrives, we are not addressed as those who used to deny it but welcomed as those who believed and prepared, finding in the Day not a devastating confrontation with what we refused but the fulfillment of what we trusted and the reward of the faith we lived.",
  "22": "“Gather all the wrongdoers along with their peers, and whatever they used to worship instead of Allah.” This verse records God’s command to the angels on the Day of Judgment: gather the wrongdoers, their peers, and the false objects of their worship. The command initiates the assembly of all who will face judgment, bringing together the wrongdoers themselves, those who were like them, and the idols they worshipped, so that all may be led to their common fate.\n\nThe comprehensiveness of the gathering is significant. The wrongdoers are gathered, but so are their peers, those who shared their ways and influenced them, and the objects of their worship, the false gods to whom they devoted themselves. The assembly brings together every element of the life of unbelief: the individuals, their companions in error, and their misplaced devotions. Nothing and no one involved in the rejection of the truth is overlooked.\n\nThe inclusion of “whatever they used to worship instead of Allah” is particularly striking. The false gods, the idols, the objects of misdirected devotion, are also gathered. They, too, will face the judgment, not as objects of power but as created things, helpless and exposed. The gathering of the idols alongside their worshippers completes the exposure of the whole system of false worship, bringing together the worshippers and the worshipped to face the One whom both should have served.\n\nThe phrase “instead of Allah” identifies the fundamental error of the wrongdoers. They took other objects of worship instead of God. Their wrongdoing was not isolated from their false worship but rooted in it. To worship something instead of God is the foundational wrong, the error from which many other wrongs flow. The gathering brings together all the consequences of this foundational error.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the comprehensiveness of the gathering on the Day of Judgment and a warning about the company we keep and the objects of our devotion. It invites us to recognise that everything involved in our lives, our companions, our influences, and the things we honor or worship, will be gathered with us on that Day. The verse challenges us to ensure that our company is the company of the righteous, that our influences are the influences of truth, and that the object of our worship is Allah alone, so that when the gathering comes, we are assembled not with the wrongdoers and their idols but with the believers and their Lord.",
  "23": "“Instead of Allah, then lead them all to the path of Hell.” This verse completes the command of the previous verse. The wrongdoers, their peers, and the false objects of their worship are to be led, all together, to the path of Hell. The destination of the entire assembly of unbelief is the same: the Fire. Having gathered them, the angels are to lead them to the place prepared for those who rejected the truth and worshipped others besides God.\n\nThe phrase “lead them all” emphasizes the universality of the destination. All who are gathered, the wrongdoers, their peers, and the idols, are led together to Hell. There is no separation among them at this stage; all share the same fate. The solidarity they had in unbelief, in worship, and in influence becomes a solidarity in punishment. They went the same way in life, and they go the same way in the Hereafter.\n\nThe mention of “the path of Hell” is significant. There is a path that leads to Hell, just as there is a path that leads to God. The wrongdoers, in this life, walked the path that leads to Hell, through their unbelief, their idolatry, and their wrongdoing. In the Hereafter, they are led along the path to its destination. The path they chose in life determines the path they walk in death. The connection between the two paths, the chosen and the walked, underscores the justice of the outcome: they are led where they themselves decided to go.\n\nThe inclusion of the idols in the leading to Hell completes their exposure. The false gods, which promised so much and delivered nothing, end up being led to Hell along with their worshippers. The very objects in which the wrongdoers placed their hope become their companions in ruin. The powerlessness of the idols is total: they could not save their worshippers in life, and they share their worshippers’ fate in death.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sober warning about the destination of the path of unbelief and idolatry and a call to choose a different path. It invites us to recognise that the path we walk in life leads to a corresponding destination in the Hereafter, that the path of rejecting God and worshipping others leads to Hell, and that those who walk that path, along with their companions and their idols, will all arrive at the same terrible end. The verse challenges us to examine the path we are on and to choose, while we can, the path that leads to God, so that we are not led, with the wrongdoers and their false gods, to the destination that the path of unbelief inevitably reaches.",
  "24": "“And detain them, for they must be questioned.” This verse continues the command to the angels regarding the gathered wrongdoers. After assembling them and their false gods, the angels are to detain them, because they must be questioned. The detention is not for punishment directly but for examination, the questioning that will establish their guilt and remove every excuse before the sentence is carried out.\n\nThe significance of the questioning lies in its role in establishing justice. The wrongdoers will be questioned about their deeds, their choices, their response to the messengers and the truth. The questioning is not because God lacks knowledge, for He knows everything, but to make the truth public, to confront the wrongdoers with their own actions, and to leave them without any basis for complaint about the justice of their sentence. The questioning itself is part of the perfect fairness of the judgment.\n\nThe command to “detain them” conveys the helplessness of the wrongdoers on that Day. They are not free to go, to argue, to escape. They are detained, held for questioning, entirely in the power of the One they defied. The freedom they exercised in this life, the freedom to reject and to do wrong, is replaced on that Day by a complete subjection to the divine process of judgment. The detention marks the transition from the time of choice to the time of accounting.\n\nThe questioning, mentioned here and detailed in the following verses, will expose the relationships among the wrongdoers: the leaders and the followers, the misleaders and the misled. The questioning will bring out into the open the dynamics of their shared unbelief, showing how each contributed to the others’ ruin. The process is thorough, leaving no aspect of their wrongdoing unexamined.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the thoroughness and fairness of the divine judgment and a call to live in the awareness of being questioned. It invites us to recognise that we will be questioned about our deeds, our choices, and our relationships, and that the questioning will be complete and fair. The verse challenges us to live now in a way that we can face such questioning with confidence rather than dread, having ordered our lives by the truth and treated our relationships with integrity, so that the questioning, when it comes, establishes not our guilt but our fidelity to the truth we professed to believe.",
  "25": "“What is the matter with you that you can no longer help each other?” This verse records the question posed to the wrongdoers on the Day of Judgment. They are asked why they can no longer help one another. The question is pointed and exposing. In this life, the wrongdoers helped each other in their unbelief, reinforcing each other’s rejection, supporting each other in wrongdoing. On the Day of Judgment, that mutual help has vanished, and the question highlights its absence.\n\nThe question carries an element of irony. In the world, the wrongdoers boasted of their solidarity, their strength in numbers, their mutual support. They imagined that their alliances would protect them, that the company of fellow wrongdoers was a source of security. The question exposes the hollowness of that confidence. On the Day when help is truly needed, the alliances of unbelief provide no help at all. Each wrongdoer is alone, unable to assist the others and unable to be assisted by them.\n\nThe question also serves to deepen the wrongdoers’ realisation of their helplessness. By asking why they cannot help each other, the question forces them to confront the complete failure of the relationships on which they relied. The bonds of shared wrongdoing, which seemed so strong, prove utterly useless in the face of the judgment. The question is part of the process of exposing the futility of everything the wrongdoers trusted besides God.\n\nThe helplessness described here is the reversal of the wrongdoers’ worldly confidence. They walked the earth as if they were powerful, as if their alliances made them secure. Now they discover that all their power and all their alliances amount to nothing before the judgment of God. The question “what is the matter with you” marvels, in effect, at the collapse of everything they depended on, as the reality of the Day strips away every false support.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the futility of alliances built on wrongdoing and a call to place our trust in God rather than in mutual support among the wicked. It invites us to recognise that the bonds of shared error cannot save us on the Day of Judgment, that every alliance not rooted in truth will collapse, and that the only help that endures is the help of God. The verse challenges us to examine the foundations of our confidence, ensuring that we rely not on the failing support of human alliances but on the unfailing support of the One before whom every other support gives way.",
  "26": "“In fact, on that Day they will be fully submissive.” This verse answers the question of the previous verse. Why can the wrongdoers no longer help each other? Because on that Day, they will be fully submissive. The subjection that replaces their former defiance is complete. They are no longer able to resist, to scheme, or to support one another in rebellion. They are entirely submitted, not willingly but by the overwhelming reality of the judgment, to the process that is unfolding.\n\nThe word “submissive” here carries the sense of being subdued, conquered, brought low. In this life, the wrongdoers were rebellious, defying God and resisting His guidance. On that Day, their rebellion is over. They are subdued, their defiance stripped away, their resistance broken. The subjection is not the willing submission of faith but the forced subjection of those who have no power left to oppose. Their wills, which they used to defy God, are now rendered powerless.\n\nThe completeness of the subjection, “fully submissive,” underscores the totality of the reversal. The wrongdoers are not partly subdued and partly defiant; they are fully submissive, with no remnant of their former rebellion. Every pretense of strength, every gesture of defiance, every scheme of resistance, is gone. What remains is the helpless subjection of creatures before their all-powerful Judge.\n\nThis verse explains why the wrongdoers cannot help each other. Their mutual help in this life depended on their shared freedom and power, their ability to act, to scheme, to support. On the Day of Judgment, that freedom and power are gone. They are fully submissive, unable to act on their own behalf or on behalf of others. The helplessness is complete, and therefore the mutual help, which required some freedom and power, is impossible.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the end of rebellion and a warning about the nature of the subjection that awaits the defiant. It invites us to recognise that the rebellion against God, however bold it may seem in this life, will end in complete subjection, and that the wise course is to submit to God now, willingly, while submission is still a free and saving choice. The verse challenges us to choose the willing submission of faith rather than the forced subjection of the Day of Judgment, knowing that all will submit in the end, but that the submission of faith leads to honor while the subjection of rebellion leads only to ruin.",
  "27": "“They will turn on each other, throwing blame.” This verse describes the collapse of the solidarity among the wrongdoers on the Day of Judgment. Having been unable to help each other, they now turn on each other, throwing blame. The alliances of unbelief, which held them together in this life, shatter in the face of the judgment. Each party seeks to shift the responsibility onto the others, and the bonds of shared error dissolve into mutual accusation.\n\nThe image of turning on each other is powerful. In this life, the wrongdoers faced the same direction, shared the same goals, supported the same causes. On the Day of Judgment, they turn and face each other, not as allies but as accusers. The friendship of shared wrongdoing, never genuine, reveals its true nature in the crisis of judgment. Each was using the others for mutual benefit, and now that the benefit is gone and the consequence has arrived, the using turns to blaming.\n\nThe phrase “throwing blame” conveys the active, mutual nature of the accusation. It is not a quiet regret but an active casting of blame, each party trying to load the responsibility onto the others. The wrongdoers who cooperated in error now cooperate in accusation, but the cooperation serves no common good; it serves only the futile attempt of each to escape a share of the blame.\n\nThe collapse of the solidarity is itself a form of punishment. The wrongdoers valued their alliances, drew comfort from their numbers, trusted in their mutual support. The loss of these, their transformation into mutual accusation, is part of the torment of the Day. The very relationships that gave them false security in this life become a source of pain and shame in the next, as each friend becomes an accuser and each ally becomes a reminder of shared guilt.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the fragility of alliances built on wrongdoing and a call to build our relationships on truth rather than error. It invites us to recognise that the friendships of shared unbelief cannot survive the Day of Judgment, that they will dissolve into blame and accusation, and that the only relationships that endure are those rooted in shared devotion to God. The verse challenges us to examine the basis of our friendships and alliances, ensuring that they are built on truth, so that they can withstand the Day when every false bond breaks and every alliance of error dissolves into the mutual blame this verse describes.",
  "28": "“It was you who deluded us away from what is right.” This verse records the words of the misled to their leaders on the Day of Judgment. The followers, the ordinary people who went astray, turn on those who led them astray and accuse them: it was you who deluded us away from what is right. The accusation places the blame on the leaders, claiming that the followers were led away from the truth by those they trusted.\n\nThe phrase “deluded us away from what is right” is significant. The followers acknowledge that there was a right path from which they were turned. They do not claim that there was no truth; they admit that the truth existed and that they were deluded away from it. The accusation implies that the followers might have walked the right path if they had not been misled, a claim that serves their attempt to shift the blame but that also reveals their awareness of the truth they abandoned.\n\nThe accusation reflects a common human tendency: to blame others for our own wrong choices. The followers chose to follow, chose to believe the delusion, chose to abandon the right path. Yet on the Day of Judgment, they seek to place the responsibility entirely on the leaders, as if they themselves had been passive victims rather than willing participants. The accusation is an attempt at evasion, an effort to escape a share of the blame by loading it all onto the leaders.\n\nThe dynamics of this accusation reveal the self-serving nature of the alliances of error. In this life, the followers and leaders benefited from each other: the leaders gained followers and influence, the followers gained direction and belonging. On the Day of Judgment, the mutual benefit is gone, and what remains is mutual accusation. The followers, who willingly followed, now claim to have been unwillingly misled. The leaders, who willingly led, will reply, as the next verse shows, that the followers were responsible for their own choices.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the danger of following others into error and the futility of blaming them on the Day of Judgment. It invites us to recognise that we are responsible for whom we follow and for the choices we make, even when influenced by others, and that the attempt to shift blame on the Day will not succeed. The verse challenges us to take responsibility for our beliefs and choices now, to follow only those who guide toward the truth, and to avoid the fate of those who, having followed others into error, can do nothing on the Day but accuse the very leaders they willingly followed.",
  "29": "“No! You disbelieved on your own.” This verse records the reply of the misleaders to the accusation of the misled. The leaders deny that they are solely responsible. They reply, “No!” rejecting the followers’ attempt to shift all the blame, and they insist, “You disbelieved on your own.” The followers, the leaders claim, were responsible for their own unbelief, not merely the passive victims of misleading.\n\nThe reply exposes the self-serving nature of both parties. The followers blamed the leaders entirely; the leaders deflect the blame back onto the followers entirely. Neither accepts a fair share of responsibility. Each seeks to minimize their own guilt by maximizing the other’s. The exchange reveals the essential selfishness of the alliances of error, which dissolve into mutual accusation the moment shared interest gives way to shared consequence.\n\nThe phrase “you disbelieved on your own” contains a partial truth. The followers did make their own choices; they were not forced to disbelieve. Even if misled, they consented to the misleading, chose to follow, embraced the error. The leaders’ reply points to this element of personal responsibility that the followers’ accusation tried to ignore. No one is led astray entirely without their own consent; the choice to follow is still a choice.\n\nAt the same time, the leaders’ complete denial of responsibility is also unfair. They did mislead, did influence, did use their position to turn others away from the truth. Their claim that the followers disbelieved entirely on their own ignores their own active role in the misguidance. The truth lies between the two extreme positions: both leaders and followers bear responsibility, the leaders for misleading and the followers for consenting to be misled.\n\nThe exchange as a whole reveals the impossibility of resolving guilt through mutual blame. Neither the followers’ accusation nor the leaders’ reply can change the facts or alter the outcome. Both parties are guilty, both will face the consequence, and the blame-throwing only deepens their mutual disgrace without providing any escape. The justice of God rests on His perfect knowledge, not on the self-serving arguments of the accused.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the futility of blame-shifting on the Day of Judgment and a call to take personal responsibility for our beliefs and choices. It invites us to recognise that neither leading others into error nor following them into it will be excused, that both the misleaders and the misled bear responsibility, and that the attempt to escape blame by accusing others will fail. The verse challenges us to take responsibility now, to ensure that we neither mislead others nor allow ourselves to be misled, knowing that on the Day, each will answer for their own choices, and no amount of mutual accusation will alter the perfect judgment of the all-knowing God.",
  "30": "“We had no authority over you. In fact, you yourselves were a transgressing people.” This verse continues the reply of the misleaders. They claim that they had no authority over the followers, implying that they could not force them to follow, and that the followers were themselves a transgressing people, responsible for their own wrongdoing. The claim of no authority is significant: it asserts that the relationship between leaders and followers was not one of compulsion but of willing participation.\n\nThe claim that the leaders had “no authority” contains a truth that the Quran elsewhere confirms. No leader, no influencer, no tempter has the authority to compel belief or unbelief. The choice to follow or to refuse is always the individual’s own. Satan himself, as other verses state, has no authority over human beings; he can only invite and deceive, not force. The leaders’ claim, in this sense, aligns with the broader Quranic teaching about the limits of influence and the reality of free choice.\n\nThe phrase “you yourselves were a transgressing people” places the responsibility on the followers’ own characters and choices. They were, the leaders claim, transgressors by their own nature and decision, not merely the products of misleading. The leaders argue that the followers’ unbelief flowed from their own transgression, their own willingness to go beyond the bounds, rather than from any compulsion by the leaders.\n\nThe exchange between the misled and the misleaders, spread across several verses, is one of the Quran’s most vivid pictures of the collapse of the alliances of error. Each party argues for its own innocence and the other’s guilt. Neither succeeds, because the all-knowing Judge is not deceived by either set of arguments. The exchange serves to expose, for all to see, the selfishness and the mutual betrayal that characterized the alliances of unbelief, even before the final sentence is pronounced.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the limits of influence and the reality of personal responsibility, and a warning against both misleading others and being misled. It invites us to recognise that no one can compel our choices, that we are responsible for what we believe and do, and that neither blaming our leaders nor excusing our followers will work on the Day of Judgment. The verse challenges us to take full responsibility for our own transgression, to repent of it now, and to ensure that we neither use influence to mislead others nor surrender our own judgment to those who would lead us astray, knowing that each of us will answer, alone, for the choices we made, however much influenced by others.",
  "31": "“The decree of our Lord has come to pass against us all: we will certainly taste the punishment.” This verse records a moment of shared recognition among the wrongdoers, both leaders and followers. After their mutual accusations, they arrive at a common acknowledgment: the decree of the Lord has come to pass against them all. They will all taste the punishment. The recognition is tragic because it is both accurate and useless: they see the truth of their situation, but the seeing comes too late to help them.\n\nThe phrase “against us all” is significant. Despite their attempts to distinguish between leaders and followers, to assign more blame to one party or the other, they finally recognize that the decree applies to all of them. The judgment does not discriminate in their favor based on their arguments. All who participated in the unbelief, whether as leaders or followers, face the same decree. The solidarity they had in error becomes a solidarity in punishment.\n\nThe recognition that “the decree of our Lord has come to pass” acknowledges both the reality and the inevitability of the judgment. The decree was not a possibility but a certainty, pronounced by the Lord and now fulfilled. The wrongdoers recognize, too late, that the warnings they received were true, that the threatened judgment was real, and that the decree they had denied or ignored has now overtaken them.\n\nThe phrase “we will certainly taste the punishment” conveys the certainty and the personal nature of the consequence. They will not merely witness the punishment; they will taste it, experience it personally and directly. The tasting is certain, marked by the emphatic “certainly.” There is no escaping it, no mitigating it, no avoiding it. The decree has come to pass, and the tasting will follow.\n\nThis verse marks a shift in the exchange between the wrongdoers. After the futile blame-shifting, they arrive at a moment of bleak realism, acknowledging their common fate. The realism does not save them, but it does complete the exposure of their condition. They see, at last, that their alliances, their arguments, and their denials have all failed, and that what remains is the certain tasting of the punishment decreed by the Lord against them all.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the final recognition of the doomed and a call to arrive at that recognition now, while it can still help. It invites us to acknowledge, before the decree comes to pass, the reality of God’s judgment, the certainty of the consequences of unbelief, and the common fate of all who participate in error, whether as leaders or followers. The verse challenges us to turn from the path that leads to this recognition too late, and to choose instead the path of faith and obedience, so that we never have to taste the punishment whose certainty the wrongdoers, on that Day, finally and uselessly confess.",
  "32": "“We caused you to deviate, for we ourselves were deviant.” This verse records a further admission from the misleaders. After denying sole responsibility in the previous verses, they now make a more honest acknowledgment: they did cause the followers to deviate, but they did so because they themselves were deviant. The admission is a confession of mutual ruin: the leaders were lost, and in their lostness they led others astray.\n\nThe admission contains a profound insight into the nature of misguidance. The misled are led astray by those who are themselves astray. A person cannot guide others to a path he has not found; he can only lead them along the path he is on, and if that path is the path of deviation, then those who follow him will be deviated along with him. The leaders’ misguidance of others was not a separate act from their own misguidance but a consequence of it. They led others into the same error in which they themselves were lost.\n\nThe phrase “for we ourselves were deviant” reveals the tragic chain of misguidance. The leaders did not start out as misleaders; they became so through their own deviation. And their deviation, once established, spread to others, who in turn may have spread it further. The error propagated through influence, each deviant leading others into the same lostness. The admission exposes the contagious nature of misguidance and the responsibility that comes with influence.\n\nThe honesty of this admission stands in contrast to the earlier blame-shifting. Here, the leaders acknowledge their role: they did cause the followers to deviate. But they couple the acknowledgment with an explanation that, while true, does not excuse them: they were themselves deviant. The explanation locates the source of their misleading in their own lostness, but it does not remove their responsibility for having led others into the same lostness. To be deviant oneself is tragic; to lead others into deviation is doubly so.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the contagious nature of misguidance and the responsibility that comes with influence over others. It invites us to recognise that we lead others, for good or for ill, along the path we ourselves are walking, and that if we are deviant, our influence will deviate those who follow us. The verse challenges us to ensure that our own path is the path of guidance, so that our influence leads others toward truth rather than away from it, and to be wary of following those whose own deviation will only lead us, in turn, into the same lostness they themselves suffer.",
  "33": "“Surely on that Day they will all share in the punishment.” This verse states the outcome of the whole exchange between the misled and the misleaders. Despite their mutual accusations and their attempts to distinguish their levels of responsibility, they will all share in the punishment. The solidarity they had in unbelief becomes a solidarity in consequence. The judgment does not separate them based on their arguments but gathers them into a common fate.\n\nThe word “all” emphasizes the universality of the sharing. Not some of them, not the leaders alone or the followers alone, but all of them. The leaders who misled and the followers who were misled, the arrogant and the compliant, the influential and the influenced, all share in the punishment. The distinctions that seemed so important in their exchange collapse before the reality of the judgment. All who participated in the unbelief face its consequence.\n\nThe sharing in the punishment is the final irony of the alliances of error. In this life, the wrongdoers shared in their unbelief, supporting each other, reinforcing each other’s rejection, enjoying the solidarity of shared error. On the Day of Judgment, that sharing continues, but in a transformed and terrible form: they share in the punishment. The very togetherness that gave them false comfort in the world becomes the togetherness of common ruin in the Hereafter.\n\nThe certainty of the sharing is marked by the word “surely.” There is no doubt, no exception, no escape. The wrongdoers who argued and accused, who denied and admitted, who tried to shift blame and to accept it, all arrive at the same destination. The arguments did not matter; the distinctions did not hold; the outcome is the same for all. The surely underscores the finality and the inevitability of the shared punishment.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the shared fate of all who participate in unbelief and a call to choose a different sharing. It invites us to recognise that the alliances of error lead to a shared punishment, that neither leading nor following in unbelief will be exempted, and that the only wise course is to separate ourselves from the alliances of error and to join instead the company of the believers. The verse challenges us to ensure that the sharing we choose in this life is the sharing of faith and righteousness, which leads to a shared reward, rather than the sharing of unbelief, which leads, with equal certainty, to the shared punishment this verse proclaims.",
  "34": "“That is certainly how We deal with the wicked.” This verse states the principle behind the fate of the wrongdoers just described. Their shared punishment, their mutual accusation, their helplessness and humiliation, all of this is how God deals with the wicked. The treatment is not arbitrary or exceptional but follows a consistent pattern. The wicked, in every age, are dealt with in this way.\n\nThe word “certainly” emphasizes the sureness of the pattern. There is no doubt that this is how the wicked are treated. The fate described in the previous verses is not a possibility but a certainty for all who fall into the category of the wicked. The certainty should sober every reader, because it removes any hope that wickedness might, in some case, escape its consequence.\n\nThe phrase “how We deal with the wicked” establishes the treatment as a divine practice, a settled way in which God responds to wickedness. The treatment includes the exposure of the alliances of error, the collapse of mutual support, the futility of blame-shifting, and the final sharing in punishment. All of these elements are part of God’s consistent dealing with those who choose wickedness. The treatment is thorough, just, and inevitable.\n\nThe principle stated here has a universal application. It is not limited to the specific wrongdoers described in the previous verses but applies to all the wicked, in every time and place. The pattern is fixed: those who ally themselves in wickedness will face the exposure, the collapse, and the punishment that the pattern describes. The recognition of this pattern should serve as both a warning and a confirmation: a warning to those tempted by wickedness, and a confirmation to the believers that the wicked will not escape.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear statement of the inevitable consequence of wickedness and a call to take heed before the pattern is applied to us. It invites us to recognise that the fate described in the previous verses is not unique but universal, that all the wicked are dealt with in this way, and that the only escape is to cease from wickedness before the dealing begins. The verse challenges us to examine our own lives for any alliance with wickedness, to repent and turn away from it while there is time, and to align ourselves instead with the way of righteousness, which leads to a very different dealing, one of mercy and reward rather than of the exposure and punishment that certainly await the wicked.",
  "35": "“For whenever it was said to them, There is no god worthy of worship except Allah, they acted arrogantly.” This verse identifies the specific fault that led to the punishment of the wrongdoers. Whenever they were told the core message of all the prophets, that there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, they responded with arrogance. The fault was not ignorance, for the message was clearly delivered to them. The fault was the arrogant refusal to accept a truth that challenged their pride and their way of life.\n\nThe message “There is no god worthy of worship except Allah” is the central declaration of Islam, the testimony of divine oneness that all the messengers brought. It affirms that God alone is worthy of worship and denies the divinity of everything else. To accept this message is to submit one’s pride, one’s allegiances, and one’s way of life to the truth of God’s oneness. The wrongdoers refused this submission. They acted arrogantly, lifting themselves up against the truth rather than bowing before it.\n\nThe word “whenever” emphasizes the consistency of their arrogant response. It was not a single refusal but a pattern. Every time the message was presented, the response was the same: arrogance. The consistency reveals the depth of their resistance. They were not occasionally weak or occasionally resistant; they were consistently arrogant, refusing the truth at every encounter.\n\nArrogance is the great enemy of faith because it refuses the humility that faith requires. To accept that God alone is worthy of worship is to acknowledge one’s own creatureliness, one’s dependence, one’s accountability. The arrogant person refuses this acknowledgment. He will not bow, will not submit, will not acknowledge any authority above his own will. The message of divine oneness, which demands precisely this bowing and submitting, is therefore unacceptable to the arrogant, who respond to it not with faith but with pride.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the danger of arrogance in the face of the truth and a call to cultivate the humility that faith requires. It invites us to examine our own response to the message of God’s oneness, to ensure that we receive it with humility rather than arrogance, and to recognize that the arrogant refusal of this message is the fault that leads to the punishment described in the previous verses. The verse challenges us to bow before the truth of God’s oneness, submitting our pride and our allegiances to the One who alone is worthy of worship, rather than acting arrogantly and bringing upon ourselves the fate that arrogance certainly earns.",
  "36": "“And argued, Should we really abandon our gods for a mad poet?” This verse records the specific argument the arrogant disbelievers used to justify their refusal of the message. They argued that they should not abandon their gods for the sake of a “mad poet.” The argument reveals the two pillars of their resistance: their attachment to their traditional gods and their dismissal of the Prophet as a madman or a mere poet.\n\nThe attachment to “our gods” was a powerful force. The idols were not just religious objects but symbols of tradition, identity, and social cohesion. To abandon them was to abandon the ways of the forefathers, to separate from the community, to become an outsider. The disbelievers felt this cost keenly and used it as an argument against belief: why should we give up everything familiar for this new message? The argument appealed to the comfort of the known against the challenge of the truth.\n\nThe dismissal of the Prophet as “a mad poet” was the other pillar. By labeling him mad, the disbelievers dismissed his message as the product of a deranged mind. By calling him a poet, they reduced the Quran, the divine revelation, to a human art form. Both labels served the same purpose: to avoid taking the Prophet and his message seriously. If he was mad, his message could be pitied and ignored; if he was a poet, his words could be admired and dismissed. Either way, the truth he brought could be refused without engagement.\n\nThe phrase “should we really abandon” conveys the disbelievers’ sense of the unreasonable cost of belief. They presented the choice as irrational: why give up the gods of the forefathers for the sake of one man’s strange claims? The framing made the choice seem foolish, when in fact it was the most reasonable choice possible: to abandon false gods for the true God, to exchange error for truth, to trade the temporary comfort of tradition for the eternal good of salvation.\n\nFor the reader, the verse exposes the common arguments used to resist the truth and a call to examine our own resistance. It invites us to recognize the patterns of evasion: the appeal to tradition, the dismissal of the messenger, the framing of belief as costly and irrational. The verse challenges us to look past these evasions to the truth itself, to be willing to abandon whatever false gods we may have, whether traditional or modern, for the sake of the true God, and to receive His messenger not as a mad poet to be dismissed but as a bearer of truth to be believed and followed.",
  "37": "“In fact, he came with the truth, confirming earlier messengers.” This verse gives the divine reply to the disbelievers’ dismissal of the Prophet. They called him a mad poet; the verse declares that he came with the truth, confirming the earlier messengers. The reply refutes both elements of the disbelievers’ accusation. He was not mad but truthful, bringing the truth from God. And he was not a mere poet, inventing his own message, but a messenger in the long line of messengers, confirming the truth they had all brought.\n\nThe affirmation that he “came with the truth” directly contradicts the label of madness. A madman does not bring truth; he brings the confused products of a disordered mind. The Prophet brought clarity, wisdom, guidance, and moral excellence, the very opposite of madness. The verse restores the truth of his message, which the disbelievers had tried to dismiss through the label of insanity.\n\nThe statement that he was “confirming earlier messengers” refutes the label of poet. A poet produces original compositions from his own imagination. The Prophet brought a message that was not original in the sense of being his own invention but was continuous with the messages brought by all the earlier messengers. He confirmed what they had taught: the worship of the One God, the reality of accountability, the path of righteousness. The continuity of his message with theirs proves its divine origin, since no human invention could so perfectly align with the messages brought by prophets across the ages.\n\nThe word “in fact” introduces the reply with emphasis. The disbelievers had said one thing; the truth is in fact another. Their arguments, their labels, their dismissals are all overturned by the simple statement of the facts: he came with the truth, and he confirmed the messengers. The reply does not engage in elaborate debate; it simply states the truth, which is sufficient to refute every evasion.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear vindication of the Prophet and his message and a call to receive him as he truly is. It invites us to recognize that the Prophet came with the truth, not with madness or poetry, and that his message confirms the long line of messengers sent by God throughout history. The verse challenges us to look past the dismissive labels that people apply to the messengers of truth and to receive the message for what it is: the truth from God, continuous with all His previous guidance, worthy of belief and obedience, and leading those who accept it to the good for which God has always sent His messengers to humanity.",
  "38": "“You will certainly taste the painful torment.” This verse pronounces the consequence on those who dismissed the Prophet and refused his message. They will certainly taste the painful torment. The certainty is marked by the emphatic “certainly.” The tasting is not a possibility but a guarantee. And the torment is painful, not mild or brief but severe in its suffering. The verse leaves no room for doubt about the fate of those who rejected the truth.\n\nThe word “taste” is significant. It conveys direct, personal experience. The torment will not merely be witnessed or described; it will be tasted, experienced firsthand by those who earned it. The word makes the consequence vivid and immediate, a reality to be endured rather than a concept to be considered. The tasting is the fulfillment of the warning, the moment when the abstract threat becomes concrete suffering.\n\nThe phrase “painful torment” describes the nature of the consequence. It is not a gentle correction or a temporary discomfort but a painful torment, severe in its intensity. The severity corresponds to the seriousness of the rejection that earned it. To dismiss the truth-bringer as a mad poet, to refuse the message of God’s oneness, to cling to false gods, all of this is serious enough to earn a painful torment. The verse does not soften the consequence but presents it honestly, as a warning to the living.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the previous one is significant. The previous verse affirmed that the Prophet came with the truth. This verse pronounces the torment on those who refused that truth. The two stand together: the truth was offered, and the refusal of the truth earns the torment. The justice of the consequence lies in its connection to the choice: those who will not have the truth will have the torment, because they have deliberately refused the one thing that could have saved them from it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a solemn warning about the consequence of refusing the truth and a call to accept it while acceptance can still save. It invites us to recognize that the rejection of God’s message leads, with certainty, to a painful torment, and that the only way to avoid that fate is to receive the truth now, in faith. The verse challenges us to examine our own response to the truth, to ensure that we are not among those who will taste the painful torment because they refused the message, but among those who, by accepting the truth, spare themselves the consequence and gain instead the reward that faith earns.",
  "39": "“And will only be rewarded for what you used to do.” This verse states the principle of perfect justice that governs the punishment of the wrongdoers. They will be rewarded only for what they used to do. The consequence corresponds exactly to the conduct. There is no arbitrary punishment, no infliction of suffering beyond what the deeds have earned. The reward, here in the sense of recompense or consequence, is precisely fitted to the actions that produced it.\n\nThe word “only” is significant. It limits the punishment to what was earned. The wrongdoers will not be punished for more than they did, nor for the deeds of others, nor beyond the measure of their own conduct. The restriction is itself a form of justice: the punishment is bounded by the crime, never exceeding it. At the same time, the punishment fully corresponds to the crime, leaving no wrong unaccounted for.\n\nThe phrase “what you used to do” ties the consequence directly to the pattern of the wrongdoers’ lives. It was not a single act that earned the punishment but the ongoing, settled pattern of their conduct, the consistent refusal, the persistent arrogance, the habitual wrongdoing. The punishment answers to the whole shape of their lives, not just to isolated incidents. The continuity between conduct and consequence is complete: as they lived, so they are recompensed.\n\nThis principle of exact recompense applies to both punishment and reward. The wrongdoers receive the punishment their deeds have earned; the righteous receive the reward their deeds have earned. In both cases, the consequence is fitted to the conduct, by a justice so perfect that nothing is overlooked and nothing is excessive. The principle leaves no room for complaint: each person receives exactly what their choices have produced.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to take responsibility for our conduct, grounded in the recognition that we will be recompensed exactly for what we do. It invites us to recognise that our present choices are shaping our future recompense, that every deed contributes to the reward or punishment we will receive, and that the justice of God is so perfect that nothing is missed and nothing is unfair. The verse challenges us to live in a way that earns a good recompense, knowing that we will receive, with perfect fairness, exactly what our deeds have deserved, and that the time to shape that recompense is now, while we still have the freedom to choose how we live.",
  "40": "“But not the chosen servants of Allah.” This verse introduces a dramatic exception to the punishment just described. The wrongdoers will taste the painful torment, but not the chosen servants of Allah. The word “but” marks a sharp contrast. There is a group that will be spared the torment, a group defined by their relationship to God: they are His chosen servants. The exception brings hope into a passage that has been full of warning.\n\nThe phrase “chosen servants of Allah” identifies the group with beautiful precision. They are servants, acknowledging their position before God and living in His service. And they are chosen, selected by God for His favor, not because of any merit of their own but by His grace. The combination of servanthood and chosenness describes the believer: one who serves God and who has been graciously chosen by Him. This group, the verse declares, will not share the fate of the wrongdoers.\n\nThe exception is absolute. The chosen servants will not taste the painful torment that awaits the wicked. Their relationship with God, expressed in their servanthood and sealed by His choice, protects them from the consequence that falls on those who refused Him. The contrast between the two groups could not be starker: the wrongdoers face certain torment; the chosen servants face exemption from it. The division is complete, based on the fundamental choice each person makes regarding God.\n\nThe significance of this verse lies in the hope it offers. In the midst of a passage detailing the terrible fate of the wrongdoers, the verse holds out the possibility of a different fate, available to those who become the chosen servants of Allah. The hope is not vague but specific: there is a group that escapes the torment, and that group is defined by a relationship with God that is available to all who will turn to Him. The verse invites every reader to consider whether they belong to that group.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a message of hope and a call to become one of the chosen servants of Allah. It invites us to recognise that the torment described in the previous verses is not inevitable, that there is a way of escape through the relationship of servanthood with God, and that the choice to become His servant is the choice that leads away from punishment and toward the reward. The verse challenges us to take our place among the chosen servants, serving God with sincerity and trusting His gracious choice, so that we are found, on the Day of judgment, not among the wrongdoers who taste the torment, but among the servants who are spared it and welcomed into their Lord’s favor.",
  "41": "“They will have a known provision.” This verse begins to describe the reward of the chosen servants of Allah. They will have a known provision. The word “provision” suggests sustenance, supply, everything needed for well-being and enjoyment. And it is “known,” meaning established, certain, assured. The provision is not in doubt; it is already prepared and waiting for them. The certainty of the reward matches the certainty of the punishment described earlier for the wrongdoers.\n\nThe phrase “known provision” is rich with meaning. In this life, provision is uncertain; we work for it, worry about it, and never fully secure it. In Paradise, the provision is known, established by divine promise, free from uncertainty. The servants of Allah do not need to strive for their provision; it is given, guaranteed by the One who has prepared it. The contrast with the anxiety of earthly provision underscores the peace of the Paradise provision.\n\nThe provision is also “known” in the sense that it has been declared, made known through revelation. The believers know, even in this life, what awaits them, because God has described it. The provision is not a vague hope but a specific promise, and the knowledge of it sustains the believers through the trials of their earthly journey. They can endure hardship because they know the provision that awaits them.\n\nThis verse stands in deliberate contrast to the punishment described in the previous verses. The wrongdoers will taste painful torment; the chosen servants will have a known provision. The two groups, defined by their response to God, receive opposite recompenses: one faces suffering, the other faces sustenance. The division that runs through the whole passage reaches its climax in this contrast between the torment of the wicked and the provision of the chosen.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful glimpse of the reward that awaits the chosen servants of Allah and an encouragement to persevere in the path that leads to it. It invites us to recognise that the provision of Paradise is certain, established by divine promise, and free from the anxieties that mark earthly provision. The verse challenges us to live as chosen servants, trusting the known provision that awaits us, and to allow the certainty of that reward to sustain us through whatever trials we face, knowing that the One who has prepared the provision is faithful and that what He has promised will surely be given.",
  "42": "“Fruits of every type. And they will be honoured in the Gardens of Bliss.” This verse adds detail to the known provision of the chosen servants. The provision includes fruits of every type, suggesting abundance, variety, and delight. And the servants will be honored, not merely fed but dignified, in the Gardens of Bliss. The combination of provision, honor, and bliss paints a picture of a reward that satisfies every dimension of the human being: bodily, social, and spiritual.\n\nThe mention of “fruits of every type” emphasizes the variety and abundance of the provision. Not one kind of fruit but every type, an endless variety of flavors, colors, and delights. The abundance reflects the generosity of the Giver, who does not provide merely adequately but lavishly. The believers, who may have known scarcity in this world, will enjoy in Paradise a provision of unimaginable variety and richness.\n\nThe honor mentioned is significant. The chosen servants, who may have been despised, persecuted, or lowly in this world, will be honored in the next. The honor reverses the judgments of the world, which often exalts the wrongdoers and humiliates the righteous. In Paradise, the righteous receive the honor that befits their faithfulness, raised to a dignity that the world denied them. The honor is part of the reward, a recognition by God of the value of their lives of service.\n\nThe “Gardens of Bliss” describe the setting of the reward. Gardens suggest beauty, shade, water, and life. Bliss suggests a happiness that is deep, complete, and untroubled. The combination creates an image of perfect well-being in a setting of perfect beauty. The Gardens of Bliss are the home prepared for the chosen servants, a place where every good is present and every evil is absent.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid picture of the reward that awaits the chosen servants and an invitation to long for that destination. It invites us to anticipate the fruits, the honor, and the bliss of the Gardens, to allow the beauty of the promise to draw us toward the life that earns it, and to live now as chosen servants, trusting that the reward described, however surpassing our imagination, is real and certain. The verse encourages us to persevere in faithfulness, knowing that the fruits, the honor, and the Gardens of Bliss await those who serve God in this life and who will be served by His generosity in the life to come.",
  "43": "“In the Gardens of Bliss.” This verse, continuing the thought of the previous one, emphasizes the setting of the believers’ reward. The chosen servants of Allah will be honored in the Gardens of Bliss. The repetition of the phrase “Gardens of Bliss” underscores its importance and invites us to dwell on its meaning. The Gardens are not merely a place but a condition: a state of bliss, of complete and lasting happiness, in a setting of beauty and abundance.\n\nThe word “Gardens” evokes images of lush vegetation, flowing water, shade, and fruit. In the arid world of the Quran’s first audience, gardens were places of refreshment, beauty, and life, oases of green in a landscape of brown. The Gardens of Paradise represent the perfection of everything an earthly garden suggests: perfect beauty, perfect refreshment, perfect life, without any of the limitations or decay that mark even the most beautiful earthly garden.\n\nThe word “Bliss” describes the condition of those who dwell in the Gardens. Bliss is more than pleasure; it is a deep, settled, comprehensive happiness that pervades every aspect of existence. It includes the joy of the senses, the peace of the heart, the satisfaction of every desire, the security of permanence, and the honor of God’s favor. Bliss is the sum of every good, gathered into a single state of being.\n\nThe placement of the believers in the Gardens of Bliss completes the contrast with the wrongdoers that has run through the whole passage. The wrongdoers are led to the path of Hell, where they share in punishment. The chosen servants are placed in the Gardens of Bliss, where they share in honor and happiness. The two outcomes stand as the two possible destinies of every human being, determined by the fundamental choice each makes regarding God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful summary of the believer’s reward and a call to choose the destiny it describes. It invites us to dwell on the meaning of the Gardens of Bliss, to let their beauty and joy draw our hearts toward the life that leads to them, and to live now in a way that makes this destiny our own. The verse challenges us to choose, by faith and service, the Gardens of Bliss over the path of Hell, knowing that the choice we make in this life determines the place we will inhabit forever, and that the Gardens, with all their bliss, await those who choose God and are chosen by Him in return.",
  "44": "“Facing each other on thrones.” This verse adds a detail to the picture of the believers in Paradise. They will be seated on thrones, facing one another. The image conveys honor, ease, and companionship. Thrones suggest dignity and exaltation; the believers are not standing or crouching but enthroned, honored by their Lord. And they face one another, suggesting the joy of companionship, the pleasure of conversation and shared delight among friends.\n\nThe detail of facing each other is significant. In this world, much of our interaction is marked by hierarchy, distance, or strain. In Paradise, the believers sit facing one another as equals and friends, enjoying a companionship free from every tension that marred earthly relationships. The arrangement suggests intimacy, mutual respect, and the pure pleasure of one another’s company. The social joys of Paradise are as much a part of the reward as its physical delights.\n\nThe thrones themselves convey the honor bestowed on the believers. To be seated on a throne is to be exalted, to occupy a position of dignity. In this world, thrones belong to kings; in Paradise, they belong to all the righteous. The reversal is complete: those who may have been poor, despised, or persecuted on earth are enthroned in Paradise, raised to a dignity that surpasses anything the world could offer or deny.\n\nThe image also suggests rest. The believers, having completed the struggles of their earthly journey, are now seated, at rest, no longer striving or fighting. The throne is not only a position of honor but a place of repose. The labor of faithful living is over, and what remains is the enjoyment of the reward, in the company of fellow believers, on thrones of dignity and rest.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful picture of the social and personal joys of Paradise and an invitation to long for that companionship and honor. It invites us to anticipate the pleasure of sitting with fellow believers, facing one another in dignity and friendship, freed from every strain, and to live now in a way that earns such a reward. The verse encourages us to value the companionship of the faithful in this life, knowing that it foreshadows the perfect companionship of Paradise, where the righteous, enthroned and honored, will enjoy one another’s company forever, in the lasting peace and joy that faithful friendship, perfected, brings.",
  "45": "“A drink of pure wine will be passed around to them from a flowing stream.” This verse describes one of the delights of Paradise: a drink, offered to the believers, passed around among them from a flowing stream. The drink is described as pure wine, and it is served in abundance, from a stream that flows continually. The image is one of generous, unending provision and shared enjoyment among the blessed.\n\nThe mention of wine in Paradise requires understanding in its context. In this world, wine is forbidden because of its harmful effects: it intoxicates, clouds the mind, and leads to strife. The wine of Paradise is different. It is pure, free from the harmful qualities of earthly wine, and its enjoyment brings no intoxication or harm, only delight. The believers are given in Paradise, in a purified form, a pleasure that was withheld from them in this world because of its dangers.\n\nThe image of the drink being “passed around” suggests sharing and abundance. The believers do not drink alone or in scarcity; they share the drink with one another, passing it among the company. The communal aspect of the enjoyment reflects the social nature of Paradise’s joys. The believers delight together, their shared happiness enhancing each one’s pleasure.\n\nThe drink comes from “a flowing stream,” suggesting continuous, unending supply. The stream does not run dry; the provision is perpetual. In a world where water sources can fail and where drinks must be produced through labor, the image of a flowing stream that continually supplies a delightful drink conveys a sense of effortless, endless abundance. The believers need only receive what the stream continually offers.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid picture of the delights of Paradise and an encouragement to persevere in the path that leads there. It invites us to anticipate the pure, harmless delights that await the believers, the shared enjoyment, and the endless abundance, and to live now in a way that earns such a reward. The verse reassures us that the pleasures withheld in this world for our own protection are not lost forever but await us, purified and perfected, in Paradise, where the believers drink from flowing streams of delight, in the company of the blessed, forever.",
  "46": "“Crystal-white, delicious to drink.” This verse adds two qualities to the description of the drink of Paradise. It is crystal-white, describing its appearance, and delicious to drink, describing its taste. The combination engages two senses at once: sight and taste. The drink is a pleasure to behold and a pleasure to consume, beautiful in appearance and delightful in flavor.\n\nThe description “crystal-white” conveys purity and beauty. The drink is clear and white, like crystal, sparkling and clean. The whiteness suggests purity, the absence of any contamination or blemish. The crystal quality suggests clarity and brilliance, a drink that is a delight to look at before it is even tasted. The appearance of the drink is part of its pleasure, engaging the eye with its beauty.\n\nThe description “delicious to drink” speaks directly to the experience of consuming it. The drink is not merely beautiful but genuinely enjoyable, a pleasure to taste. The word “delicious” emphasizes the quality of the pleasure: this is not a mediocre or ordinary drink but one that delights the palate, satisfying and exquisite. The believers, in drinking it, experience a pleasure that exceeds anything they knew in the world.\n\nThe combination of visual beauty and gustatory delight reflects the comprehensive nature of Paradise’s pleasures. Every sense is engaged; every dimension of enjoyment is fulfilled. The believers do not receive merely functional sustenance but comprehensive delight, in which the appearance, the taste, and the experience of the provision all contribute to the joy. The detail reflects the generosity of the Giver, who provides not only what is needed but what gives pleasure in every aspect.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid picture of the comprehensive delights of Paradise and an invitation to anticipate a reward that engages every dimension of human enjoyment. It invites us to recognise that the pleasures of Paradise are not one-dimensional but complete, beautiful to behold and delightful to experience, and to live now in a way that earns such a reward. The verse encourages us to look beyond the limited and often disappointing pleasures of this world to the perfect, comprehensive delights of Paradise, where every provision is both crystal-white in beauty and delicious in enjoyment, offered by a generous Lord to His faithful servants.",
  "47": "“It will neither harm them, nor will they be intoxicated by it.” This verse states two crucial qualities of the drink of Paradise that distinguish it from earthly wine. First, it will not harm them. Earthly wine harms the body, the mind, and the relationships of those who consume it; the wine of Paradise brings no harm of any kind. Second, it will not intoxicate them. Earthly wine clouds the reason and leads to loss of control; the wine of Paradise leaves the mind clear and the dignity intact.\n\nThe absence of harm is significant. In this world, many pleasures come at a cost: the food that delights may damage the health, the drink that pleases may harm the body, the enjoyment that satisfies may lead to later suffering. The pleasures of Paradise are free from this trade-off. They bring pure enjoyment without any harmful consequence. The believers can indulge without fear, because what they enjoy has been purified of every harmful quality.\n\nThe absence of intoxication is particularly important. Intoxication, the clouding of the reason, is one of the main reasons wine is forbidden in this world. The reason is a precious gift, the faculty by which human beings know God, discern right from wrong, and conduct their lives with dignity. To cloud the reason through intoxication is to dishonor this gift and to risk behavior that one would otherwise avoid. The wine of Paradise, by contrast, does not cloud the reason. The believers enjoy the pleasure of the drink with their minds fully clear, their dignity fully intact.\n\nThe combination of these two qualities transforms the drink from a source of danger into a source of pure delight. Earthly wine is forbidden precisely because it harms and intoxicates; Paradise wine is permitted precisely because it does neither. The same category of pleasure, in its purified form, becomes one of the joys of the blessed. The transformation reflects the perfection of Paradise, where every good is present in a form free from every evil.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reassurance about the nature of Paradise’s pleasures and an invitation to trust the wisdom of God’s prohibitions in this world. It invites us to recognise that the things forbidden in this world are forbidden because of their harmful qualities, and that the same pleasures, purified of those qualities, await the believers in Paradise. The verse encourages us to observe God’s prohibitions now, trusting that the withholding of certain pleasures in this world is for our protection, and that the purified, harmless, non-intoxicating versions of those pleasures await us in Paradise, where every good is enjoyed without any of the costs that mar earthly pleasure.",
  "48": "“And with them will be maidens of modest gaze and gorgeous eyes.” This verse describes another aspect of the companionship of Paradise: the presence of maidens characterized by modest gaze and gorgeous eyes. The description emphasizes both moral beauty and physical beauty. The maidens are modest in their gaze, conveying purity and restraint, and they have gorgeous eyes, conveying physical splendor. The combination presents a beauty that is both inward and outward.\n\nThe phrase “modest gaze” is significant. In the Quranic vision, beauty is not separated from virtue. The maidens of Paradise are beautiful, but their beauty is accompanied by modesty, by a purity of demeanor that enhances rather than diminishes their attractiveness. The modest gaze suggests dignity, self-respect, and a beauty that does not need to be flaunted to be appreciated. The moral quality is part of the aesthetic quality; the two are inseparable.\n\nThe “gorgeous eyes” describe a specific physical beauty. The eyes are often considered the most expressive and captivating feature of the face, and their beauty is highlighted as a mark of the maidens’ splendor. The physical description is part of the comprehensive pleasure of Paradise, which engages every dimension of human enjoyment, including the aesthetic and the relational.\n\nThe description must be understood within the Quran’s broader vision of Paradise as a place of complete satisfaction, where every good and legitimate human desire is fulfilled in a purified form. The companionship described is part of this fulfillment, offering the believers a relational and aesthetic joy that is perfect, pure, and free from every strain or impurity that marks earthly relationships.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a glimpse of the relational and aesthetic joys of Paradise and a reminder that the Quran’s vision of reward includes the fulfillment of every good human desire in a purified form. It invites us to recognise that Paradise is a place of comprehensive satisfaction, where beauty, companionship, and purity are joined, and to live now in a way that earns such a reward. The verse encourages us to value purity and modesty in this life, recognising that the beauty accompanied by virtue, glimpsed imperfectly here, is perfected in Paradise, where the believers enjoy companionship marked by both gorgeous splendor and modest dignity, free from every imperfection.",
  "49": "“As if they were pristine pearls.” This verse uses a simile to describe the beauty of the maidens of Paradise: they are as if they were pristine pearls. The image of pearls conveys several qualities at once: rarity, value, beauty, purity, and a luminous, delicate splendor. The pearls are described as “pristine,” meaning unblemished, untouched, in their original perfection. The simile captures the flawless beauty of the Paradise companions.\n\nThe choice of pearls as the image is significant. Pearls are among the most valued of gems, prized for their luster, their smoothness, and their natural beauty. Unlike stones that must be cut and polished, pearls emerge from the oyster already beautiful, formed through a natural process into objects of splendor. The image suggests a beauty that is natural, complete, and effortless, not manufactured or enhanced.\n\nThe word “pristine” emphasizes the perfection of the pearls. They are not flawed, scratched, or dull; they are pristine, in the full perfection of their beauty. The image conveys the flawless quality of the Paradise companions, whose beauty is unmarred by any imperfection. In a world where every beauty is mixed with some flaw, the image of pristine pearls points to a beauty that is pure and complete.\n\nThe luminous quality of pearls is also significant. Pearls have a soft, inner glow, a luster that seems to come from within. This luminous quality suggests a beauty that is not merely surface but depth, a radiance that reflects an inner light. The Paradise companions, like pristine pearls, possess a beauty that is both outward and inward, a radiance that comes from the purity and perfection of their whole being.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid image of the beauty of Paradise and an invitation to appreciate the Quran’s use of the most beautiful images to convey the wonders of the reward. It invites us to recognise that the beauty of Paradise surpasses anything we can imagine, that the Quran uses the most precious and perfect images, like pristine pearls, to give us a glimpse of it, and to live now in a way that earns such a reward. The verse encourages us to look beyond the flawed beauties of this world to the perfect beauties of Paradise, where every splendor is as flawless and as luminous as a pristine pearl, prepared by God for the joy of His faithful servants.",
  "50": "“Then they will turn to one another inquisitively.” This verse describes a moment of conversation among the believers in Paradise. They will turn to one another, engaging in mutual inquiry and discussion. The word “inquisitively” suggests curiosity, interest, and the pleasure of shared exploration. The believers, in the ease and intimacy of Paradise, will talk with one another, sharing their experiences and reflecting together on the journey that brought them to their present blessed state.\n\nThe significance of this detail lies in what it reveals about the nature of Paradise. Paradise is not a place of silent isolation or mindless pleasure; it is a place of relationship, conversation, and shared reflection. The believers engage with one another, interested in each other’s stories, curious about each other’s experiences. The social and intellectual dimensions of human beings are fulfilled in Paradise, not left behind. The believers think, talk, and share, in the freedom and joy of their eternal home.\n\nThe conversation that follows, described in the next verses, will focus on the contrast between the believers’ present blessed state and their former earthly lives, including the people they knew who did not believe. The inquisitive turning to one another sets up this conversation, in which a believer will recall a former companion who used to doubt the resurrection. The conversation reveals that the believers in Paradise remember their earthly lives and reflect on them, with gratitude for their salvation and awareness of the fate of those who refused to believe.\n\nThe ease of the conversation reflects the peace of Paradise. There is no hurry, no anxiety, no tension. The believers have all the time and all the freedom to turn to one another, to inquire, to share, to reflect. The conversation is itself a pleasure, a manifestation of the harmonious relationships that fill Paradise, where the believers, freed from every strain, enjoy one another’s company in perfect ease.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a glimpse of the relational and reflective joys of Paradise and a reminder that the reward includes the fulfillment of our social and intellectual natures. It invites us to recognise that Paradise is a place of conversation, relationship, and shared reflection, where the believers, in perfect ease, engage with one another, and to live now in a way that earns such companionship. The verse encourages us to value and cultivate good companionship and meaningful conversation in this life, knowing that these foreshadow the perfect companionship and conversation of Paradise, where the believers, freed from every care, turn to one another in the lasting joy of shared reflection and mutual interest.",
  "51": "“I once had a companion in the world.” This verse records the beginning of a story told by one of the believers in Paradise. Turning to his fellow believers, he says, “I once had a companion in the world.” The words introduce a recollection of an earthly relationship, a companion who had been close to the believer during his life. The recollection sets up a remarkable scene, in which the believer will reflect on the fate of this former companion.\n\nThe phrase “in the world” is significant. It locates the relationship in the past, in the earthly life that the believer has now left behind. The companion belongs to the world, to the time before Paradise. The believer’s present state, in the Gardens of Bliss, is separated from that former companionship by the great divide of death and judgment. The recollection bridges that divide, as the believer, from the vantage point of Paradise, looks back on a relationship that ended differently than he might have hoped.\n\nThe word “companion” suggests closeness. This was not a passing acquaintance but a companion, someone with whom the believer spent time, shared experiences, perhaps even friendship. The closeness of the relationship adds poignancy to the story, because the companion, as the next verses will reveal, did not share the believer’s faith. The believer had a close companion in the world who rejected the truth, and the believer now reflects, from Paradise, on what became of him.\n\nThe recollection itself is part of the believers’ conversation in Paradise. They share their stories, their memories, their reflections. This particular believer chooses to share the story of a companion who doubted, a story that will highlight the mercy of God in saving the believer and the justice of God in dealing with the doubter. The sharing of the story serves both as gratitude for salvation and as reflection on the seriousness of unbelief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is the beginning of a powerful parable-within-Paradise, a story that illustrates the contrast between belief and unbelief through the relationship of two former companions. It invites us to consider our own companions and the paths we and they are walking, to recognise the seriousness of the choices that divide believer from unbeliever, and to reflect on the mercy of God that places some in Paradise and the justice that deals with those who refuse to believe. The verse challenges us to examine our closest relationships in the light of eternity, recognising that the companionships of this world will be sorted, on the Day of Judgment, into very different eternal destinies.",
  "52": "“Who used to ask me, Do you actually believe in resurrection?” This verse continues the believer’s recollection of his former companion. The companion used to ask him, with evident incredulity, whether he actually believed in resurrection. The question, recorded by the believer from the vantage point of Paradise, conveys the scornful skepticism of the companion during their earthly life. He could not believe that his friend actually accepted the doctrine of resurrection.\n\nThe form of the question, “Do you actually believe,” conveys astonishment and perhaps mockery. The companion found the believer’s faith in resurrection incredible, worthy of questioning rather than respect. The questioning was not sincere inquiry but a challenge to the believer’s intelligence or sanity for accepting what the companion considered absurd. The companion’s skepticism was a recurring feature of their relationship, a constant pressure on the believer’s faith.\n\nThe specific object of the companion’s skepticism was resurrection. This is significant, because resurrection is the doctrine that most directly challenges a materialist worldview. The companion could not accept that the dead would be raised, that decayed remains would be reassembled, that there would be a life after this life. His skepticism about resurrection was the symptom of a deeper unbelief, a refusal to accept the reality of God’s power and the accountability that resurrection implies.\n\nThe fact that the believer remembers this questioning from Paradise suggests the impression it made on him. The companion’s persistent skepticism was a real trial for the believer, a constant challenge to his faith. Yet the believer held firm, continuing to believe in resurrection despite his companion’s scorn. Now, in Paradise, the believer’s faith has been vindicated, and the companion’s skepticism has been exposed, as the next verses will show, in the most dramatic way.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the pressure that the faith of believers often faces from skeptical companions and a call to hold firm to the truth regardless of such pressure. It invites us to recognise that skepticism about the central truths of faith is common, that it can come even from close companions, and that the believer’s task is to remain steadfast, trusting the truth even when others question it. The verse encourages us to hold fast to our faith in the realities the world scoffs at, including resurrection and judgment, knowing that the faith the world questions will, in the end, be vindicated, and the skepticism that questioned it will be exposed, as the rest of this story will powerfully reveal.",
  "53": "“When we are dead and reduced to dust and bones, will we really be brought to judgment?” This verse records the specific form of the companion’s skeptical question. He asked, with scornful incredulity, whether he and the believer, once dead and reduced to dust and bones, would really be brought to judgment. The question is the classic expression of materialist skepticism: pointing to the thorough decomposition of the dead and concluding that judgment is impossible.\n\nThe question reveals the companion’s worldview. He measured possibility by the visible processes of decay. Once a body has become dust and bones, scattered and decomposed, he could not conceive of its reassembly for judgment. His reasoning ignored the power of God, the One who created from nothing and who could surely re-create from decay. The question expressed a confidence that death was the end, that decomposition was final, and that the notion of judgment after death was a fantasy.\n\nThe phrase “brought to judgment” is significant. The companion did not merely question resurrection in general but specifically the notion of being brought to judgment. Judgment implies accountability, the weighing of deeds, the assignment of reward or punishment. The companion’s skepticism about judgment reveals his deeper desire: to escape accountability. If there is no judgment, then life can be lived without reference to its consequences, without fear of accounting for one’s choices. The skepticism served a practical purpose: it freed the skeptic from the burden of moral responsibility.\n\nThe believer, recalling this question from Paradise, does so with the awareness that the question has now been definitively answered. The companion who scornfully asked about being brought to judgment has himself been brought to judgment. The very thing he doubted has happened to him. The recollection, from the vantage point of Paradise, is charged with irony: the skeptic who asked “will we really be brought to judgment?” has discovered, to his sorrow, that yes, he has been.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful exposure of the skepticism that denies judgment and a call to take seriously the reality that the skeptic denied. It invites us to recognise that skepticism about judgment often serves as a way to escape accountability, that the visible processes of decay do not limit the power of God, and that the judgment the skeptic questions is as certain as the resurrection he doubts. The verse challenges us to live in the awareness of the judgment that the companion scornfully questioned, knowing that the question has already been answered for countless souls and will be answered for all of us, and that the wise course is to prepare for that judgment now, in faith, rather than to mock it and discover its reality too late.",
  "54": "“Would you care to see his fate?” This verse records the believer’s question to his companions in Paradise, after recalling his skeptical former companion. He asks whether they would care to see the fate of that companion. The question is remarkable. The believer, from the vantage point of Paradise, is able to look and see what has become of his former companion. The question invites his fellow believers to share in the sight, to witness the dramatic vindication of faith and the equally dramatic exposure of skepticism.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in what it reveals about the awareness of the believers in Paradise. They are not ignorant of the fate of those who refused to believe. The believer knows where his former companion is, and he can show his companions. This awareness serves several purposes. It deepens the believer’s gratitude for his own salvation, as he sees what he escaped. It confirms the justice of God, as he sees the skeptic receiving the consequence of his unbelief. And it provides a powerful contrast that highlights the mercy shown to the believer.\n\nThe phrase “his fate” is sobering. The companion has a fate, a determined destiny, and it is not the destiny of the believer. The two former companions, who walked together in the world, who conversed and shared experiences, have arrived at radically different ends. The believer is in Paradise; the companion is elsewhere. The divergence of their fates is the direct result of the divergence of their beliefs: one believed, the other scoffed; one is saved, the other is lost.\n\nThe question “would you care to see” suggests that the sight is available, that the believer can direct his gaze, and the gaze of his companions, to the place where the former companion now is. The mechanics of this seeing belong to the realities of the Hereafter, which exceed our earthly understanding. What matters is the truth the scene conveys: that the fates of the believer and the skeptic are visibly different, and that the difference corresponds exactly to the choices each made.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a striking illustration of the contrast between the fate of the believer and the fate of the skeptic, made visible in the Hereafter. It invites us to recognise that the choices we make about faith have eternal consequences, that the believer and the skeptic, however close in this life, will end in very different places, and that the difference will be visible and undeniable. The verse challenges us to ensure that we are on the side of faith, so that when the fates are revealed, we are found among the saved, looking with gratitude at the mercy that rescued us, rather than among the lost, whose fate the saved behold as a confirmation of the justice and the seriousness of the choices we all must make.",
  "55": "“Then he and the others will look and spot him in the midst of the Hellfire.” This verse describes the fulfillment of the believer’s question. He and his companions look, and they spot the former companion in the midst of the Hellfire. The scene is dramatic and sobering. The companion who scornfully asked about being brought to judgment is now seen, by his former friend, in the very place of judgment’s consequence.\n\nThe act of looking and spotting conveys the clarity and certainty of the sight. The believer does not merely imagine or guess the fate of his companion; he sees it, clearly and definitely. The companion is spotted, identified, located in the midst of the Hellfire. The sight removes any doubt about the companion’s fate. The skeptic who questioned judgment is now visibly undergoing the judgment he denied.\n\nThe phrase “in the midst of the Hellfire” locates the companion precisely. He is not at the edge or the periphery but in the midst, fully within the punishment he earned through his unbelief. The location conveys the completeness of his fate. He is not partially punished or tentatively held; he is in the midst of the Fire, experiencing its full reality. The fate is as total as his unbelief was persistent.\n\nThe contrast between the believer, looking from Paradise, and the companion, seen in the Hellfire, is the climax of the story. The two former companions, who conversed in the world, who shared the experiences of earthly life, are now separated by the greatest possible distance: one in the Gardens of Bliss, the other in the midst of the Hellfire. The separation is the direct result of their divergent beliefs: the believer’s faith placed him in Paradise; the companion’s skepticism placed him in the Fire.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is one of the most sobering images in the Quran, a vivid illustration of the eternal consequences of the choices made in this life. It invites us to recognise that the divergence between belief and unbelief, however subtle it may seem in the conversation of earthly life, leads to a divergence visible and vast in the Hereafter. The verse challenges us to consider our own beliefs and the beliefs of those close to us, to recognise the seriousness of the choices that divide believer from skeptic, and to ensure that we, and those we love, are found among the believers, looking from Paradise, rather than among the skeptics, seen in the Fire, when the eternal fates are revealed and the temporary closeness of earthly companionship gives way to the permanent separation that faith and unbelief, between them, inevitably produce.",
  "56": "“By Allah! You nearly ruined me.” This verse records the words the believer speaks upon seeing his former companion in the Hellfire. He says, “By Allah! You nearly ruined me.” The exclamation “By Allah!” underscores the solemnity and the intensity of the moment. The believer, seeing the fate of his former companion, recognizes how close he himself came to sharing it. The companion’s influence nearly led the believer to ruin.\n\nThe phrase “you nearly ruined me” is revealing. It acknowledges the real danger the companion posed to the believer’s faith. The companion’s persistent skepticism, his scornful questioning of resurrection and judgment, was a genuine threat. Had the believer listened, had he let the skepticism erode his faith, he too would have ended in the Fire. The companion’s influence was nearly fatal to the believer’s eternal welfare. The word “nearly” carries immense weight: the believer escaped, but only just, only by the grace of God and by his own holding fast to faith.\n\nThe acknowledgment from Paradise is significant. The believer, now secure in the Gardens of Bliss, looks back and recognizes the danger he was in. In the world, the companion’s skepticism may have seemed harmless, just conversation, just a difference of opinion. From the vantage point of Paradise, with the companion’s fate visible in the Fire, the believer sees that the skepticism was poison, a force that nearly dragged him to ruin. The clarity of hindsight reveals the true nature of what seemed, at the time, like casual disagreement.\n\nThe verse serves as a warning about the influence of skeptical companions. The believer survived the influence, but he acknowledges how dangerous it was. The implication for the reader is clear: the skepticism of those around us is not a harmless difference of opinion but a real threat to our faith, a force that can, if we let it, drag us to the same ruin it brings to the skeptics themselves. The believer’s words from Paradise warn us to take seriously the spiritual danger of skeptical companionship.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful warning about the influence of others on our faith and a call to guard ourselves against skepticism. It invites us to recognise that the skepticism of companions is a real danger, that it can nearly, as the believer says, ruin us, and that we must hold fast to our faith despite the pressure of skeptical questioning. The verse challenges us to examine the influences around us, to resist the skepticism that threatens our belief, and to hold firm to the truth, knowing that the believer who survived the companion’s influence did so by the grace of God and by his own steadfastness, and that we too must depend on that grace and practice that steadfastness if we are to escape the ruin that skeptical companionship so nearly brings.",
  "57": "“Had it not been for the grace of my Lord, I too would have certainly been among those brought to Hell.” This verse records the believer’s continuing reflection upon seeing his former companion in the Fire. He acknowledges that he escaped the same fate only by the grace of his Lord. Had it not been for that grace, he too would certainly have been among those brought to Hell. The acknowledgment is one of profound gratitude and deep humility.\n\nThe believer’s recognition of God’s grace is the heart of the verse. He does not attribute his salvation to his own merit, his own intelligence, or his own strength. He attributes it entirely to the grace of his Lord. The companion is in the Fire; the believer is in Paradise; the difference is not that the believer was inherently better but that he received God’s grace. This recognition preserves the believer from pride and fills him with gratitude.\n\nThe phrase “I too would have certainly been among those brought to Hell” acknowledges the real possibility of the believer’s ruin. He was not immune to the fate he sees in his companion. He shared the human condition, the human vulnerability to unbelief, the human exposure to skepticism and temptation. Had God’s grace not intervened, the believer’s end would have been the same as the companion’s. The word “certainly” underscores the reality of the danger: apart from grace, ruin was certain.\n\nThe grace mentioned is comprehensive. It includes the grace that gave the believer faith in the first place, the grace that sustained his faith through the companion’s skepticism, the grace that kept him steadfast, and the grace that finally brought him to Paradise. Every step of the believer’s salvation, from the first stirring of faith to the entry into the Gardens of Bliss, is the work of God’s grace. The believer’s only contribution was his willingness to receive and cooperate with that grace.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a profound lesson in gratitude and humility and a call to depend wholly on God’s grace for our salvation. It invites us to recognise, as the believer did, that our escape from ruin is not our own achievement but God’s gift, that apart from His grace we too would certainly be among the lost, and that the fitting response is gratitude rather than pride. The verse challenges us to cultivate the humility that acknowledges our dependence on grace, to thank God continually for the salvation He has given us, and to depend on that same grace, rather than on our own strength, to keep us faithful to the end, so that we, like the believer in the story, escape the ruin that, without grace, would certainly have been ours.",
  "58": "“Can you imagine that we will never die, except our first death, nor be punished like the others?” This verse records the believer’s continuing words to his companions in Paradise, as he reflects on the blessedness of their state. He asks them to imagine the wonder of their situation: they will never die again, having already experienced their first death, and they will never be punished like the others. The question is one of marveling gratitude, an expression of amazement at the goodness of their eternal state.\n\nThe first part, “we will never die, except our first death,” reflects on the finality of the believer’s victory over death. The believer has already died once, the earthly death that ended his physical life. But that death was the last. In Paradise, there is no more death. The believer, having passed through death once, now lives forever, free from its shadow. The wonder of immortality, of a life that will never end, fills the believer with amazement and gratitude.\n\nThe second part, “nor be punished like the others,” reflects on the believer’s exemption from the punishment that befalls the wrongdoers. The believer has seen, or knows of, the fate of those who refused to believe, including his former companion. He marvels that he has been spared that fate. The contrast between his own blessed state and the punishment of the others deepens his appreciation of the mercy that saved him.\n\nThe form of a question, “can you imagine,” conveys the believer’s amazement. He can hardly believe the goodness of his situation. The wonder is not jaded or taken for granted; it is fresh, alive, full of marveling. Even in Paradise, the believer continues to reflect on the greatness of God’s gift, never ceasing to be amazed at the grace that brought him there and at the contrast between his eternal blessedness and the fate he escaped.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful expression of the believer’s marveling gratitude in Paradise and a call to cultivate such gratitude even now. It invites us to recognise the wonder of the salvation promised to the believers: a life that will never end, free from every death and every punishment, and to begin now the gratitude that will fill Paradise. The verse encourages us to reflect on the greatness of God’s gift, to marvel at the grace that saves us from death and punishment, and to live in the grateful amazement that the believer’s words express, allowing the wonder of our promised eternal blessedness to fill our present lives with thanksgiving and joy.",
  "59": "“Except our first death, nor be punished like the others.” This verse completes the thought of the previous one, stating the two great blessings that fill the believer with wonder: the first death, already past, after which there will be no more dying; and the exemption from the punishment that befalls the wrongdoers. The two blessings together define the security and the joy of the believer’s eternal state.\n\nThe mention of “our first death” is significant. The believer does not pretend that death never happened. He died, as every human being does. But that death was the first and the last, the only death he would ever experience. Beyond it lies eternal life, with no further death to fear. The acknowledgment of the first death makes the promise of no more dying even more precious: the believer has tasted death once and will never taste it again. The finality of death’s defeat is complete.\n\nThe exemption from punishment is the second great blessing. The believer, who might have deserved punishment for his sins, has been spared it through God’s grace and forgiveness. He sees the punishment of the wrongdoers and recognizes, with deep gratitude, that he has been rescued from it. The exemption is not because the believer was sinless but because he received God’s mercy, which covered his sins and spared him their consequence.\n\nThe two blessings are connected. The defeat of death and the exemption from punishment together constitute the believer’s complete salvation. Death, the great enemy, has been rendered harmless, experienced once and never again. Punishment, the just consequence of sin, has been averted through mercy. What remains for the believer is eternal life, free from both death and punishment, in the enjoyment of God’s favor and the delights of Paradise.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a concise summary of the believer’s complete salvation and a call to live in the gratitude and hope that such salvation inspires. It invites us to recognise that the believer’s eternal state is defined by two great deliverances: from death, which is defeated, and from punishment, which is averted. The verse encourages us to live now in the light of these deliverances, grateful for the mercy that spares us from punishment and hopeful for the eternal life that defeats death, allowing the security of our promised salvation to fill our present lives with the peace and joy that come from knowing that, for the believer, the first death will be the last and the punishment of the wicked will never touch us.",
  "60": "“This is truly the ultimate triumph.” This verse records the believer’s conclusion about the blessed state he and his companions enjoy in Paradise. He declares it to be the ultimate triumph. The word “triumph” conveys victory, success, the achievement of the highest goal. And it is “ultimate,” meaning the greatest, the final, the unsurpassable. The believer recognizes that the state he now enjoys is the highest possible success, the fulfillment of every worthy aspiration.\n\nThe significance of calling Paradise “the ultimate triumph” lies in the redefinition of success it implies. In the world, success is measured by wealth, power, status, pleasure, or achievement. The believer, looking from Paradise, declares all these measures inadequate. The true success, the ultimate triumph, is not any worldly attainment but the attainment of Paradise, the salvation of the soul, the eternal enjoyment of God’s favor. Compared to this, every worldly success is trivial; compared to this, every worldly failure is bearable.\n\nThe word “truly” emphasizes the reality of the triumph. This is not an imagined or hoped-for success but a real, attained, experienced one. The believer is not anticipating the triumph; he is living it. The reality of the triumph, as opposed to the uncertainty of worldly success, underscores its value. Worldly successes are uncertain, partial, and temporary; the triumph of Paradise is certain, complete, and eternal.\n\nThe triumph is “ultimate” in the sense that nothing surpasses it. There is no higher goal to which the believer could aspire, no greater good that could be added. Paradise, with its deliverance from death and punishment, its enjoyment of God’s favor, its delights and honors, is the summit of human aspiration. To attain it is to have arrived at the ultimate purpose of existence. The believer’s declaration honors the greatness of the gift by recognizing it as the highest possible good.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful redefinition of success and a call to orient our lives toward the ultimate triumph. It invites us to recognise that the true measure of success is not worldly attainment but the attainment of Paradise, and to order our priorities accordingly. The verse challenges us to pursue the ultimate triumph above every lesser goal, to value the salvation of our souls above every worldly achievement, and to live now in a way that aims at the triumph the believer celebrates, knowing that compared to the ultimate triumph of Paradise, every worldly success is as nothing, and every sacrifice made for the sake of faith is the best investment we could possibly make.",
  "61": "“For such honour all should strive.” This verse draws the practical conclusion from the believer’s declaration that Paradise is the ultimate triumph. If Paradise is the highest good, the ultimate success, then it is worthy of the greatest effort. The verse states that all should strive for such honor. The striving is not optional or casual; it is the fitting response to the magnitude of the prize.\n\nThe word “strive” is significant. It suggests effort, exertion, the giving of oneself fully to a goal. The attainment of Paradise is not passive; it requires striving. This does not mean that salvation is earned by human effort alone, for it is the gift of God’s grace. But it does mean that the believer is called to active effort: to faith, to obedience, to perseverance, to the struggle against sin and temptation. The striving is the human side of the salvation that God’s grace makes possible.\n\nThe honor mentioned is the blessed state of Paradise: the deliverance from death and punishment, the enjoyment of God’s favor, the delights and dignities of the Gardens of Bliss. This honor, the verse declares, is worthy of striving. It is a prize so great that every effort made for its sake is well spent. The recognition of the magnitude of the prize fuels the magnitude of the effort. The believer who grasps what is at stake will strive with all his or her being.\n\nThe universality of the call, “all should strive,” is significant. The striving is not for a select few but for everyone. Every human being is invited to strive for the honor of Paradise. The invitation is universal because the need is universal and the prize is available to all who will turn to God in faith and live accordingly. The universality of the call underscores the seriousness of the choice: all are invited, all should strive, and those who refuse to strive forfeit the honor that striving would have gained.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to wholehearted striving for the honor of Paradise and a reminder of the worthiness of such striving. It invites us to recognise that the prize is so great that it deserves our fullest effort, and to give that effort, day by day, through faith, obedience, and perseverance. The verse challenges us to examine whether we are truly striving for the ultimate honor or whether we have grown complacent, and to renew our effort, knowing that the honor we strive for is worth every sacrifice, every struggle, and every act of faithful endurance, because it is the ultimate triumph, the highest good, the eternal blessedness for which all who understand its value will surely strive.",
  "62": "“Is this bliss a better accommodation or the tree of Zaqqûm?” This verse poses a stark question that contrasts the bliss of Paradise with the tree of Zaqqum, a notorious tree of Hell. The question asks which is the better accommodation: the bliss just described, with its fruits, its drinks, its honor, its gardens, or the tree of Zaqqum, a tree whose fruit is a torment for the wrongdoers. The question is rhetorical; the answer is obvious. The bliss is infinitely better. But the asking of the question serves to sharpen the contrast and to drive home the seriousness of the choice between belief and unbelief.\n\nThe “tree of Zaqqum” is a fearful symbol in the Quranic description of Hell. It is a tree that grows in the Fire, whose fruit is bitter, harmful, and tormenting, the food of the wrongdoers in Hell. The very name “Zaqqum” carries a sense of dread. The tree represents the perverse provision of Hell: while the believers in Paradise are fed with fruits of every type, the wrongdoers in Hell are fed with the fruit of Zaqqum, a food that increases rather than relieves their suffering.\n\nThe contrast between the bliss and the tree of Zaqqum is the contrast between two accommodations, two dwellings, two provisions. The believers are accommodated in Gardens of Bliss, with fruits and drinks and honor. The wrongdoers are accommodated with the tree of Zaqqum, with bitter fruit and torment. The two accommodations represent the two possible destinies, and the question asks which is better. The obvious answer, the bliss, underscores the tragedy of those who choose the path that leads to the tree.\n\nThe asking of the question, rather than merely stating the contrast, engages the listener directly. It forces the listener to consider the two options, to weigh them, to recognize the vast difference between them. The engagement is meant to produce a choice: given such a contrast, which accommodation would any reasonable person prefer? And if the bliss is better, then the path that leads to it, the path of faith, is the path to choose.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful presentation of the contrast between the destiny of the believers and the destiny of the wrongdoers, designed to clarify the choice and motivate the right decision. It invites us to weigh the two accommodations, to recognize the vast superiority of the bliss over the tree of Zaqqum, and to choose the path of faith that leads to the bliss. The verse challenges us to live in the awareness of this stark contrast, allowing the beauty of the one destiny and the horror of the other to shape our choices, so that we strive for the bliss and avoid the tree, knowing that the choice between them is the most important choice we will ever make.",
  "63": "“We have surely made it a test for the wrongdoers.” This verse states the purpose of the tree of Zaqqum: it is a test for the wrongdoers. The word “test” here carries the sense of a trial, an affliction, something that tries and torments those who experience it. The tree is part of the punishment of Hell, a specific torment designed for the wrongdoers, a test that they must endure as part of their recompense.\n\nThe description of the tree as a test reveals the nature of the punishment. It is not arbitrary or purposeless but is fitted to those who receive it. The wrongdoers, who in this life refused the tests of faith and obedience, now face a different kind of test, the test of the tree of Zaqqum, a torment that tries them and from which there is no escape. The test is part of the perfect justice of the punishment: those who would not pass the tests of faith now face a test they cannot avoid.\n\nThe word “surely” emphasizes the certainty that the tree is a test for the wrongdoers. It is not a possibility but a settled fact. The wrongdoers will face this test; the tree is prepared for them. The certainty underscores the seriousness of wrongdoing and the inevitability of its consequence. Those who choose the path of wrongdoing choose, whether they know it or not, the path that leads to the test of Zaqqum.\n\nThis verse, following the question about whether the bliss or the tree is the better accommodation, explains why the tree exists: it is a test for the wrongdoers. The tree is not a random feature of Hell but a purposeful element of the punishment, designed to try those who earned it through their wrongdoing. The purposefulness of the tree reflects the purposefulness of all of God’s dealings: nothing is arbitrary, everything has a reason, and the test of Zaqqum is the fitting consequence of a life of wrongdoing.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering statement about the purposeful nature of the punishment of Hell and a warning about the test that awaits the wrongdoers. It invites us to recognise that the tree of Zaqqum is real, that it is a test prepared specifically for those who do wrong, and that the way to avoid it is to cease from wrongdoing and turn to God in faith. The verse challenges us to examine our own lives for wrongdoing, to repent and turn away from it while there is time, and to choose the path that leads to the bliss of Paradise rather than the path that leads to the test of Zaqqum, knowing that the choice we make now determines which test we will face: the joyous test of faithful striving, which leads to bliss, or the dreadful test of Zaqqum, which is the consequence of wrongdoing refused and unrepented.",
  "64": "“Indeed, it is a tree that grows in the depths of Hell.” This verse describes the location of the tree of Zaqqum. It grows in the depths of Hell, in the very bottom of the punishment. The image is striking: a tree that grows not in fertile soil under a beneficent sun but in the depths of Hell, in the place of greatest torment. The very existence of a tree in such a place is part of its horror, a perverse inversion of the trees that grow in the gardens of Paradise.\n\nThe contrast with the trees of Paradise is deliberate and powerful. In Paradise, trees grow in beauty, providing shade, fruit, and delight. In Hell, the tree of Zaqqum grows in the depths, providing bitter, tormenting fruit. The two kinds of tree represent the two kinds of provision: the good provision of the blessed and the terrible provision of the damned. The contrast underscores the completeness of the division between the two destinies.\n\nThe location “in the depths of Hell” conveys the severity of the punishment associated with the tree. The depths are the lowest, the most intense, the most inescapable part of the punishment. The tree grows where the torment is greatest, and its fruit is part of that torment. The wrongdoers who are fed from it are in the worst possible place, receiving the worst possible food, as the just consequence of their wrongdoing.\n\nThe certainty of the description, marked by “indeed,” underscores the reality of the tree. It is not a metaphor or a symbol only but a real feature of the punishment, described so that the listener may take warning. The reality of the tree is meant to produce the seriousness that leads to repentance. To disbelieve in the tree, to dismiss it as a fiction, is to remain on the path that leads to it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering description of one of the features of Hell and a warning about the reality of the punishment. It invites us to recognise that the tree of Zaqqum is real, that it grows in the depths of Hell, and that its existence is part of the just consequence of wrongdoing. The verse challenges us to take seriously the punishment that awaits the wrongdoers, to use the fear of it as a motive for repentance and righteousness, and to choose the path that leads to the trees of Paradise rather than the path that leads to the tree that grows in the depths of Hell, knowing that the contrast between the two is as great as the contrast between the destinies they respectively nourish.",
  "65": "“Bearing fruit like devils’ heads.” This verse describes the appearance of the fruit of the tree of Zaqqum. Its fruit resembles devils’ heads. The image is grotesque and horrifying, deliberately so. The fruit that the wrongdoers must eat is not pleasant to look at but repellent, shaped like the heads of devils. The appearance of the fruit is part of its torment: even before it is eaten, it horrifies; and the eating of it is an act of desperate compulsion rather than appetite.\n\nThe choice of the image, devils’ heads, is significant. The devils are the enemies of humanity, the beings who tempt and mislead. The fruit that resembles them suggests a connection between the wrongdoers’ sin and their punishment. Having followed the whispers of devils in this life, the wrongdoers now feed on fruit that resembles them. The punishment reflects the sin: the company they kept in unbelief becomes, in a sense, the food they eat in punishment.\n\nThe grotesque nature of the image serves the purpose of deterrence. The Quran does not shy away from vivid, even horrifying, descriptions of Hell, because the reality is horrifying and because the horror is meant to wake the living to the danger. A mild description of Hell would fail to convey the seriousness of the punishment and would fail to motivate the repentance and righteousness that the warning is meant to produce. The grotesque fruit is part of the honest, unflinching presentation of the consequence of wrongdoing.\n\nThe contrast with the fruit of Paradise is complete. The believers eat fruits of every type, beautiful, delicious, delightful to behold and to taste. The wrongdoers eat the fruit of Zaqqum, resembling devils’ heads, horrifying to behold and tormenting to taste. The two fruits represent the two provisions, and the vast difference between them represents the vast difference between the two destinies. The choice between them is the choice between belief and unbelief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid and sobering description of the punishment of Hell and a powerful warning about the consequence of wrongdoing. It invites us to recognise the horror of the fate that awaits the wrongdoers, the grotesque nature of their provision, and the stark contrast with the delightful provision of Paradise. The verse challenges us to choose, by faith and righteousness, the fruit of Paradise rather than the fruit of Zaqqum, knowing that the appearance of the one and the appearance of the other reflect the nature of the choices that lead to them, and that the beauty of the one destiny and the horror of the other are both real and both eternal.",
  "66": "“The evildoers will certainly be left to eat from it, filling up their bellies with it.” This verse describes the consumption of the fruit of Zaqqum by the evildoers. They will certainly eat from it, and they will fill their bellies with it. The certainty is marked by the emphatic “certainly.” The eating is not optional or occasional; it is the food of the damned, and they will consume it, filling themselves with its torment.\n\nThe phrase “filling up their bellies with it” conveys the compulsion and the quantity of the eating. The evildoers do not merely taste the fruit; they fill their bellies with it. The image suggests hunger so extreme that even the horrifying, tormenting fruit is consumed in quantity, filling the stomach. The very fullness of the eating is part of the torment, since the fruit causes suffering with every bite, and the filling of the belly means the maximum intake of that suffering.\n\nThe reason for this terrible feeding is suggested by the context. The evildoers, in Hell, experience hunger, and the only food available is the fruit of Zaqqum. Their hunger drives them to eat what would, in any other circumstance, be unthinkable. The eating is both a necessity, driven by hunger, and a torment, since the food itself causes suffering. The combination of necessity and torment is part of the ingenuity of the punishment: the evildoers are driven to consume the very thing that increases their suffering.\n\nThe word “left” suggests abandonment. The evildoers are left to eat the fruit, with no alternative, no relief, no better provision. They are abandoned to the tree and its fruit, with nothing else to sustain them. The abandonment is part of the punishment: those who turned from God find themselves turned from, left to the consequences of their choices, with no rescue and no alternative.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering description of the feeding of the damned and a warning about the fate that awaits the evildoers. It invites us to recognise the horror of a hunger that can only be fed with tormenting fruit, the abandonment that leaves the damned with no alternative, and the certainty of this fate for those who choose evil. The verse challenges us to choose, by faith and righteousness, the path that leads to the fruits of Paradise rather than the path that leads to the filling of the belly with Zaqqum, knowing that the contrast between the two provisions is as great as the contrast between the two choices that earn them, and that the fate we choose in this life determines the food we receive in the next.",
  "67": "“Then on top of that they will be given a blend of boiling drink.” This verse adds another layer to the description of the punishment. After eating the fruit of Zaqqum and filling their bellies with it, the evildoers will be given a drink, described as a blend of boiling liquid. The drink, far from relieving the torment of the fruit, adds to it. The boiling drink, poured over the suffering caused by the fruit, intensifies the agony.\n\nThe phrase “on top of that” is significant. It suggests layering, the adding of one torment upon another. The punishment is not a single, uniform suffering but a composite of multiple torments, each adding to the others. The fruit torments; then the drink torments on top of the fruit. The accumulation conveys the comprehensive nature of the suffering, which engages the evildoers from every direction and in every form.\n\nThe description of the drink as a “blend of boiling” conveys its nature. It is hot, scalding, a liquid that burns as it is consumed. The thirst that the evildoers suffer, after eating the tormenting fruit, drives them to drink, but the only drink available is boiling, which burns rather than refreshes. The combination of thirst and scalding drink is another instance of the punishment’s cruel ingenuity: the need drives to the drink, and the drink intensifies the suffering.\n\nThe contrast with the drink of Paradise is complete. The believers drink pure, crystal-white, delicious wine from flowing streams, a drink that delights and never harms. The evildoers drink a blend of boiling liquid, a drink that burns and torments. The two drinks represent the two provisions, and the vast difference between them represents the vast difference between the two destinies. The choice between them is the choice between belief and unbelief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a further description of the layered torments of Hell and a warning about the comprehensive nature of the punishment. It invites us to recognise that the punishment of the evildoers is not a single suffering but an accumulation of torments, each adding to the others, and that the contrast with the delights of Paradise is complete in every dimension, including the drink. The verse challenges us to choose the path that leads to the pure, delightful drink of Paradise rather than the path that leads to the boiling, tormenting drink of Hell, knowing that the contrast between the two is as great as the contrast between the choices that lead to them, and that the destiny we earn in this life determines the drink we receive in the next.",
  "68": "“Then they will ultimately return to their place in Hell.” This verse describes the final stage of the cycle of punishment. After eating the fruit of Zaqqum and drinking the boiling liquid, the evildoers will return to their place in Hell. The word “return” suggests that they have been somewhere, perhaps to the tree to eat, and now they go back to the Fire itself. The cycle continues: they go to the tree, eat, drink, and return to the Fire, only to begin again.\n\nThe cyclical nature of the description conveys the endlessness of the punishment. The evildoers do not suffer once and then rest; they suffer, go to the tree, eat, drink, return to the Fire, and repeat. The cycle has no conclusion, no end point at which the suffering ceases. The return to the Fire is the return to the beginning of the cycle, which will play out again, indefinitely, forever. The endlessness is itself part of the torment.\n\nThe phrase “their place in Hell” is significant. The evildoers have a place, a designated location, in Hell. It is their place, the place prepared for them, the place they earned through their choices. The personalization of the place, “their place,” underscores the justice of the punishment: each evildoer receives the specific place that corresponds to their specific deeds. The place is not random but fitted to the one who occupies it.\n\nThe word “ultimately” suggests that the return to the Fire is the final destination of each cycle, the place to which all the other torments lead. The eating and drinking are interruptions, excursions from the Fire, but the Fire is the home, the place to which the evildoers always return. The Fire is the central reality of the punishment, and the other torments, the tree and the drink, are additional layers that surround and intensify it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the cyclical, endless nature of the punishment of Hell and a warning about the permanence of the fate that awaits the evildoers. It invites us to recognise that the punishment is not a single event but an ongoing cycle, that the evildoers have a place in the Fire to which they always return, and that the endlessness of the cycle is part of the torment. The verse challenges us to choose, by faith and righteousness, the path that leads to the eternal rest of Paradise rather than the path that leads to the endless cycle of Hell, knowing that the two destinies are as different as their durations: one an unending joy, the other an unending torment, each the fitting consequence of the choices made in this life.",
  "69": "“Indeed, they found their forefathers astray.” This verse begins to explain the root of the wrongdoers’ error. They found their forefathers astray. The error was not original to them but inherited. They discovered that the generations before them had gone astray from the path of true faith, and they, rather than correcting the error, accepted it and continued in it. The appeal to ancestral tradition is identified as a key factor in the persistence of misguidance.\n\nThe significance of this explanation lies in what it reveals about the transmission of error. Misguidance is often not the product of fresh investigation but of inherited assumption. The wrongdoers did not independently arrive at their unbelief; they found it already established among their forefathers and adopted it. The path of error was well-worn by those who went before, and the wrongdoers simply followed it, without examining whether it was the right path.\n\nThe phrase “found their forefathers astray” contains a sad irony. The wrongdoers found their forefathers astray, which is to say they recognized that the older generations were not on the right path. And yet, despite this recognition, they did not correct the error. They continued in the astray path of their forefathers, preferring inherited error to the effort of finding and following the truth. The recognition without correction deepens their responsibility: they knew the path was wrong, but they stayed on it.\n\nThe mention of forefathers connects this verse to a recurring theme in the Quran: the appeal to ancestral custom as a justification for unbelief. Throughout the Quran, the disbelievers defend their rejection by pointing to the ways of their forefathers: we follow what we found our fathers doing. The Quran consistently challenges this defense, pointing out that the forefathers’ ways are not a proof of truth but may be a path of inherited error. The obligation to seek and follow the truth overrides the obligation to imitate the ancestors.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the danger of inherited error and a call to examine the beliefs we have received. It invites us to recognise that the ways of our forefathers may be astray, that tradition is not a proof of truth, and that each generation, and each individual, has the responsibility to seek and follow the truth rather than blindly continuing in inherited error. The verse challenges us to examine the beliefs and practices we have received, to test them against the truth God has revealed, and to have the courage to depart from inherited error when the truth requires it, rather than following, as the wrongdoers did, the astray footsteps of those who went before.",
  "70": "“So they rushed in their footsteps!” This verse continues the thought of the previous one with a note of astonishment. The wrongdoers not only found their forefathers astray; they rushed in their footsteps. The word “rushed” conveys eagerness and speed. The wrongdoers did not reluctantly or cautiously follow the error of their forefathers; they rushed after it, eager to continue in the same path. The eagerness makes their error more inexcusable.\n\nThe word “rushed” is striking because it suggests the opposite of the careful consideration that important choices deserve. When the path is uncertain, when the forefathers are astray, the wise response is to slow down, to examine, to seek the truth. The wrongdoers did the opposite: they rushed, hurrying along the path of error without pausing to consider where it led. The rushing reflects a kind of intellectual and spiritual recklessness, a refusal to think that compounded the inherited error.\n\nThe image of following “in their footsteps” conveys the idea of treading the exact same path. The wrongdoers did not merely make similar errors; they followed the same footsteps, walking precisely where their forefathers had walked. The image suggests conformity, imitation, the absence of independent judgment. The footsteps were already laid down, and the wrongdoers simply placed their feet in them, without asking whether the path was right.\n\nThe exclamation mark, conveyed by the tone of the verse, expresses a kind of marveling sorrow. How could they rush to follow a path that was demonstrably wrong? The verse marvels at the eagerness with which the wrongdoers embraced inherited error, at their refusal to pause, examine, and seek the truth. The marveling underscores the inexcusability of their choice: they had every opportunity to think, but they rushed instead.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against the eager, thoughtless adoption of inherited error and a call to careful, independent seeking of the truth. It invites us to recognise the danger of rushing in the footsteps of those who went before without examining whether their path was right, and to cultivate the careful consideration that important choices deserve. The verse challenges us not to rush blindly after tradition, however venerable, but to test every inherited path against the truth God has revealed, and to follow the truth even when it requires departing from the footsteps of our forefathers, knowing that the eager following of inherited error is no excuse and that each of us is responsible for the path we choose to walk.",
  "71": "“And surely most of the earlier generations had strayed before them.” This verse broadens the observation about inherited error into a general statement about human history. Most of the earlier generations had strayed. The error was not unique to the wrongdoers or their immediate forefathers; it was the common condition of the majority throughout history. The statement presents straying as the default, the common outcome, against which the guided are the exception.\n\nThe word “surely” emphasizes the certainty of the observation. It is a settled fact, established by the record of history, that most of the earlier generations went astray. The certainty underscores the seriousness of the human tendency toward error. The straying of the majority is not an occasional accident but a consistent pattern, observed across ages and peoples. The pattern should make every generation alert to the danger of following the crowd.\n\nThe phrase “most of the earlier generations” quantifies the pattern. Not all, but most. The majority of past generations strayed. This means that the path of truth has typically been the path of the minority, while the path of error has been the broad, crowded way of the majority. The observation has a sobering implication: to follow the majority is to follow a path that has, more often than not, led to ruin. The size of the crowd is no proof of the correctness of its direction.\n\nThe connection to the previous verses is significant. The wrongdoers found their forefathers astray and rushed in their footsteps. Now we learn that this pattern, of one generation inheriting and continuing the error of the previous, has played out across most of human history. The straying of the forefathers was itself the continuation of the straying of their forefathers, and so on, back through the generations. The error has been transmitted, with rare interruptions of prophetic guidance, through most of human history.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering reminder of the prevalence of error throughout human history and a warning about the danger of following the majority. It invites us to recognise that the path of truth has often been the path of the few, that the majority has frequently strayed, and that the size of the crowd is no evidence of the truth of its direction. The verse challenges us to seek the truth independently of the crowd, to be willing to walk the narrower path of the guided minority rather than the broad path of the straying majority, and to examine every inherited and popular belief against the standard of God’s revealed truth, knowing that the prevalence of an error does not make it true, and that the straying of most earlier generations is a warning rather than an example to follow.",
  "72": "“Although We had certainly sent warners among them.” This verse adds a crucial qualification to the statement that most earlier generations strayed. They did not stray without warning. God had certainly sent warners among them. The straying of the majority was not the result of ignorance or neglect on God’s part but of the refusal of the warned. The warners came, and the majority refused them, choosing error despite the warning.\n\nThe word “certainly” emphasizes the fact of the warning. God did not leave the earlier generations without guidance. He sent warners, messengers who alerted them to the truth and its consequences. The sending of warners was a consistent feature of God’s dealings with humanity: whenever a people strayed, a warner was sent to call them back. The consistency of the warning underscores the consistency of God’s care and the inexcusability of the straying.\n\nThe significance of this verse lies in what it establishes about the justice of God. The earlier generations were not punished for straying without warning; they were punished for straying despite warning. The warners came, the truth was made clear, the consequences were explained, and the majority still chose error. Their straying, in the face of clear warning, was a deliberate refusal, not an innocent mistake. The justice of the punishment that followed rested on the clarity of the warning that preceded it.\n\nThe connection to the previous verses is important. The wrongdoers followed the error of their forefathers, and most earlier generations strayed, but in every case, warners were sent. The pattern, then, is not one of God’s neglect but of human refusal. The warners came to every straying generation; the majority refused them; the error continued through inheritance and imitation. At every stage, the opportunity to return was offered, and at every stage, the majority refused it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the universality of God’s warning and the inexcusability of straying despite it. It invites us to recognise that God has not left humanity without guidance, that warners have been sent to every people, and that straying in the face of such warning is a deliberate refusal rather than an innocent error. The verse challenges us to receive the warners God has sent, to heed the warning they bring, and to avoid the fate of the majority who, despite being warned, chose error and passed it on, knowing that the warning we have received is the same warning they received, and that our response to it will determine, as theirs did, whether we join the straying majority or the guided minority who heeded the warners and walked the path of truth.",
  "73": "“See then what was the end of those who had been warned.” This verse invites reflection on the fate of the earlier generations who were warned but refused the warning. It asks the listener to consider what was the end of those who had been warned, meaning the consequence that overtook them despite, and because of, their refusal. The invitation is meant to draw a lesson from history that applies to the present.\n\nThe significance of the invitation lies in its use of history as a teacher. The ends of the warned-but-refusing generations are matters of record, visible in their ruins and remembered in their stories. By inviting the listener to see these ends, the verse turns history into a warning. The fate of those who refused the warning, despite having received it, becomes a forecast of the fate of anyone who refuses the warning now.\n\nThe phrase “those who had been warned” is important. These were not people who lacked guidance; they had been warned. Their end was not the result of ignorance but of refused warning. The distinction matters because it establishes the justice of their fate. They knew, because they were warned; they refused, because they chose to; and their end was the consequence of that refusal. The same justice applies to anyone who, having been warned, chooses to refuse.\n\nThe word “see” invites active reflection, not passive observation. To see the end of the warned is to draw the obvious conclusion: that refusing the warning leads to ruin. The seeing is meant to produce a change in the seer, a turning from the path of refusal to the path of heeding. The verse uses the lessons of history to motivate present repentance, showing the listener, through the fate of others, where the path of refusal leads.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to learn from the fate of those who were warned but refused and to apply the lesson to our own lives. It invites us to look at the ends of the earlier generations, to recognise that their ruin was the consequence of refused warning, and to resolve not to repeat their refusal. The verse challenges us to heed the warning we have received, learning from the fate of those who did not, and to choose the path of heeding rather than the path of refusing, knowing that the end of the warned-but-refusing is visible in history as a forecast of the end of anyone who, despite warning, chooses the path that leads to ruin.",
  "74": "“But not the chosen servants of Allah.” This verse repeats, with slight variation, the exception introduced earlier in the chapter. The punishment and ruin that overtook the warned-but-refusing majority did not touch the chosen servants of Allah. They are the exception, the minority who, unlike the straying generations, heeded the warning, remained faithful, and were saved. The repetition of the exception underscores its importance and offers hope amid the warnings.\n\nThe significance of the exception lies in what it reveals about the possibility of salvation even in the midst of widespread straying. Most generations strayed; most refused the warning; most faced the ruinous end. But not all. There was a minority, the chosen servants of Allah, who escaped the common fate. Their existence proves that straying is not inevitable, that the warning can be heeded, that salvation is possible even when the majority goes astray.\n\nThe phrase “chosen servants of Allah” identifies the saved minority with precision. They are servants, living in the service of God rather than in the service of their own desires or the ways of the majority. And they are chosen, selected by God for His favor, not through their own merit but through His grace. The combination of servanthood and chosenness describes the believer: one who serves God and who has been graciously chosen by Him.\n\nThe placement of this verse, immediately after the invitation to see the end of the warned, creates a powerful contrast. The end of the warned-but-refusing was ruin. But the chosen servants of Allah did not share that end. Their end was different, because their response to the warning was different. They heeded; the majority refused; the ends corresponded to the responses. The contrast confirms the principle that runs through the whole chapter: the destiny of each person corresponds to their response to the truth.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a message of hope and a call to join the saved minority. It invites us to recognise that straying is not inevitable, that the warning can be heeded, and that the chosen servants of Allah are the proof that salvation is possible even in a straying world. The verse challenges us to take our place among the chosen servants, serving God with sincerity and trusting His gracious choice, so that we do not share the end of the warned-but-refusing majority but instead enjoy the salvation and the reward that God grants to those who, unlike the majority, heed His warning and remain faithful to the truth He has sent.",
  "75": "“Indeed, Noah cried out to Us, and how excellent are We in responding!”” records the cry, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the cry: or \"wa laqad nadana Noohun fa-lani'ma al-mujeeboon,\" and the commentators note the cry, for Noah cried and the Lord responded, and the cry is the prayer, the recourse of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the response is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cry is the prayer and the response is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the cry, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the prophet is the recourse, and the one who cries is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the cry of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Noah cried and was answered, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the cry is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the cry, and the teaching of the trust that the prayer produces.",
  "76": "“We delivered him and his family from the great distress,” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the deliverance: or \"wa najjaynahu wa ahlahu mina al-karbi al-'azeem,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved Noah and the family, and the deliverance is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the rescue is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: Noah was delivered, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "77": "“and made his descendants the sole survivors.”” records the survivors, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the survivors: or \"wa ja'alna thurriyyatahu humu al-baqeen,\" and the commentators note the survivors, for the Lord made the descendants the survivors, and the survivors are the reward, the grant of the continuity, and the verse is the record of the survivors, the reward that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the continuity is the reward, and the verse is the record of the survivors, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the survivors are the reward and the continuity is the grant, and the verse is the record of the survivors, the reward that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the survivors, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the continuity of the faith is the reward, and the one who is saved is the one whose line the Lord preserves, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose continuity the Lord grants, and the survivors of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the continuity is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the survivors is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the continuity of the faith: the descendants survived, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose continuity the Lord grants, and the survivors are the teaching that the continuity is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the survivors, and the teaching of the hope that the continuity carries.",
  "78": "“And We blessed him ˹with honourable mention˺ among later generations:” records the mention, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mention: or \"wa tarakna 'alayhi fi al-akhireen,\" and the commentators note the mention, for the Lord blessed Noah with the mention, and the mention is the reward, the grant of the good name, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention is the reward, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mention is the reward and the name is the grant, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mention, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the good name is the reward, and the one who is mentioned is the one whose name the Lord raises, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord grants, and the mention of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the good name: Noah was mentioned, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord raises, and the mention is the teaching that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the mention, and the teaching of the hope that the name carries.",
  "79": "““Peace be upon Noah among all peoples.”” records the peace, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the peace: or \"salamun 'ala Noohin fi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the peace, for the Lord granted Noah the peace, and the peace is the reward, the grant of the safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace is the reward, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace is the reward and the safety is the grant, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose peace the Lord gives, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the peace is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the Lord: Noah was granted the peace, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace is the teaching that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the peace, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "80": "“Indeed, this is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the principle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the principle: or \"inna kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,\" and the commentators note the principle, for the Lord declared the reward of the good-doers, and the principle is the teaching, the declaration of the recompense, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reward is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the principle is the teaching and the reward is the good, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the principle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the reward of the good-doers is the promise, and the one who does the good is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the principle of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the principle is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the good: the good-doers are rewarded, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the principle is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the principle, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "81": "“˹For˺ he was truly one of Our faithful servants.”” records the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the description: or \"innahu min 'ibadina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the description, for the Lord described Noah, and the description is the praise, the declaration of the faith, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the faith is the praise, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the praise and the faith is the servant, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the praise, and the one who is faithful is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the description of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the servant: Noah was the faithful, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the faith that the servant requires.",
  "82": "“Then We drowned the others.”” records the drowning, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the drowning: or \"thumma aghraqna al-akhareen,\" and the commentators note the drowning, for the Lord drowned the others, and the drowning is the judgment, the end of the deniers, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the drowning is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the drowning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the drowning of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the drowning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the deniers drowned and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the drowning, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "83": "“And indeed, one of those who followed his way was Abraham.”” records Abraham, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Abraham: or \"wa inna min shi'atihi la-Ibraheem,\" and the commentators note Abraham, for the Lord declared Abraham of the way of Noah, and the verse is the record of Abraham, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the way is the faith, and the verse is the record of Abraham, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the way is the faith, and the verse is the record of Abraham, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the following of the way is the faith, and the one who follows is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the way: Abraham followed the way of Noah, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Abraham, and the teaching of the faith that the way requires.",
  "84": "“˹Remember˺ when he came to his Lord with a pure heart.”” records the heart, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the heart: or \"ith ja'a rabbahu bi-qalbin saleem,\" and the commentators note the heart, for Abraham came with the pure heart, and the heart is the praise, the declaration of the soundness, and the verse is the record of the heart, the praise that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"qalb saleem\" is the sound heart, and the verse is the record of the heart, the praise of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the heart is the praise and the soundness is the faith, and the verse is the record of the heart, the praise that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the heart, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the sound heart is the praise, and the one who purifies is the one whose heart the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the heart of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the soundness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the heart is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sound heart: Abraham came with the sound heart, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the heart is the teaching that the soundness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the heart, and the teaching of the sincerity that the soundness requires.",
  "85": "“and said to his father and his people, “What are you worshipping?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse records the question: or \"ith qala li-abeehi wa qawmihi matha ta'budoon,\" and the commentators note the question, for Abraham asked the worship, and the question is the argument, the opening of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the argument, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the argument and the worship is the object, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reasoning. The believer is taught that the question of the false is the argument, and the one who asks is the one whose reasoning the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord accepts, and the question of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reasoning the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the question: Abraham asked and exposed, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reasoning that the argument requires.",
  "86": "“Is it false gods that you desire instead of Allah?”” continues the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the question: or \"a-ifkan alihatan doona Allahi tureedoon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Abraham exposed the false gods, and the continuation is the argument, the exposure of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exposure is the argument, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the argument and the exposure is the false, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the exposure of the false is the argument, and the one who exposes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham exposed the false, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation is the teaching that the false is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "87": "“What then do you expect from the Lord of all worlds?”” completes the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the question: or \"fa-ma zannukum bi-rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham asked the expectation, and the completion is the argument, the question of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the argument, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the question is the Lord, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the question of the Lord is the argument, and the one who asks is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham asked and exposed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the argument produces.",
  "88": "“He later looked up to the stars ˹in contemplation˺,” records the look, and the commentators note the two parts of the look. The verse records the look: or \"fa-nazara nazratan fi an-nujoom,\" and the commentators note the look, for Abraham looked at the stars, and the look is the plan, the beginning of the scheme, and the verse is the record of the look, the plan that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the look is the plan, and the verse is the record of the look, the plan of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the look is the plan and the stars are the pretext, and the verse is the record of the look, the plan that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the look, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the plan of the truth is the wisdom, and the one who plans is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the look of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the look is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the plan: Abraham looked and planned, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the look is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the look, and the teaching of the wisdom that the plan requires.",
  "89": "“then said, “I am really sick.”” records the claim, and the commentators note the two parts of the claim. The verse records the claim: or \"fa-qala innee saqeem,\" and the commentators note the claim, for Abraham claimed the sickness, and the claim is the plan, the pretext of the stay, and the verse is the record of the claim, the plan that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the claim is the plan, and the verse is the record of the claim, the plan of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the claim is the plan and the sickness is the pretext, and the verse is the record of the claim, the plan that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the claim, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the pretext of the plan is the wisdom, and the one who plans is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the claim of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the claim is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the plan: Abraham claimed and stayed, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the claim is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the claim, and the teaching of the wisdom that the plan requires.",
  "90": "“So they turned their backs on him and went away.”” records the departure, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the departure: or \"fa-tawallaw 'anhu mudbireen,\" and the commentators note the departure, for the people departed, and the departure is the providence, the occasion of the plan, and the verse is the record of the departure, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the departure is the occasion, and the verse is the record of the departure, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the departure is the occasion and the providence is the plan, and the verse is the record of the departure, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the departure, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the providence of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who watches is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the plan is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the departure of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the departure is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: the people departed and the plan was done, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the departure is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the departure, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "91": "“Then he ˹stealthily˺ advanced towards their gods, and said ˹mockingly˺, “Will you not eat ˹your offerings˺?”” records the advance, and the commentators note the two parts of the advance. The verse records the advance: or \"fa-ragha ila alihatihim fa-qala a-la ta'kuloon,\" and the commentators note the advance, for Abraham advanced to the idols, and the advance is the plan, the beginning of the destruction, and the verse is the record of the advance, the plan that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the advance is the plan, and the verse is the record of the advance, the plan of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the advance is the plan and the mockery is the argument, and the verse is the record of the advance, the plan that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the advance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the mockery of the idols is the argument, and the one who mocks is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who exposes the false is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the advance of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the advance is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the mockery: Abraham mocked the idols, and the believer who exposes the false is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the advance is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the advance, and the teaching of the wisdom that the mockery requires.",
  "92": "“What is wrong with you that you cannot speak?”” continues the mockery, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the mockery: or \"ma lakum la tantiqoon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Abraham mocked the silence, and the continuation is the argument, the exposure of the powerless, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the exposure is the argument, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the argument and the silence is the powerless, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the silence of the idols is the proof, and the one who exposes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham exposed the silence, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation is the teaching that the false is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "93": "“Then he swiftly turned on them, striking ˹them˺ with his right hand.”” records the striking, and the commentators note the two parts of the striking. The verse records the striking: or \"fa-ragha 'alayhim darban bil-yameen,\" and the commentators note the striking, for Abraham struck the idols, and the striking is the action, the destruction of the false, and the verse is the record of the striking, the action that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the striking is the action, and the verse is the record of the striking, the action of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the striking is the action and the destruction is the false, and the verse is the record of the striking, the action that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the striking, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own courage. The believer is taught that the destruction of the false is the courage, and the one who strikes is the one whose courage the Lord values, and the believer who acts against the false is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the striking of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the courage is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the striking is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the action: Abraham struck the idols, and the believer who acts against the false is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the striking is the teaching that the courage is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the striking, and the teaching of the courage that the action requires.",
  "94": "“Later, his people came rushing towards him ˹furiously˺.”” records the rush, and the commentators note the two parts of the rush. The verse records the rush: or \"fa-aqbaloo ilayhi yaziffoon,\" and the commentators note the rush, for the people rushed to Abraham, and the rush is the anger, the return of the people, and the verse is the record of the rush, the anger that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the rush is the anger, and the verse is the record of the rush, the anger of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rush is the anger and the return is the confrontation, and the verse is the record of the rush, the anger that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rush, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the anger of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who is rushed is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the anger is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the rush of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the anger is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rush is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the anger: Abraham was rushed and answered, and the believer who bears the anger is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the rush is the teaching that the anger is the thing that the falsehood holds. The verse is the record of the rush, and the teaching of the patience that the anger requires.",
  "95": "“He argued, “How can you worship what you carve ˹with your own hands˺,”” records the argument, and the commentators note the two parts of the argument. The verse records the argument: or \"qala a-ta'budoona ma tanhitoon,\" and the commentators note the argument, for Abraham exposed the carving, and the argument is the truth, the declaration of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the argument, the truth that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exposure is the argument, and the verse is the record of the argument, the truth of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the argument is the truth and the exposure is the carving, and the verse is the record of the argument, the truth that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the exposure of the false is the argument, and the one who exposes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the argument of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the argument is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham exposed the carving, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the argument is the teaching that the false is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the argument, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "96": "“when it is Allah Who created you and whatever you do?”” completes the argument, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the argument: or \"wallahu khalaqakum wa ma ta'maloon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham declared the creation, and the completion is the argument, the declaration of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the creation is the argument, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the creation is the proof, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the creation is the proof, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the creation: Abraham declared the creation, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "97": "“They said ˹to one another˺, “Build him a furnace and cast him into the blazing fire.”” records the plan, and the commentators note the two parts of the plan. The verse records the plan: or \"qaloo ubnoo lahu bunyanan fa-alqoohu fi al-jaheem,\" and the commentators note the plan, for the people planned the fire, and the plan is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the plan, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the plan is the violence, and the verse is the record of the plan, the violence of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plan is the violence and the fire is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the plan, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the violence of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the plan of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the plan is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the violence of the falsehood: the people planned the fire, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the plan is the teaching that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the plan, and the teaching of the trust that the violence requires.",
  "98": "“And so they sought to harm him, but We made them inferior.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two parts of the result. The verse records the result: or \"fa-aradoo bihi kaydan fa-ja'alnahumu al-asfaleen,\" and the commentators note the result, for the Lord made the people inferior, and the result is the judgment, the frustration of the scheme, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the frustration is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the result is the judgment and the inferiority is the frustration, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the frustration of the plot is the judgment, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the result of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the plot was frustrated, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the result is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "99": "“He later said, “I am leaving ˹in obedience˺ to my Lord. He will guide me.”” records the migration, and the commentators note the two parts of the migration. The verse records the migration: or \"wa qala innee thahibun ila rabbee sa-yahdeen,\" and the commentators note the migration, for Abraham declared the departure, and the migration is the faith, the trust of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the migration, the faith that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the migration is the faith, and the verse is the record of the migration, the faith of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the migration is the faith and the trust is the guidance, and the verse is the record of the migration, the faith that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the migration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the migration of the believer is the trust, and the one who departs is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the migration of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the migration is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the migration: Abraham departed and was guided, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the migration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the migration, and the teaching of the trust that the departure requires.",
  "100": "“My Lord! Bless me with righteous offspring.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The verse records the prayer: or \"rabbi hab lee mina as-saliheen,\" and the commentators note the prayer, for Abraham asked the righteous offspring, and the prayer is the hope, the seeking of the good, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the hope that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the hope, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the hope of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the hope and the offspring is the request, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the hope that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer for the righteous offspring is the hope, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the good is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the prayer of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the hope: Abraham prayed and was answered, and the believer who hopes the good is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the prayer is the teaching that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "101": "“So We gave him good news of a forbearing son.”” records the news, and the commentators note the two parts of the news. The verse records the news: or \"fa-bashsharnahu bi-ghulamin haleem,\" and the commentators note the news, for the Lord announced the son, and the news is the answer, the grant of the offspring, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the forbearing son is the gift, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the news is the answer and the forbearing is the son, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the news, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the answer of the prayer is the grant, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the good is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the news of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the news is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Abraham was given the son, and the believer who prays is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the news is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the news, and the teaching of the hope that the answer carries.",
  "102": "“Then when the boy reached the age to work with him, Abraham said, “O my dear son! I have seen in a dream that I ˹must˺ sacrifice you. So tell me what you think.” He replied, “O my dear father! Do as you are commanded. Allah willing, you will find me steadfast.”” records the trial, and the commentators note the two parts of the trial. The verse records the trial: or \"fa-lamma balagha ma'ahu as-sa'ya qala ya bunayya innee ara fi al-manami annee athbahuka fanzur matha tara qala ya abati if'al ma tu'maru satajidunee in sha'a Allahu mina as-sabireen,\" and the commentators note the trial, for Abraham told the son the dream, and the trial is the test, the sacrifice of the son, and the verse is the record of the trial, the test that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the submission is the test, and the verse is the record of the trial, the test of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the trial is the test and the submission is the dream, and the verse is the record of the trial, the test that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the trial, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the submission of the son is the model, and the one who submits is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the trial of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the trial is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the sacrifice: the son submitted and the father was tested, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the trial is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the trial, and the teaching of the trust that the submission requires.",
  "103": "“Then when they submitted ˹to Allah’s Will˺, and Abraham laid him on the side of his forehead ˹for sacrifice˺,” records the submission, and the commentators note the two parts of the submission. The verse records the submission: or \"fa-lamma aslama wa tallahu lil-jabeen,\" and the commentators note the submission, for the two submitted and the father laid the son, and the submission is the test, the peak of the sacrifice, and the verse is the record of the submission, the test that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the submission is the test, and the verse is the record of the submission, the test of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the submission is the test and the laying is the sacrifice, and the verse is the record of the submission, the test that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the submission, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the submission of the self is the peak of the trust, and the one who submits is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the submission of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the submission is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peak of the trust: the two submitted and were ransomed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the submission is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the submission, and the teaching of the trust that the sacrifice requires.",
  "104": "“We called out to him, “O Abraham!”” records the call, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"wa nadaynahu an ya Ibraheem,\" and the commentators note the call, for the Lord called Abraham, and the call is the mercy, the interruption of the sacrifice, and the verse is the record of the call, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the call, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the mercy and the interruption is the ransom, and the verse is the record of the call, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the call of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is called is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the call to Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: Abraham was called and ransomed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the call is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the trust that the mercy produces.",
  "105": "“You have already fulfilled the vision.”” records the fulfillment, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the fulfillment: or \"qad saddaqta ar-ru'ya,\" and the commentators note the fulfillment, for the Lord declared the vision fulfilled, and the fulfillment is the acceptance, the declaration of the completion, and the verse is the record of the fulfillment, the acceptance that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the fulfillment is the acceptance, and the verse is the record of the fulfillment, the acceptance of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fulfillment is the acceptance and the vision is the completion, and the verse is the record of the fulfillment, the acceptance that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fulfillment, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the fulfillment of the vision is the acceptance, and the one who submits is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the fulfillment of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the fulfillment is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the acceptance of the Lord: Abraham fulfilled and was accepted, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the fulfillment is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the fulfillment, and the teaching of the trust that the acceptance produces.",
  "106": "“Indeed, that was truly a revealing test.”” records the test, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the test: or \"inna hatha la-huwa al-bala'u al-mubeen,\" and the commentators note the test, for the Lord declared the trial, and the test is the teaching, the declaration of the great trial, and the verse is the record of the test, the teaching that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the trial is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the test, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the test is the teaching and the trial is the revealing, and the verse is the record of the test, the teaching that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the test, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the trial of the Lord is the test, and the one who is tested is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the test of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the test is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the Lord: Abraham was tested and held, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the test is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the test, and the teaching of the trust that the trial requires.",
  "107": "“And We ransomed his son with a great sacrifice,” records the ransom, and the commentators note the two parts of the ransom. The verse records the ransom: or \"wa fadaynahu bi-thibhin 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the ransom, for the Lord ransomed the son, and the ransom is the mercy, the grant of the substitute, and the verse is the record of the ransom, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the ransom is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the ransom, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ransom is the mercy and the substitute is the grant, and the verse is the record of the ransom, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ransom, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the ransom of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who trusts is the one whose mercy the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the ransom of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the ransom is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: the son was ransomed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the ransom is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the ransom, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "108": "“and blessed Abraham ˹with honourable mention˺ among later generations:” records the mention, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mention: or \"wa tarakna 'alayhi fi al-akhireen,\" and the commentators note the mention, for the Lord blessed Abraham with the mention, and the mention is the reward, the grant of the good name, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention is the reward, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mention is the reward and the name is the grant, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mention, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the good name is the reward, and the one who is mentioned is the one whose name the Lord raises, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord grants, and the mention of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the good name: Abraham was mentioned, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord raises, and the mention is the teaching that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the mention, and the teaching of the hope that the name carries.",
  "109": "““Peace be upon Abraham.”” records the peace, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the peace: or \"salamun 'ala Ibraheem,\" and the commentators note the peace, for the Lord granted Abraham the peace, and the peace is the reward, the grant of the safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace is the reward, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace is the reward and the safety is the grant, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose peace the Lord gives, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the peace is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the Lord: Abraham was granted the peace, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace is the teaching that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the peace, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "110": "“This is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the principle, and the commentators note the two parts of the principle. The verse records the principle: or \"kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,\" and the commentators note the principle, for the Lord declared the reward of the good-doers, and the principle is the teaching, the declaration of the recompense, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reward is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the principle is the teaching and the reward is the good, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the principle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the reward of the good-doers is the promise, and the one who does the good is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the principle of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the principle is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the good: the good-doers are rewarded, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the principle is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the principle, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "111": "“He was truly one of Our faithful servants.”” records the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the description: or \"innahu min 'ibadina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the description, for the Lord described Abraham, and the description is the praise, the declaration of the faith, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the faith is the praise, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the praise and the faith is the servant, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the praise, and the one who is faithful is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the description of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the servant: Abraham was the faithful, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the faith that the servant requires.",
  "112": "“We ˹later˺ gave him good news of Isaac—a prophet, and one of the righteous.”” records the news, and the commentators note the two parts of the news. The verse records the news: or \"wa bashsharnahu bi-Ishaqa nabiyyan mina as-saliheen,\" and the commentators note the news, for the Lord announced Isaac, and the news is the answer, the grant of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the news is the answer, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the news is the answer and the grant is the prophet, and the verse is the record of the news, the answer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the news, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the answer of the prayer is the grant, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the good is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the news of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the news is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Abraham was given Isaac, and the believer who prays is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the news is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the news, and the teaching of the hope that the answer carries.",
  "113": "“We blessed him and Isaac as well. Some of their descendants did good, while others clearly wronged themselves.”” records the blessing, and the commentators note the two parts of the blessing. The verse records the blessing: or \"wa barakna 'alayhi wa 'ala Ishaqa wa min thurriyyatihima muhsinun wa zalimun li-nafsihi mubeen,\" and the commentators note the blessing, for the Lord blessed the two, and the blessing is the grant, the description of the descendants, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two kinds are the description, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the blessing is the grant and the two kinds are the descendants, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blessing, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the blessing of the Lord is the grant, and the one who is blessed is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose hope the Lord values, and the blessing of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the blessing is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the grant of the Lord: the two were blessed, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the blessing is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the blessing, and the teaching of the hope that the grant carries.",
  "114": "“And We certainly showed favour to Moses and Aaron,” records the favour, and the commentators note the two parts of the favour. The verse records the favour: or \"wa laqad mananna 'ala Moosa wa Haroon,\" and the commentators note the favour, for the Lord showed favour to Moses and Aaron, and the favour is the grant, the mercy of the two, and the verse is the record of the favour, the grant that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the favour is the grant, and the verse is the record of the favour, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the favour is the grant and the mercy is the two, and the verse is the record of the favour, the grant that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the favour, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the favour of the Lord is the grant, and the one who is favoured is the one whose mercy the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the favour of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the favour is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the grant of the Lord: the two were favoured, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the favour is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the favour, and the teaching of the hope that the grant carries.",
  "115": "“and delivered them and their people from the great distress.”” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the deliverance. The verse records the deliverance: or \"wa najjaynahuma wa qawmahuma mina al-karbi al-'azeem,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved the two and the people, and the deliverance is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the rescue is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: the two were delivered, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "116": "“We helped them so it was they who prevailed.”” records the victory, and the commentators note the two parts of the victory. The verse records the victory: or \"wa nasarnahum fa-kanoo humu al-ghalibeen,\" and the commentators note the victory, for the Lord helped the two, and the victory is the support, the grant of the triumph, and the verse is the record of the victory, the support that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the victory is the support, and the verse is the record of the victory, the support of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the victory is the support and the triumph is the grant, and the verse is the record of the victory, the support that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the victory, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the victory of the Lord is the support, and the one who trusts is the one whose victory the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the victory of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the support is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the victory is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the support of the Lord: the two prevailed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose victory the Lord grants, and the victory is the teaching that the support is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the victory, and the teaching of the trust that the support produces.",
  "117": "“We gave them the clear Scripture,” records the Scripture, and the commentators note the two parts of the Scripture. The verse records the Scripture: or \"wa ataynahuma al-kitaba al-mustabeen,\" and the commentators note the Scripture, for the Lord gave the two the Book, and the Scripture is the gift, the grant of the guide, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the Book is the gift, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Scripture is the gift and the guide is the grant, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the Scripture, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own guidance. The believer is taught that the Scripture of the Lord is the guide, and the one who is given is the one whose guidance the Lord values, and the believer who follows the Book is the believer whose guidance the Lord accepts, and the Scripture of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the guide is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the Scripture is the believer whose guidance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the Scripture: the two were given the Book, and the believer who follows the guide is the believer whose guidance the Lord values, and the Scripture is the teaching that the guide is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the Scripture, and the teaching of the guidance that the Book grants.",
  "118": "“and guided them to the Straight Path.”” records the guidance, and the commentators note the two parts of the guidance. The verse records the guidance: or \"wa hadaynahuma as-sirata al-mustaqeem,\" and the commentators note the guidance, for the Lord guided the two, and the guidance is the gift, the grant of the path, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the path is the gift, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the guidance is the gift and the path is the grant, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the guidance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the guidance of the Lord is the gift, and the one who is guided is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the path is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the guidance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the path is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the guidance is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the guidance: the two were guided, and the believer who holds the path is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the guidance is the teaching that the path is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the guidance, and the teaching of the faith that the guidance produces.",
  "119": "“And We blessed them ˹with honourable mention˺ among later generations:” records the mention, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mention: or \"wa tarakna 'alayhima fi al-akhireen,\" and the commentators note the mention, for the Lord blessed the two with the mention, and the mention is the reward, the grant of the good name, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention is the reward, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mention is the reward and the name is the grant, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mention, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the good name is the reward, and the one who is mentioned is the one whose name the Lord raises, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord grants, and the mention of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the good name: the two were mentioned, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord raises, and the mention is the teaching that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the mention, and the teaching of the hope that the name carries.",
  "120": "““Peace be upon Moses and Aaron.”” records the peace, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the peace: or \"salamun 'ala Moosa wa Haroon,\" and the commentators note the peace, for the Lord granted the two the peace, and the peace is the reward, the grant of the safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace is the reward, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace is the reward and the safety is the grant, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose peace the Lord gives, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the peace is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the Lord: the two were granted the peace, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace is the teaching that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the peace, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "121": "“Indeed, this is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the principle, and the commentators note the two parts of the principle. The verse records the principle: or \"inna kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,\" and the commentators note the principle, for the Lord declared the reward of the good-doers, and the principle is the teaching, the declaration of the recompense, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reward is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the principle is the teaching and the reward is the good, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the principle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the reward of the good-doers is the promise, and the one who does the good is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the principle of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the principle is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the good: the good-doers are rewarded, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the principle is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the principle, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "122": "“They were truly ˹two˺ of Our faithful servants.”” records the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the description: or \"innahuma min 'ibadina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the description, for the Lord described the two, and the description is the praise, the declaration of the faith, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the faith is the praise, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise of the two. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the praise and the faith is the servant, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the praise, and the one who is faithful is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the description of the two is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the servant: the two were the faithful, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the faith that the servant requires.",
  "123": "“And Elias was indeed one of the messengers.”” records Elias, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Elias: or \"wa inna Ilyasa la-mina al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note Elias, for the Lord declared Elias of the messengers, and the verse is the record of Elias, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Elias, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Elias, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the messenger is the station, and the one who is sent is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the messenger: Elias was the sent, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Elias, and the teaching of the faith that the messenger requires.",
  "124": "“˹Remember˺ when he said to his people, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the call. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala li-qawmihi a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Elias called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Elias. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Elias is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Elias called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "125": "“Do you call upon ˹the idol of˺ Ba’l and abandon the Best of Creators—”” records the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the rebuke. The verse records the rebuke: or \"a-tad'oona Ba'lan wa tatharoona ahsana al-khaliqeen,\" and the commentators note the rebuke, for Elias rebuked the idol, and the rebuke is the correction, the exposure of the false, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exposure is the correction, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction of Elias. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rebuke is the correction and the exposure is the idol, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the exposure of the false is the correction, and the one who rebukes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the rebuke of Elias is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the correction of the false: Elias rebuked the idol, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the rebuke is the teaching that the false is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the rebuke, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "126": "“Allah, your Lord and the Lord of your forefathers.”” completes the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the rebuke: or \"Allaha rabbakum wa rabba aba'ikumu al-awwaleen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Elias declared the Lord, and the completion is the correction, the declaration of the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction of Elias. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the correction and the declaration is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the declaration of the truth is the correction, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of Elias is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the declaration of the truth: Elias declared the Lord, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the declaration produces.",
  "127": "“But they rejected him, so they will certainly be brought ˹for punishment˺.”” records the rejection, and the commentators note the two parts of the rejection. The verse records the rejection: or \"fa-kaththaboohu fa-innahum la-muhdaroon,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people rejected Elias, and the rejection is the denial, the end of the people, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the denial, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the bringing is the end, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the denial, and the one who rejects is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the rejection: the people rejected and were brought, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "128": "“But not the chosen servants of Allah.”” records the exception, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the exception: or \"illa 'ibada Allahi al-mukhlaseen,\" and the commentators note the exception, for the Lord excepted the chosen servants, and the exception is the mercy, the salvation of the sincere, and the verse is the record of the exception, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the exception, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exception is the mercy and the chosen is the sincere, and the verse is the record of the exception, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exception, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the exception of the chosen is the mercy, and the one who is sincere is the one whose salvation the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the sincerity is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the exception of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the exception is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the chosen: the sincere are saved, and the believer who holds the sincerity is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the exception is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the exception, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "129": "“We blessed him ˹with honourable mention˺ among later generations:” records the mention, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mention: or \"wa tarakna 'alayhi fi al-akhireen,\" and the commentators note the mention, for the Lord blessed Elias with the mention, and the mention is the reward, the grant of the good name, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention is the reward, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mention is the reward and the name is the grant, and the verse is the record of the mention, the reward that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mention, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the good name is the reward, and the one who is mentioned is the one whose name the Lord raises, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord grants, and the mention of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the good name: Elias was mentioned, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord raises, and the mention is the teaching that the good name is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the mention, and the teaching of the hope that the name carries.",
  "130": "““Peace be upon Elias.”” records the peace, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the peace: or \"salamun 'ala Ilyaseen,\" and the commentators note the peace, for the Lord granted Elias the peace, and the peace is the reward, the grant of the safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace is the reward, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace is the reward and the safety is the grant, and the verse is the record of the peace, the reward that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose peace the Lord gives, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the peace is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the Lord: Elias was granted the peace, and the believer who hopes the peace is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace is the teaching that the peace is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the peace, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "131": "“Indeed, this is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the principle, and the commentators note the two parts of the principle. The verse records the principle: or \"inna kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,\" and the commentators note the principle, for the Lord declared the reward of the good-doers, and the principle is the teaching, the declaration of the recompense, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reward is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the principle is the teaching and the reward is the good, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the principle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the reward of the good-doers is the promise, and the one who does the good is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the principle of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the principle is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the good: the good-doers are rewarded, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the principle is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the principle, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "132": "“He was truly one of Our faithful servants.”” records the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the description: or \"innahu min 'ibadina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the description, for the Lord described Elias, and the description is the praise, the declaration of the faith, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the faith is the praise, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise of Elias. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the praise and the faith is the servant, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the praise, and the one who is faithful is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the description of Elias is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the servant: Elias was the faithful, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the faith that the servant requires.",
  "133": "“And Lot was indeed one of the messengers.”” records Lot, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Lot: or \"wa inna Lootan la-mina al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note Lot, for the Lord declared Lot of the messengers, and the verse is the record of Lot, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Lot, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Lot, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the messenger is the station, and the one who is sent is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the messenger: Lot was the sent, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Lot, and the teaching of the faith that the messenger requires.",
  "134": "“˹Remember˺ when We delivered him and all of his family,” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the deliverance. The verse records the deliverance: or \"ith najjaynahu wa ahlahu ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved Lot and the family, and the deliverance is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the rescue is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: Lot was delivered, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "135": "“except an old woman, who was one of the doomed.”” qualifies the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the qualification. The verse qualifies the deliverance: or \"illa 'ajoozan fi al-ghabireen,\" and the commentators note the qualification, for the wife was excepted, and the qualification is the teaching, the declaration that the kinship does not save, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the qualification is the teaching and the exception is the faith, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the qualification, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the kinship does not save, and the one who is excepted is the one whose faith the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the qualification of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the condition of the salvation, and the believer who reads the qualification is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the condition of the salvation: the wife was excepted, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the qualification is the teaching that the faith is the condition. The verse is the record of the qualification, and the teaching of the faith that the salvation requires.",
  "136": "“Then We ˹utterly˺ destroyed the rest.”” records the destruction, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the destruction: or \"thumma dammarna al-akhareen,\" and the commentators note the destruction, for the Lord destroyed the rest, and the destruction is the judgment, the end of the deniers, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the judgment that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the destruction is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the destruction is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the judgment that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destruction, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the destruction of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the destruction is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the deniers were destroyed and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the destruction is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the destruction, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "137": "“You ˹Meccans˺ certainly pass by their ruins day”” records the ruins, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the ruins: or \"wa innakum la-tamurroona 'alayhim musbiheen,\" and the commentators note the ruins, for the Lord reminded the passers, and the ruins are the witness, the lesson of the destruction, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the ruins are the witness, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness of the destruction. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruins are the witness and the passing is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ruins, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the ruins of the destroyed are the witness, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the ruins of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the ruins is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the witness of the ruins: the ruins are passed, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the ruins are the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the ruins, and the teaching of the reflection that the witness invites.",
  "138": "“and night. Will you not then understand?”” completes the ruins, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the ruins: or \"wa bil-layli afala ta'qiloon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord asked the understanding, and the completion is the teaching, the appeal of the reflection, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the appeal is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the teaching and the appeal is the reflection, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the reflection upon the ruins is the duty, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the ruins: the appeal is the reflection, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reflection that the ruins invite.",
  "139": "“And Jonah was indeed one of the messengers.”” records Jonah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Jonah: or \"wa inna Yoonusa la-mina al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note Jonah, for the Lord declared Jonah of the messengers, and the verse is the record of Jonah, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Jonah, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the messenger is the station, and the verse is the record of Jonah, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the messenger is the station, and the one who is sent is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the messenger: Jonah was the sent, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Jonah, and the teaching of the faith that the messenger requires.",
  "140": "“˹Remember˺ when he fled to the overloaded ship.”” records the flight, and the commentators note the two parts of the flight. The verse records the flight: or \"ith abaqa ila al-fulki al-mashhoon,\" and the commentators note the flight, for Jonah fled to the ship, and the flight is the slip, the departure of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the flight, the slip that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the flight is the slip, and the verse is the record of the flight, the slip of Jonah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the flight is the slip and the ship is the overloaded, and the verse is the record of the flight, the slip that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the flight, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the slip of the prophet is the human, and the one who slips is the one whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord values, and the flight of Jonah is the teaching, the declaration that the slip is the thing that the repentance repairs, and the believer who reads the flight is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the slip: Jonah fled and repented, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the flight is the teaching that the slip is the thing that the repentance repairs. The verse is the record of the flight, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "141": "“Then ˹to save it from sinking,˺ he drew straws ˹with other passengers˺. He lost ˹and was thrown overboard˺.”” records the draw, and the commentators note the two parts of the draw. The verse records the draw: or \"fa-sahama fa-kana mina al-mudhadeen,\" and the commentators note the draw, for Jonah drew and lost, and the draw is the trial, the casting of the lot, and the verse is the record of the draw, the trial that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the draw is the trial, and the verse is the record of the draw, the trial of Jonah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the draw is the trial and the loss is the lot, and the verse is the record of the draw, the trial that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the draw, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the trial of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who is tested is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the decree is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the draw of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the draw is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the decree: Jonah drew and lost, and the believer who trusts the decree is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the draw is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the draw, and the teaching of the trust that the trial requires.",
  "142": "“Then the whale engulfed him while he was blameworthy.”” records the whale, and the commentators note the two parts of the whale. The verse records the whale: or \"faltaqamahu al-hootu wa huwa muleem,\" and the commentators note the whale, for the whale engulfed Jonah, and the whale is the trial, the darkness of the belly, and the verse is the record of the whale, the trial that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the whale is the trial, and the verse is the record of the whale, the trial of Jonah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the whale is the trial and the blame is the slip, and the verse is the record of the whale, the trial that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the whale, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the trial of the Lord is the thing that the believer bears, and the one who is tried is the one whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord values, and the whale of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the trial is the thing that the repentance answers, and the believer who reads the whale is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trial of the Lord: Jonah was engulfed and prayed, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the whale is the teaching that the trial is the thing that the repentance answers. The verse is the record of the whale, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "143": "“Had he not ˹constantly˺ glorified ˹Allah˺,” records the glorification, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the glorification: or \"fa-law la annahu kana mina al-musabbiheen,\" and the commentators note the glorification, for Jonah was of the glorifiers, and the glorification is the shield, the cause of the rescue, and the verse is the record of the glorification, the shield that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the glorification is the shield, and the verse is the record of the glorification, the shield of Jonah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the glorification is the shield and the rescue is the cause, and the verse is the record of the glorification, the shield that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the glorification, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own remembrance. The believer is taught that the glorification of the Lord is the shield, and the one who glorifies is the one whose rescue the Lord grants, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose remembrance the Lord values, and the glorification of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the glorification is the believer whose remembrance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the shield of the remembrance: Jonah glorified and was rescued, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose rescue the Lord grants, and the glorification is the teaching that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the glorification, and the teaching of the remembrance that the rescue requires.",
  "144": "“he would have certainly remained in its belly until the Day of Resurrection.”” completes the glorification, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the glorification: or \"la-labitha fee batnihi ila yawmi yub'athoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord declared the stay, and the completion is the teaching, the declaration of the rescue, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the stay is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the teaching and the stay is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own remembrance. The believer is taught that the remembrance of the Lord is the rescue, and the one who forgets is the one whose stay the Lord declares, and the believer who glorifies is the believer whose rescue the Lord grants, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose remembrance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the rescue of the remembrance: the glorification saved Jonah, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose rescue the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the remembrance that the rescue requires.",
  "145": "“But We cast him onto the open ˹shore˺, ˹totally˺ worn out,” records the casting, and the commentators note the two parts of the casting. The verse records the casting: or \"fa-nabathnahu bil-'ara'i wa huwa saqeem,\" and the commentators note the casting, for the Lord cast Jonah onto the shore, and the casting is the mercy, the rescue of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the casting, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the casting is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the casting, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the casting is the mercy and the shore is the rescue, and the verse is the record of the casting, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the casting, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the rescue of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is cast is the one whose rescue the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the casting of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the casting is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the rescue: Jonah was cast and saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the casting is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the casting, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "146": "“and caused a squash plant to grow over him.”” records the plant, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the plant: or \"wa anbatna 'alayhi shajaratan min yaqteen,\" and the commentators note the plant, for the Lord grew the squash, and the plant is the mercy, the provision of the shade, and the verse is the record of the plant, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the plant is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the plant, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plant is the mercy and the shade is the provision, and the verse is the record of the plant, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plant, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the provision of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is provided is the one whose mercy the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the plant of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the plant is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the provision: the plant was grown, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the plant is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the plant, and the teaching of the hope that the provision carries.",
  "147": "“We ˹later˺ sent him ˹back˺ to ˹his city of˺ at least one hundred thousand people,” records the return, and the commentators note the two parts of the return. The verse records the return: or \"wa arsalnahu ila mi'ati alfin aw yazeedoon,\" and the commentators note the return, for the Lord sent Jonah back, and the return is the mercy, the second mission, and the verse is the record of the return, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the return is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the return, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the return is the mercy and the second is the mission, and the verse is the record of the return, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the return, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who returns is the one whose mercy the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the return of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the return is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: Jonah was returned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the return is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "148": "“who then believed ˹in him˺, so We allowed them enjoyment for a while.”” records the belief, and the commentators note the two parts of the belief. The verse records the belief: or \"fa-amanoo fa-matta'nahum ila heen,\" and the commentators note the belief, for the people believed, and the belief is the mercy, the salvation of the people, and the verse is the record of the belief, the mercy that the account closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the belief is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the belief, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the belief is the mercy and the salvation is the people, and the verse is the record of the belief, the mercy that the account closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the belief, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the belief of the people is the mercy, and the one who believes is the one whose salvation the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the belief of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the belief is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: the people believed and were saved, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the belief is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the belief, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "149": "“Ask them, O Prophet, if your Lord has daughters, while the pagans prefer to have sons.” This verse turns from the prophetic narratives to a direct challenge of the pagans’ beliefs about God. The Prophet is told to ask them a searching question about their attribution of daughters to God. The pagans claimed that the angels were daughters of God, while they themselves, for their own families, preferred sons. The question exposes the inconsistency and the insult of their claim.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the pagans’ attitude toward daughters. In the society of the time, sons were preferred over daughters. The birth of a son was a cause for celebration, while the birth of a daughter was often a cause for disappointment. The pagans, holding this preference, attributed to God what they themselves considered inferior: daughters. The attribution was both an insult to God, assigning to Him what they deemed lesser, and a revelation of their own devaluation of daughters.\n\nThe inconsistency is the heart of the exposure. If the pagans preferred sons for themselves, why did they attribute daughters to God? If sons were better, as their preference implied, then attributing daughters to God was to give God the lesser portion. The inconsistency reveals that their theology was shaped not by reverence but by convenience: they assigned to God what they did not want for themselves, treating the divine as a dumping ground for what they considered inferior.\n\nThe form of a question, “if your Lord has daughters,” invites the pagans to consider the absurdity of their claim. The question is not seeking information but exposing error. By framing the issue as a question, the verse leads the pagans to confront the inconsistency of their position, to recognize that their attribution of daughters to God, combined with their preference for sons, revealed a theology that dishonored God rather than revering Him.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sharp exposure of the inconsistency in pagan theology and a reminder of the importance of attributing to God only what is worthy of Him. It invites us to recognize that our beliefs about God should be shaped by reverence, not by convenience or cultural prejudice, and that attributing to God what we consider inferior is an insult to His greatness. The verse also challenges the devaluation of daughters that the pagans practiced, affirming, by implication, that what God has created, whether sons or daughters, is worthy of honor, and that our theology and our practice should reflect the dignity that God has bestowed on all human beings, male and female, whom He has made.",
  "150": "“Or ask them if We created the angels as females right before their eyes.” This verse adds another layer to the challenge of the pagans’ beliefs. The Prophet is told to ask whether the angels, whom the pagans called the daughters of God, were created as females before their very eyes. The question exposes the groundlessness of the pagans’ claim. They had no knowledge of the angels’ nature, no firsthand observation of their creation, yet they confidently asserted that the angels were female and that they were God’s daughters.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the pagans’ lack of evidence. The angels are unseen beings, part of the realm that lies beyond human observation. The pagans had never seen an angel, had never witnessed the creation of an angel, had no way of knowing whether angels were male, female, or beyond such categories. Yet they made confident assertions about the angels’ nature and their relationship to God. The question exposes the audacity of claiming knowledge about things that lie entirely beyond human experience.\n\nThe phrase “right before their eyes” is pointed. It asks whether the pagans had actually witnessed the creation of the angels as females. The answer, of course, is no. No human being has witnessed the creation of the angels. The pagans’ claim about the angels was therefore pure invention, an assertion made without any basis in observation or revelation. The question strips away the pretense of knowledge and reveals the claim for what it was: a baseless fabrication.\n\nThe exposure of the groundlessness is part of a broader argument against the pagans’ theology. Their beliefs about God, the angels, and the relationships among them were not based on revelation or observation but on invention and convention. They made up their theology as they went along, attributing to God and to the unseen realm whatever suited their preferences and their imaginations. The verse challenges the entire foundation of their religious thought by showing that it rested on no evidence.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sharp exposure of the groundlessness of invented theology and a call to ground our beliefs about God and the unseen in revelation rather than imagination. It invites us to recognize that claims about the unseen must be based on what God has revealed, not on what human beings invent, and that confident assertions about things beyond our experience are a form of arrogance. The verse challenges us to examine the basis of our own beliefs, to ensure that they rest on revealed truth rather than on convention or convenience, and to approach the unseen with the humility that befits creatures whose knowledge of it depends entirely on what the Creator has chosen to reveal.",
  "151": "“Indeed, it is one of their outrageous fabrications to say.” This verse identifies the pagans’ claims about God as fabrications. The Arabic word carries the sense of a lie, an invented falsehood, a made-up story. The claims that the pagans made about God having children, specifically daughters, were not based on truth but on invention. The verse labels these claims as “outrageous,” emphasizing their severity and their offensiveness.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its directness. The Quran does not engage the pagans’ claims as if they were legitimate theological positions to be debated. It identifies them as fabrications, invented falsehoods, and moves on. The directness reflects the clarity of the truth: God has no children, and any claim that He does is a fabrication, regardless of who makes it or how sincerely. The identification removes the claims from the realm of legitimate discussion and places them where they belong, in the category of error.\n\nThe word “outrageous” underscores the severity of the fabrication. The claims were not minor errors or understandable misunderstandings but outrageous inventions, serious offenses against the truth about God. The severity reflects the seriousness of the subject: claims about God’s nature are not trivial, and false claims about Him are serious offenses, because they misrepresent the One who is the source of all truth and the object of all worship.\n\nThe phrase “one of their fabrications” suggests that the claim about God’s children was not the only falsehood the pagans had invented. It was one among many. The pagans had constructed an entire theology of fabrication, a system of false beliefs about God, the angels, and the unseen realm. The verse identifies the claim about children as a representative example of this broader system of invented religion.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear identification of false claims about God as fabrications and a call to ensure that our beliefs about Him are true. It invites us to recognize that claims about God must be based on truth, not invention, and that fabricating things about God is a serious offense against His nature and His dignity. The verse challenges us to examine our own beliefs about God, to reject any that are based on invention rather than revelation, and to speak about God only what is true, knowing that the One about whom we speak is the source of all truth and that to misrepresent Him, whether through carelessness or through deliberate fabrication, is an offense that the verse identifies as outrageous.",
  "152": "“Allah has children. They are simply liars.” This verse records the specific fabrication that the pagans uttered: that Allah has children. The claim is stated plainly, and then the verdict is given: they are simply liars. The directness is striking. The Quran states the false claim and immediately pronounces the verdict, without hesitation or qualification. The claim is a lie, and those who make it are liars.\n\nThe significance of the claim lies in its fundamental misrepresentation of God’s nature. God is one, unique, the Creator of all. He does not beget, nor is He begotten. The claim that He has children, whether sons or daughters, compromises His uniqueness, introduces multiplicity into the divine, and misrepresents the relationship between the Creator and the creation. The claim is not a minor error but a fundamental distortion of the truth about who God is.\n\nThe verdict, “they are simply liars,” is uncompromising. Those who claim that God has children are liars. The verdict does not allow for the claim to be treated as a sincere mistake or a legitimate difference of opinion. It is a lie, and those who propagate it are liars. The severity of the verdict reflects the severity of the claim: to misrepresent God so fundamentally is not a matter of honest disagreement but of falsehood that must be named as falsehood.\n\nThe word “simply” is emphatic. They are simply, plainly, nothing other than liars. The word strips away any pretense of legitimacy that the claim might have. Whatever the pagans’ motivations, whatever their sincerity, the claim itself is a lie, and those who make it are, in relation to this claim, liars. The directness leaves no room for equivocation.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the previous one is clear. The previous verse identified the pagans’ claims as outrageous fabrications. This verse names the specific fabrication, that Allah has children, and pronounces the verdict. The two verses together expose the claim, identify it as a fabrication, and declare those who make it to be liars, providing a complete refutation in the space of a few words.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear, uncompromising statement of the truth about God’s nature and a warning against the claim that He has children. It invites us to recognize that God is one, unique, without offspring, and that any claim to the contrary is a lie that must be rejected. The verse challenges us to hold fast to the truth of God’s oneness and uniqueness, to refuse the fabrications that misrepresent Him, and to speak about God only what is true, knowing that the One who has no children is the One who created all, and that to attribute offspring to Him is to lie about the very nature of the One whom we are called to worship in spirit and in truth.",
  "153": "“Has He chosen daughters over sons?” This verse poses another searching question to the pagans. It asks whether God has chosen daughters over sons. The question exposes the absurdity of the pagan claim from their own perspective. The pagans preferred sons and devalued daughters. Yet they claimed that God had chosen daughters, the very thing they considered inferior, for Himself. The question highlights the contradiction.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the pagans’ inconsistency. If the pagans believed that sons were better, as their preference demonstrated, then to claim that God had chosen daughters was to attribute to God what they themselves considered the lesser choice. The question asks, in effect: would God, who is the highest, choose what you consider the lowest? The inconsistency reveals that the pagan claim was not based on reverence for God but on convenience: they attributed to God what they did not want for themselves.\n\nThe form of the question is pedagogically powerful. Rather than simply stating that the claim is absurd, the verse asks the pagans to consider their own logic. The question leads them to recognize, if they are honest, that their claim does not make sense even on their own terms. The pedagogy respects the listeners’ intelligence while exposing the error of their position, inviting them to discover the contradiction through their own reflection.\n\nThe question also implies a correction of the pagans’ devaluation of daughters. By highlighting the absurdity of attributing to God what the pagans considered inferior, the verse implicitly challenges the devaluation itself. The problem is not that daughters are unworthy of God but that the pagans’ whole framework of valuing sons over daughters is flawed, and their theology is distorted by this flaw. The verse points toward a right understanding in which both sons and daughters are valued as God’s creation and in which God is not subject to the pagans’ distorted preferences.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sharp exposure of the inconsistency in pagan theology and a call to examine the logic of our beliefs about God. It invites us to recognize that our beliefs about God should be consistent and reverent, not shaped by cultural prejudices or conveniences, and that attributing to God what we consider inferior reveals a theology that dishonors Him. The verse challenges us to examine the logic of our own beliefs, to ensure that they reflect the true greatness of God rather than our own preferences, and to value all of God’s creation, whether sons or daughters, with the dignity that He has bestowed on it, refusing the distorted valuations that lead, as they led the pagans, to a theology that misrepresents the very God it claims to honor.",
  "154": "“What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” This verse poses two searching questions to the pagans. The first, “what is the matter with you,” expresses astonishment at their condition. The second, “how do you judge,” challenges the basis of their judgment. Together, the questions expose the unreasonableness of the pagans’ position and challenge them to examine the foundations of their thinking.\n\nThe significance of the first question lies in its expression of astonishment. “What is the matter with you” marvels at the pagans’ condition, at their ability to hold and propagate beliefs so unreasonable. The question implies that something has gone wrong with their thinking, that their judgment has been impaired, that they have departed from the path of sound reasoning. The astonishment is itself a form of rebuke, highlighting the gap between the pagans’ beliefs and the reasonableness that should characterize human thought about God.\n\nThe second question, “how do you judge,” challenges the basis of the pagans’ judgment. The question asks, in effect, what standard or process they use to reach their conclusions about God. The implication is that their judgment is flawed, that whatever process they use to arrive at their beliefs about God’s children is not a sound process. The question invites them to examine their own reasoning, to recognize its defects, and to reform their judgment in accordance with the truth.\n\nThe combination of the two questions is powerful. The first expresses astonishment at the condition; the second challenges the process that produced it. Together, they expose both the result, the unreasonable beliefs, and the cause, the flawed judgment. The exposure is complete, addressing both the symptom and the disease, and inviting the pagans to a thorough reconsideration of their thinking about God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a searching challenge to examine the soundness of our own judgment about God and a call to reason carefully about the most important truths. It invites us to recognize that sound judgment about God is essential, that flawed judgment leads to false beliefs, and that the questions posed to the pagans are questions we should pose to ourselves. The verse challenges us to examine how we judge, to ensure that our reasoning about God is sound and reverent, and to reform any thinking that, like the pagans’, has departed from reasonableness, knowing that the truth about God is reasonable and that those who seek it with sound judgment will find it, while those whose judgment is impaired will persist in the kinds of errors that the verse so searchingly exposes.",
  "155": "“Will you not then be mindful?” This verse poses a question that recurs throughout the Quran as a challenge to the heedless. After the exposure of the pagans’ unreasonable beliefs and the challenge to their judgment, the verse asks whether they will not become mindful. The question is an invitation to wake up, to pay attention, to let the truth that has been presented produce the awareness that the pagans currently lack.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its identification of what is lacking. The pagans’ problem was not a lack of information; the truth about God had been clearly presented. Their problem was a lack of mindfulness, of the awareness and attention that would allow the truth to penetrate and to transform. The question identifies this lack and invites its correction. Will you not become mindful? The question places the responsibility on the pagans: the mindfulness is available, if they will choose it.\n\nThe word “then” connects the question to what has preceded. After the exposure of the inconsistencies, the fabrications, and the flawed judgment, the question asks whether all of this will finally produce mindfulness. The “then” suggests that the preceding arguments should be sufficient to awaken the pagans, that the truth has been presented clearly enough, and that the only remaining question is whether they will let it do its work.\n\nMindfulness, in the Quranic sense, is the reverent awareness of God that shapes thought and conduct. The question asks whether the pagans will move from their heedless, fabricated theology to the mindfulness that recognizes God as He truly is: one, unique, without children, the Creator and Lord of all. The mindfulness would correct the fabrications, heal the flawed judgment, and restore the pagans to a right relationship with the truth about God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a searching challenge to mindfulness and a call to let the truth produce its proper effect in our lives. It invites us to recognize that the truth about God has been presented, that the question is whether we will be mindful of it, and that mindfulness is the gateway to a right relationship with God. The verse challenges us to examine our own mindfulness, to ensure that we are awake to the truth rather than heedless, and to allow the clear presentation of God’s nature and claims to produce in us the reverent awareness that corrects error, transforms conduct, and draws us near to the One about whom the truth has been so clearly spoken.",
  "156": "“Or do you have any compelling proof?” This verse poses another question to the pagans, challenging the basis of their claims. After asking whether they will be mindful, the verse asks whether they have any compelling proof for their beliefs about God. The question expects the answer no. The pagans had no proof, no evidence, no revelation to support their claims about God’s children. Their beliefs were based on fabrication, not on proof.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the groundlessness of the pagan claims. The pagans spoke confidently about God’s nature, attributing children to Him, but they had no proof to support their claims. The question challenges them to produce the proof, knowing that they cannot. The challenge exposes the emptiness of their position: confident assertions without any evidence to back them.\n\nThe phrase “compelling proof” sets a high standard. The question does not ask for a vague possibility or a weak argument but for compelling proof, evidence that would force agreement, that would establish the claim beyond doubt. The pagans could not produce such proof, because their claims were false, and false claims cannot be supported by compelling evidence. The demand for compelling proof exposes the weakness of the pagan position by showing that it cannot meet the standard that truth would meet.\n\nThe question is part of a series of challenges that the chapter directs at the pagans. Having exposed the inconsistency of their claims, the flaw in their judgment, and the need for mindfulness, the verse now challenges the evidential basis of their beliefs. The cumulative effect of the challenges is devastating: the pagan position is shown to be inconsistent, unreasonable, heedless, and unsupported by proof. The thoroughness of the refutation leaves the pagans without any ground on which to stand.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a challenge to ground our beliefs about God in proof rather than in fabrication and a call to examine the evidential basis of what we claim. It invites us to recognize that claims about God must be supported by compelling evidence, by revelation from God Himself, and that beliefs based on anything less are groundless. The verse challenges us to examine the basis of our own beliefs, to seek the compelling proof that God has provided through His revelation, and to refuse to hold or propagate claims about God that lack such proof, knowing that the God who is the source of all truth has provided, in His revelation, the evidence that those who sincerely seek Him need in order to believe what is true and to reject what is false.",
  "157": "“Then bring us your scripture, if what you say is true!” This verse issues a direct challenge to the pagans. Having asked whether they have compelling proof, the verse now demands that they produce their scripture, if their claims are true. The challenge is devastating because the pagans had no scripture. They had no revealed book that supported their claims about God’s children. Their beliefs were based on nothing more than invention and tradition, not on any divine revelation.\n\nThe significance of the challenge lies in its exposure of the absence of any revealed support for the pagan claims. The true religion, throughout history, has been supported by revelation, by scriptures given through prophets. The pagans had no such scripture. Their claims about God were not backed by any divine communication. The challenge to produce their scripture exposes this absence and reveals that their beliefs, however confidently held, had no divine authorization.\n\nThe condition, “if what you say is true,” is pointed. The challenge is conditional: produce the scripture, if you are truthful. The condition implies that the pagans are not truthful, that their claims are false, and that they cannot produce the scripture because no scripture supports them. The condition turns the challenge into an exposure: the inability to produce the scripture proves the falsity of the claims.\n\nThe demand for a scripture reflects the Quranic understanding of how religious truth is established. Truth about God is not invented by human beings but revealed by God through His messengers, and the revelation is preserved in scriptures. The pagans’ claims, lacking any scriptural support, were therefore not genuine religious truth but human invention. The demand invites the pagans to recognize that their beliefs lack the only foundation that could make them true: divine revelation.\n\nThe challenge also implies a contrast with the Quran itself. The Quran is a revealed scripture, given through a prophet, supporting its claims about God with divine authority. The pagans’ claims have no such support. The contrast highlights the difference between revealed truth and invented error, between the scripture that God has given and the fabrications that human beings devise. The challenge places the two side by side and asks the pagans to consider which has the authority that genuine religious truth requires.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a challenge to ground our religious beliefs in revelation rather than in invention and a reminder that genuine truth about God comes through the scriptures He has given. It invites us to recognize that claims about God must be supported by divine revelation, that beliefs lacking such support are mere invention, and that the Quran, as a revealed scripture, provides the authoritative basis for knowing the truth about God. The verse challenges us to test all religious claims against the revelation God has provided, to reject those that lack scriptural support, and to hold fast to the truth that God has made known through the scriptures and the messengers He has sent.",
  "158": "“They have also established a marital relationship between Him and the jinn.” This verse exposes another of the pagans’ false claims about God. Not content with attributing children to Him, they also established a marital relationship between God and the jinn, the unseen beings created by God. The claim compounded the error, weaving an ever more elaborate and ever more false web of assertions about God’s nature and relationships.\n\nThe significance of the exposure lies in its revelation of the pagans’ willingness to fabricate extensively about God. Their errors were not limited to a single false claim but formed a system of interconnected falsehoods. Having claimed that God had children, they now claimed a marital relationship between God and the jinn, building a whole mythology around their fundamental errors. The extensiveness of the fabrication reveals the depth of their departure from the truth.\n\nThe absurdity of the claim is striking. The jinn are created beings, part of the creation, not partners of the Creator. To establish a marital relationship between the Creator and any part of the creation is to confuse the fundamental distinction between God and what God has made. The claim drags God down to the level of the creation, subjecting Him to relationships and processes that belong to the created order. The absurdity reveals how far the pagan imagination had strayed from any reverent understanding of who God is.\n\n“Yet the jinn themselves know well that such people will certainly be brought for punishment.” The verse adds a remarkable detail: the jinn, the very beings whom the pagans linked to God, know that the pagans will be brought for punishment. The jinn, being part of the unseen realm, possess a knowledge that the pagans lack. They recognize the falsity of the pagan claims and the certainty of the consequences. The detail adds an ironic dimension to the exposure: the beings the pagans invoked in their false theology knew the theology was false and knew the fate that awaited its propagators.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a further exposure of the pagans’ fabricated theology and a reminder of the seriousness of making false claims about God. It invites us to recognize that elaborate systems of religious error are still error, that confusing the Creator with the creation is a fundamental distortion, and that false claims about God carry consequences that even the unseen beings recognize. The verse challenges us to ensure that our understanding of God is reverent and true, grounded in revelation rather than in the kind of elaborate fabrication that the pagans constructed, and to remember that the truth about God is not whatever we imagine but what He has revealed, and that departing from the revealed truth, however elaborately, leads to the very consequences that even the jinn, in their knowledge, recognize as certain.",
  "159": "“Glorified is Allah far above what they claim!” This verse is a declaration of God’s perfection and transcendence in response to the false claims just exposed. Glorified is Allah, far above what they claim. The declaration exalts God above the fabrications of the pagans, affirming that He is utterly unlike the false picture they have constructed. The glorification both honors God and repudiates the errors.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its affirmation of God’s transcendence. God is far above the claims of the pagans. He is not as they describe Him. He has no children, no marital relationships, no likeness to the creation. He is wholly other, wholly pure, wholly beyond the distortions that human invention has imposed on His nature. The declaration affirms the gap between the true God and the false gods of human fabrication, a gap that no amount of invention can bridge.\n\nThe word “glorified” carries the sense of declaring God’s perfection, of affirming that He is free from every imperfection and every false attribution. To glorify God is to affirm what He is and to deny what He is not. In this context, the glorification specifically denies the pagans’ claims: God is far above having children, far above marital relationships with the jinn, far above any of the fabrications that have been exposed. The glorification restores the true understanding of God by clearing away the false.\n\nThe phrase “far above” emphasizes the distance between God and the pagan claims. The claims do not merely misrepresent God slightly; they fall vastly short of the truth. God is not close to what the pagans claim but infinitely far above it. The distance underscores the magnitude of the error and the perfection of the truth. The true God is so far above the fabricated gods that comparison is impossible.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to glorify God by affirming His true nature and rejecting false claims about Him. It invites us to recognize that God is far above anything we could invent, that His nature is pure and perfect, free from the distortions of human imagination, and that glorifying Him includes both affirming what He is and denying what He is not. The verse challenges us to hold a reverent and accurate understanding of God, to glorify Him by rejecting the fabrications that misrepresent Him, and to join the declaration that Allah is far above every false claim, honoring Him with the truth about His nature rather than dishonoring Him with the inventions that the verse so clearly repudiates.",
  "160": "“But not the chosen servants of Allah.” This verse introduces the exception that recurs throughout the chapter. The consequences that will befall the fabricators, those who make false claims about God, will not touch the chosen servants of Allah. They are the exception, the minority who, unlike the fabricating majority, hold the truth about God, glorify Him as He deserves, and refuse the inventions that misrepresent Him. The exception brings hope into a passage of warning.\n\nThe significance of the exception lies in its consistency. It has appeared after the stories of Noah, Abraham, Moses, and Elias, and it appears again here, after the exposure of the pagans’ fabricated theology. The repetition establishes the pattern: in every generation, amid the erring majority, there is a faithful minority, the chosen servants of Allah, who escape the common fate. Their existence proves that error is not inevitable, that the truth can be held, that salvation is available to those who turn to God.\n\nThe phrase “chosen servants of Allah” identifies the saved minority with precision. They are servants, living in God’s service and honoring Him with truth rather than fabrication. And they are chosen, selected by God for His favor, through His grace. The combination describes the believer: one who serves God truly and who has been graciously chosen by Him. This group, the verse declares, will not share the fate of the fabricators.\n\nThe placement of the exception, immediately after the declaration of God’s glorification, is significant. The chosen servants are those who join in the glorification, who affirm God’s true nature, who reject the false claims. Their identity as chosen servants is tied to their holding of the truth about God. They do not fabricate; they glorify. They do not invent; they receive and affirm what God has revealed. Their faithfulness to the truth is part of what makes them the chosen servants, exempt from the fate of the fabricators.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a message of hope and a call to join the saved minority by holding the truth about God. It invites us to recognize that the consequences of fabricating about God are real, but that they are not inevitable, that there is a way of escape through the holding of the truth and the service of God, and that the chosen servants of Allah are the proof that salvation is possible even in a fabricating world. The verse challenges us to take our place among the chosen servants, glorifying God with truth rather than dishonoring Him with invention, so that we are not among the fabricators who face consequence but among the servants who are spared it and welcomed into God’s favor.",
  "161": "“Surely you pagans and whatever idols you worship.” This verse begins a statement about the pagans and their idols that will be completed in the following verses. It addresses the pagans directly, naming them along with the objects of their worship. The pairing of the worshippers and the worshipped sets up the point that will follow: the inability of either to cause real harm or to lead astray except those already destined for ruin.\n\nThe significance of the address lies in its directness. The verse speaks to the pagans, not about them in the third person but to them in the second. The directness confronts them with the truth about themselves and their idols, removing any distance that might soften the confrontation. The pagans are addressed as those who worship idols, and their idols are named alongside them, exposed as what they are: objects of worship that have no real power.\n\nThe phrase “whatever idols you worship” is comprehensive. It does not name a specific idol but encompasses all the objects of pagan worship. The comprehensiveness reflects the universal nature of the Quran’s critique of idolatry: whatever form the idols take, whatever names they bear, they are all equally powerless, equally false, equally unable to help or harm. The verse addresses the pagans in their idolatry, whatever its specific form, and prepares to expose the impotence of all their objects of worship.\n\nThe pairing of the pagans and their idols is significant. The two are named together because they share a common condition: both are powerless to accomplish what the pagans attribute to them. The pagans cannot harm the believers, and the idols cannot help the pagans. The pairing sets up the exposure of this shared powerlessness, which the following verses will make explicit.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a direct confrontation of idolatry and a preparation for the exposure of its powerlessness. It invites us to recognize that idolatry, in all its forms, involves the worship of powerless objects, and that both the worshippers and the worshipped are unable to accomplish what the worship assumes. The verse challenges us to examine our own objects of trust and worship, to ensure that we direct our devotion only to the One who has real power, and to recognize the powerlessness of every idol, however revered, that competes for the devotion that belongs to God alone, as the following verses will make unmistakably clear.",
  "162": "“Can never lure anyone away from Him.” This verse completes part of the thought begun in the previous verse. The pagans and their idols can never lure anyone away from God. The statement exposes the ultimate powerlessness of the forces of idolatry. Despite all their efforts, despite all their allurements, despite all the pressure they may bring to bear, they cannot, in the final analysis, lead astray anyone whom God has guided. Their power to mislead is limited and ultimately ineffective against the will and the protection of God.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its revelation of the limits of evil’s power. The pagans and their idols may scheme, may tempt, may pressure, may persecute. But they cannot, ultimately, lure away from God anyone whom God intends to keep. The statement sets a boundary around the power of falsehood: it may operate within limits, but it cannot override the protection that God grants to His faithful servants. The boundary is a comfort to the believers, who may face the pressure of idolatrous surroundings but who can trust that no amount of pressure can ultimately separate them from God.\n\nThe word “never” is absolute. The pagans and their idols will never, under any circumstances, succeed in luring away those whom God protects. The absoluteness underscores the completeness of God’s protection and the completeness of the idols’ impotence. The idols, for all the devotion paid to them, cannot accomplish the one thing that their worshippers might most want: the leading astray of the believers. Their powerlessness is total, not partial.\n\nThe phrase “away from Him” identifies the direction of the attempted luring. The pagans and their idols seek to draw people away from God, away from the worship of the One, away from the truth. Their entire effort is directed toward this separation. But the verse declares that this effort will fail. Those whom God holds cannot be drawn away, regardless of the means employed. The protection of God is stronger than all the allurements of idolatry.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful reassurance of God’s protection and a revelation of the limits of evil’s power. It invites us to recognize that the forces of falsehood, however active, cannot ultimately triumph over those whom God protects, and that our safety lies not in our own strength but in God’s keeping. The verse challenges us to trust the protection of God, to remain steadfast in His worship regardless of the pressures around us, and to take comfort in the knowledge that no idolatrous force, however formidable it may appear, can ever lure away from God the one whom He has chosen to keep, for the power of the idols is nothing and the power of God is everything.",
  "163": "“Except those destined to burn in Hell.” This verse completes the thought of the previous verses by acknowledging the one group that the pagans and their idols can lead astray: those destined for Hell. The exception is sobering. The forces of idolatry cannot lure away those whom God protects, but they can and do lead astray those who have already chosen the path that leads to Hell. The exception clarifies the scope of the idols’ influence while affirming the protection of the faithful.\n\nThe significance of the exception lies in its revelation of the relationship between human choice and misleading influence. The idols do not lead astray innocent victims who would otherwise be saved; they lead astray those who are already destined for Hell, those whose own choices have set them on the path to ruin. The misleading influence operates within the framework of the individual’s own chosen direction. Those who want to go astray find in the idols a means of doing so; those whom God protects are immune.\n\nThe phrase “destined to burn in Hell” identifies the group with sobering clarity. These are individuals whose trajectory, established by their own choices, leads to Hell. The idols become the means by which this trajectory is expressed, the vehicle through which those heading for ruin complete their journey. The idols do not create the destination; they facilitate the journey to a destination already chosen. The distinction preserves both the justice of God, who holds each person responsible for their own choices, and the reality of misleading influence, which operates within the framework of those choices.\n\nThe exception also serves to define the scope of the idols’ power. Their power is real but limited: it extends to those destined for Hell, but no further. They cannot reach those whom God protects. The limitation is itself a testimony to God’s sovereignty: even the power of falsehood operates only within the boundaries that God has set. The idols may lead astray, but only those who have already chosen the path of straying. Their power is permitted, bounded, and ultimately subordinate to the will of God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering acknowledgment of the reality of misleading influence and a reminder of the importance of our own chosen direction. It invites us to recognize that the forces of falsehood can influence those whose choices have set them on the path to ruin, but that they cannot touch those whom God protects, and that our safety lies in choosing, by God’s grace, the path that leads to Him rather than the path that leads to Hell. The verse challenges us to examine our own direction, to ensure that we are among the protected rather than among those destined for ruin, and to trust the protection of God, knowing that the idols can only lead astray those who have already chosen to be led, while those who cling to God are held fast by a power that no idolatrous force can overcome.",
  "164": "“There is not one of us without an assigned station of worship.” This verse records the words of the angels, spoken in response to the pagans’ false claims. The angels declare that each of them has an assigned station, a designated position in the worship and service of God. None of them is without a place; none is idle or purposeless. Every angel has been given a specific role in the continual worship and administration of the divine order. The declaration reveals the order and the purposefulness of the angelic realm.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its contrast with the pagan claims about the angels. The pagans had fabricated relationships between God and the angels, calling them God’s daughters and inventing marital connections. The angels’ own testimony corrects these fabrications. They are not God’s children or His partners; they are His servants, each assigned a station, each occupied in worship and service. The declaration replaces the pagan mythology with the truth about the angels’ nature and role.\n\nThe phrase “assigned station” suggests both order and assignment. The angels do not choose their own roles or wander without direction; they are assigned their stations by God. Each angel has a specific place and a specific function in the divine order. The assignment reflects the sovereignty of God over the angelic realm: He directs, and they serve, each in the place He has appointed.\n\nThe universality of the assignment is emphasized: “there is not one of us without an assigned station.” Every angel, without exception, has a role. The completeness of the assignment reveals the completeness of the divine order. Nothing in the angelic realm is wasted or purposeless; every angel contributes to the worship and the service of God according to a divine plan. The completeness stands in sharp contrast to the pagans’ chaotic and fabricated mythology.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a glimpse into the ordered, purposeful nature of the angelic realm and a correction of false beliefs about the angels. It invites us to recognize that the angels are servants of God, each assigned a specific role, not the mythological figures of pagan imagination, and that the unseen realm is characterized by order, purpose, and devotion rather than by the fanciful relationships that human invention has imposed on it. The verse challenges us to hold a reverent and accurate understanding of the unseen, recognizing the angels as servants occupied in worship, and to reject the fabrications that misrepresent their nature, allowing the angels’ own testimony, as recorded in the verse, to correct our understanding and to deepen our appreciation of the ordered devotion that characterizes the realm of those who serve God without ceasing.",
  "165": "“We are indeed the ones lined up in ranks for Allah.” This verse continues the angels’ testimony about their own nature and service. They declare that they are the ones lined up in ranks for Allah. The image of ranks conveys order, discipline, and coordination. The angels are not a disordered crowd but an organized host, arranged in ranks, each in his place, all serving together in perfect coordination. The orderliness of their service reflects the orderliness of the God they serve.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its revelation of the nature of angelic worship. The worship of the angels is not casual or sporadic but ordered and disciplined. They are lined up in ranks, suggesting both their multitude and their coordination. The ranks imply that the angels are many, organized into a structured host, and that their service is harmonious, each angel contributing to the whole according to his position. The picture is one of majestic, coordinated devotion.\n\nThe phrase “for Allah” identifies the object and the orientation of the angelic ranks. They are lined up for Allah, dedicated to His service, oriented toward His worship. The angels exist for God, and their entire being is given to His praise and service. The orientation stands in contrast to the pagans’ misuse of the angels in their mythology. The angels are not objects of worship or subjects of speculation; they are servants, lined up for Allah, wholly devoted to Him.\n\nThe word “indeed” emphasizes the certainty of the declaration. The angels affirm, with emphasis, that they are the ones lined up in ranks. The emphasis underscores the reality of the angelic service and contrasts it with the pagans’ fabrications. The angels know who they are and what they do, and their testimony corrects the false picture that the pagans have constructed. The certainty of the declaration leaves no room for the pagan mythology to stand.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the opening of the chapter is significant. The chapter began with an oath by the angels lined up in ranks. Now, near the end of the chapter, the angels themselves confirm that they are indeed the ones lined up in ranks. The confirmation closes a circle, connecting the chapter’s opening oath with the angels’ own testimony, and reinforcing the truth about the angels’ nature that the chapter has presented from its first verses.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful glimpse of the ordered worship of the angels and a reminder of the devotion that characterizes the unseen realm. It invites us to recognize the angels as a coordinated host, lined up in ranks for Allah, wholly devoted to His service, and to allow their example to inspire our own devotion. The verse challenges us to consider whether our own worship and service reflect something of the order, the coordination, and the wholehearted devotion that the angels display, knowing that the God whom the angels serve in ranks is the same God whom we are called to serve, and that our worship, however imperfect, joins the great chorus of the angelic host that serves Him without ceasing.",
  "166": "“And we are indeed the ones constantly glorifying His praise.” This verse completes the angels’ testimony about their service. Having declared that they are lined up in ranks, they now declare that they are the ones constantly glorifying God’s praise. The worship of the angels is not only ordered but continual. They glorify God without ceasing, declaring His perfection and His worthiness of praise as their unending occupation. The constancy of their glorification reveals the depth of their devotion and the greatness of the God they serve.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its revelation of the ceaseless nature of angelic worship. The angels do not worship occasionally or when they feel inclined; they worship constantly, without interruption. Their glorification of God is their permanent activity, the unending expression of their nature as servants of the Most High. The constancy stands in contrast to the intermittent, often distracted worship of human beings, and it reveals the ideal toward which our own devotion should aspire.\n\nThe phrase “glorifying His praise” combines two related acts: glorifying God, declaring His perfection and greatness, and offering Him praise, acknowledging His goodness and His worthiness. The combination is comprehensive, covering both who God is, His glory, and what He does, His praiseworthy acts. The angels’ worship addresses both aspects, declaring God’s perfection and thanking Him for His works. The comprehensiveness of their worship reflects the comprehensiveness of their knowledge of God.\n\nThe word “constantly” is key. The angels glorify God not sometimes but always. Their worship has no beginning and no end, no breaks and no interruptions. The constancy reflects both the nature of the angels, who are made for worship, and the nature of God, who is worthy of unceasing praise. The God who deserves constant glorification is served by beings whose entire existence is given to offering it.\n\nThe connection to the broader chapter is significant. The chapter has presented the angels as those who drive the clouds, who recite the Reminder, who are lined up in ranks. Now it adds that they are constantly glorifying God’s praise. The accumulation of descriptions builds a complete picture of the angelic realm: ordered, active, devoted, and ceaselessly worshipful. The picture corrects the pagan mythology and reveals the true nature of the beings whom the pagans had misrepresented.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful glimpse of the ceaseless worship of the angels and a call to deepen our own devotion. It invites us to recognize that the angels glorify God constantly, without interruption, and that their worship is the model toward which our own should aspire. The verse challenges us to cultivate a more constant awareness of God, a more continual offering of praise, knowing that while our worship will never match the ceaseless devotion of the angels, it can grow toward it, and that every act of glorification, however small, joins the great, unending chorus of the angelic host that constantly declares the praise of the God whom we and they are privileged to serve.",
  "167": "“They certainly used to say.” This verse introduces a quotation of what the pagan deniers used to say. The “they” refers to the pagans, and the verse begins to record a claim they used to make. The introduction sets up an exposure of the pagans’ inconsistency, which the following verses will develop. The pagans said one thing in the past, but their actions in the present contradicted it.\n\nThe significance of the introduction lies in its preparation for the exposure that follows. The Quran often exposes the inconsistency of the opponents of truth by quoting their own words and then contrasting them with their actions. The pagans, the following verses will show, used to claim that if they had received a Reminder like earlier peoples, they would have been devoted servants of God. But now that a Reminder had actually come to them, they rejected it. The introduction sets up this devastating contrast between their past claim and their present conduct.\n\nThe word “certainly” emphasizes that the pagans did indeed used to say what follows. The claim was not a one-time utterance but a repeated assertion, something the pagans said regularly. The certainty of the quotation removes any possibility of denial: the pagans cannot disown the words that are about to be quoted, because they were their own, spoken certainly and repeatedly.\n\nThe timing, “used to say,” places the claim in the past, before the coming of the Prophet and the Quran. The pagans made this claim during the period when they had not yet received a warner, when they could comfortably assert what they would do if one came. The claim was easy to make in the abstract, before the actual test arrived. The following verses will show how the claim collapsed when the test actually came.\n\nThe verse serves as a bridge between the testimony of the angels, which has just concluded, and the exposure of the pagans, which is about to begin. The angels declare their faithful service; the pagans are about to be exposed for their unfaithfulness, despite their claims to the contrary. The contrast between the angels’ actual devotion and the pagans’ claimed-but-unfulfilled devotion sharpens the exposure and highlights the gap between word and deed that characterized the pagans’ religion.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a preparation for an exposure of inconsistency and a reminder that our claims will be tested by our actions. It invites us to recognize that what we say we would do in hypothetical situations may be very different from what we actually do when the situations arise, and that the test of our devotion is not our claims but our conduct. The verse challenges us to examine the consistency between our words and our deeds, to ensure that we are not, like the pagans, making claims of devotion that our actions contradict, and to let the exposure that follows serve as a warning against the gap between profession and practice that ruined the pagans and that can ruin anyone whose actions do not match their words.",
  "168": "“If only we had a Reminder like those of earlier peoples.” This verse records the claim that the pagans used to make. They said that if only they had a Reminder, a scripture or a warning, like the ones given to earlier peoples, they would believe. The claim presented their unbelief as the result of a lack of guidance rather than as a choice. They implied that they were ready to believe, even eager to believe, if only the guidance were available.\n\nThe significance of the claim lies in its self-serving nature. By attributing their unbelief to the absence of a Reminder, the pagans excused themselves and shifted the blame. They were not unbelievers by choice, they implied, but by circumstance: they simply had not received the guidance that earlier peoples had received. The claim allowed them to maintain a positive self-image while continuing in their unbelief. It was a convenient position, placing the responsibility for their condition on God rather than on themselves.\n\nThe phrase “like those of earlier peoples” reveals the pagans’ awareness that earlier generations had received guidance. The pagans knew that God had sent Reminders to previous communities through earlier prophets. Their claim was that they deserved the same, and that if they received it, they would respond rightly. The claim acknowledged the pattern of divine guidance while using it as an excuse for their own lack of response to the guidance that was, in fact, already on its way.\n\nThe claim is deeply ironic, as the following verses will show. The pagans asserted that they would believe if they had a Reminder. But when the Reminder actually came, in the form of the Quran through the Prophet, they rejected it. The claim was exposed as empty: the pagans did not really want a Reminder; they wanted an excuse for their unbelief. When the excuse was removed by the arrival of the Reminder, their true disposition was revealed, and it was one of rejection, not of belief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the self-serving nature of excuses for unbelief and a warning against the gap between claimed readiness and actual response. It invites us to recognize that the claim “I would believe if only” is often a cover for an unwillingness to believe, and that the real test of our readiness is our response to the guidance that actually comes. The verse challenges us to examine our own response to God’s guidance, to ensure that we are not, like the pagans, claiming a readiness we do not possess, and to receive the Reminder that God has, in fact, given us, rather than using its hypothetical absence as an excuse for the unbelief that flows, not from a lack of guidance, but from a heart that has not truly chosen to believe.",
  "169": "“We would have truly been Allah’s devoted servants.” This verse completes the pagans’ claim. They said that if they had a Reminder like those of earlier peoples, they would have truly been Allah’s devoted servants. The claim was bold: not merely would they have believed, but they would have been devoted servants, wholly given to God’s service. The claim presented them as ready for the highest level of devotion, lacking only the opportunity.\n\nThe significance of the claim lies in its ambition and its exposure. The pagans did not claim that they would have been marginal believers or occasional worshippers. They claimed they would have been devoted servants, a description that the Quran reserves for the highest caliber of faithfulness. The ambition of the claim made its subsequent exposure all the more devastating. When the Reminder came and they rejected it, the gap between their claimed devotion and their actual rejection was vast and undeniable.\n\nThe word “truly” is emphatic. The pagans claimed that they would have truly, genuinely, really been God’s devoted servants. The emphasis reveals the confidence with which they made the claim. They were certain, or claimed to be certain, that they would have responded with genuine devotion. The emphasis makes the later exposure sharper: the claim was made with such confidence, yet it proved so empty.\n\nThe phrase “devoted servants” describes the ideal relationship between God and the believer. The pagans claimed they would have occupied this ideal position, wholly given to God’s service, if only they had received the Reminder. The claim borrowed the language of the highest devotion to describe what they would have done, presenting themselves as potential saints whose only lack was opportunity. The borrowing of this language made the claim sound pious while its emptiness made it hollow.\n\nThe exposure that follows turns the claim on its head. The pagans said they would have been devoted servants if they had a Reminder. But when the Reminder came, they rejected it. The rejection proved that the claim was false: the pagans were not potential devoted servants lacking only opportunity; they were actual rejecters whose claim of readiness was a fiction. The gap between the claimed devotion and the actual rejection reveals the true condition of their hearts, which was not devotion but resistance, however disguised by pious claims.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful exposure of the gap between claimed devotion and actual conduct and a warning against the self-deception that such claims involve. It invites us to recognize that claims of what we would do if circumstances were different are often covers for what we are unwilling to do in the circumstances we have, and that the real measure of our devotion is not our hypothetical readiness but our actual response to the guidance God has given. The verse challenges us to examine the sincerity of our own devotion, to ensure that it is genuine and not merely claimed, and to respond to the Reminder we have received with the actual devoted service that the pagans only claimed they would offer, knowing that the test of devotion is always the present response, not the hypothetical claim.",
  "170": "“But now they reject it, so they will soon know.” This verse delivers the devastating exposure of the pagans’ claim. They had said that if they had a Reminder, they would have been devoted servants. But now that the Reminder has come, they reject it. The “but” marks the contrast between the claim and the reality. The claim was empty; the reality is rejection. And because of the rejection, they will soon know, experiencing the consequences of their refusal.\n\nThe significance of the exposure lies in its directness and its timing. The verse does not argue or persuade; it simply states the fact: they reject it. The fact itself is the refutation of their claim. They said they would believe if they had a Reminder; they have a Reminder; they reject it. The exposure is complete in a single sentence, because the contradiction between the claim and the conduct is so obvious that it needs no elaboration.\n\nThe word “now” is pointed. The pagans had said what they would do in a hypothetical future. Now, in the actual present, the Reminder has arrived, and their response is rejection. The “now” brings the hypothetical claim into contact with the actual test, and the contact exposes the claim as false. The pagans cannot hide behind their hypothetical any longer; the test has come, and they have failed it.\n\nThe phrase “so they will soon know” announces the consequence of the rejection. The pagans will soon know, through experience, the reality of what they have rejected and the consequences of their refusal. The “soon” suggests that the knowledge is not far off, that the consequences, whether in this life or the next, are approaching. The announcement is both a warning and a statement of certainty: the knowledge will come, and when it comes, it will confirm the truth that the pagans now reject.\n\nThe verse completes the exposure that began several verses earlier. The pagans’ claim of hypothetical devotion has been tested by the actual arrival of the Reminder and has been found empty. Their rejection of the Reminder, which they claimed they would embrace, proves that their claim was a fiction. The consequence of the rejection, the certain coming knowledge, will seal the exposure, as the pagans discover, too late, the reality of what they denied and the emptiness of the claim with which they had excused their unbelief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering exposure of the gap between claimed readiness and actual rejection and a warning about the consequences of refusing the truth when it comes. It invites us to recognize that the test of our devotion is always the present response to the guidance we have received, and that claims of what we would do under different circumstances are meaningless if we reject the guidance that is actually before us. The verse challenges us to receive the Reminder with the devotion the pagans only claimed, to ensure that our response to God’s guidance matches our profession, and to heed the warning that those who reject the truth, however piously they may have claimed they would embrace it, will soon know the reality and the consequences of what they have refused.",
  "171": "“Our Word has already gone forth to Our servants, the messengers.” This verse states a decree that God has already issued regarding His messengers. The Word has gone forth, meaning the decree has been established, fixed, made certain. The decree concerns the messengers, God’s servants whom He has sent to convey His guidance. The verse prepares for the content of the decree, which the following verses will reveal: that the messengers will be helped and that God’s forces will prevail.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its revelation of God’s prior decree regarding the messengers. The outcome of the messengers’ missions is not uncertain or contingent. It has already been decreed. The Word has gone forth. Whatever opposition the messengers face, whatever rejection they encounter, the final outcome has been settled in advance by divine decree. The messengers will not ultimately fail, because God has already determined their success.\n\nThe phrase “Our servants, the messengers” identifies the recipients of the decree. The messengers are, first and foremost, servants. Their exalted role as messengers does not remove them from the fundamental identity of servants of God. They are servants who have been entrusted with a message, and the decree that has gone forth concerns them in their capacity as both servants and messengers. The double identification honors their devotion and their mission.\n\nThe timing, “has already gone forth,” emphasizes the antiquity and the certainty of the decree. The decision to help the messengers was not made recently or in response to circumstances. It is an established decree, part of the eternal plan of God. The “already” underscores the settled nature of the decree: it is not subject to revision or reversal. What God has decreed will come to pass, regardless of the opposition it faces.\n\nThe verse serves as a reassurance to the Prophet and to all who carry God’s message. The opposition they face is real, but it is not the final word. The final word has already gone forth: the messengers will be helped. The reassurance is grounded not in optimistic hope but in divine decree, the most secure foundation possible. Those who serve God’s message can take comfort in knowing that the outcome of their service has already been settled by the One who sent them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful reassurance of the certainty of God’s help for His messengers and a call to trust the decree that has gone forth. It invites us to recognize that the success of God’s messengers is not in doubt, that it has been decreed from before, and that the opposition they face, however fierce, cannot ultimately prevail against a decree that has already gone forth. The verse challenges us to take our place, in whatever way we can, on the side of God’s messengers and their message, knowing that the side of the decreed victory is the side that will ultimately triumph, and that the Word of God, once gone forth, returns not empty but accomplishing the purpose for which it was sent.",
  "172": "“That they would surely be helped.” This verse reveals the content of the decree: the messengers would surely be helped. The help is certain, marked by the emphatic “surely.” The messengers, who face opposition, rejection, and persecution in the course of their missions, are assured of divine help. The help may come in various forms and at various times, but it is certain, because it has been decreed.\n\nThe significance of the help lies in its source and its certainty. The help comes from God, the Almighty, whose power is greater than any opposition the messengers might face. And the help is certain, decreed from before, not dependent on circumstances or on the strength of the messengers. The combination of divine source and decreed certainty makes the help the most secure foundation possible for the messengers’ work. Whatever they face, they will be helped.\n\nThe word “surely” is emphatic. The help is not a possibility or a probability but a certainty. The emphasis underscores the reliability of the decree and the faithfulness of God to His messengers. Those whom God sends, God helps. The help is part of the arrangement of prophethood: the messenger delivers the message, and God provides the help. The two go together, and neither can be separated from the other.\n\nThe timing of the help is significant. The decree states that the messengers “would” be helped, suggesting that the help comes in God’s time, not necessarily immediately. The messengers may face periods of difficulty, opposition, and apparent failure before the help arrives. But the help is certain, whenever it comes. The timing belongs to God’s wisdom; the certainty belongs to God’s decree. The messengers can endure the waiting because they know that the help, however delayed, is sure.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful reassurance of God’s help for His messengers and, by extension, for all who serve His cause. It invites us to recognize that those who carry God’s message are not abandoned to face opposition alone, that divine help is certain for them, decreed from before, and that the help, however it comes and whenever it arrives, is sufficient. The verse challenges us to take courage in whatever work God has given us, to trust the decree of help, and to persevere through difficulty, knowing that the same God who decreed help for the messengers helps all who serve Him faithfully, and that the help, though it may tarry, is as certain as the Word that decreed it.",
  "173": "“And that Our forces will certainly prevail.” This verse extends the decree to include the ultimate victory of God’s forces. Not only will the messengers be helped, but God’s forces will certainly prevail. The victory is certain, marked by the emphatic “certainly.” The forces of God, whether understood as the angels, the believers, or the divine power itself, will triumph over all opposition. The decree settles the outcome of the cosmic struggle between truth and falsehood.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its universality and its certainty. The victory is not limited to one messenger or one time but extends to all of God’s forces, in every age. And the victory is certain, decreed from before, not dependent on the balance of earthly power. The combination of universality and certainty provides the most comprehensive assurance possible: God’s forces, whoever and wherever they are, will prevail, because the victory has been decreed.\n\nThe phrase “Our forces” is significant. God has forces, whether seen or unseen, whether human or angelic, that work toward the accomplishment of His purposes. These forces are engaged in the struggle between truth and falsehood, and the decree assures their victory. The mention of forces reminds us that the struggle is real, that there is opposition, and that the victory is won through engagement, not through passivity. The forces of God prevail because they fight, with God’s help, against the forces of error.\n\nThe word “prevail” conveys the completeness of the victory. God’s forces do not merely survive or hold their ground; they prevail, they triumph, they overcome. The prevailing is decisive, establishing the truth and defeating the falsehood. The completeness of the victory reflects the completeness of God’s power and the certainty of His decree. Whatever the appearance of things in the short term, the long-term outcome is settled: God’s forces prevail.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful assurance of the ultimate victory of God’s forces and a call to align ourselves with the side that will prevail. It invites us to recognize that the struggle between truth and falsehood has a certain outcome, decreed by God, and that those who align themselves with God’s forces align themselves with the winning side. The verse challenges us to examine which forces we serve, to ensure that we are among God’s forces rather than among the forces of error, and to take courage in the certainty of the prevailing, knowing that the struggle, however difficult in the present, leads to a victory that has already been decreed and that no opposition can ultimately prevent.",
  "174": "“So turn away from the deniers for a while, O Prophet.” This verse gives the Prophet an instruction based on the decree just stated. Since God’s forces will certainly prevail, the Prophet can turn away from the deniers for a while. The turning away is not a surrender or an abandonment of the mission but a temporary disengagement, a stepping back from the futile effort to persuade those who have determined to deny. The “for a while” indicates the temporary nature of the turning away.\n\nThe significance of the instruction lies in its grounding in the decree. The Prophet is told to turn away from the deniers because their fate is already settled. The messengers will be helped; God’s forces will prevail. Given this certainty, the Prophet need not exhaust himself in trying to persuade the implacable deniers. He can turn away, leaving them to the consequences that the decree has established, and focus his efforts elsewhere, on those who are more receptive.\n\nThe phrase “turn away” suggests a deliberate disengagement. The Prophet had been actively engaging the deniers, presenting the truth, answering their objections, enduring their hostility. The instruction tells him to turn away, to cease the active engagement, to leave the deniers to their chosen course. The turning away is an act of wisdom, recognizing the futility of further effort with those who have rejected every appeal.\n\nThe temporary nature, “for a while,” is important. The turning away is not permanent. It is for a season, a defined period, after which the situation may change. The temporariness keeps the door open for future engagement while providing relief from the present futility. The Prophet is not abandoning the deniers forever but stepping back for a while, trusting that the decree will accomplish what his efforts could not.\n\nThe instruction offers a measure of relief to the Prophet, who had been laboring intensively against stubborn opposition. The turning away allows him to conserve his energy, to focus on more receptive audiences, and to leave the deniers to the consequences that their rejection has earned. The relief is grounded in the decree: since God’s forces will prevail, the Prophet can afford to disengage from the hopeless effort to persuade the determined deniers.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a model of wisdom in the face of stubborn rejection and a call to recognize when disengagement is appropriate. It invites us to recognize that there are times when turning away from those who reject the truth is the wise course, that persistence with the implacable may be futile, and that our energy may be better spent on those who are more receptive. The verse challenges us to discern when to engage and when to turn away, to trust the decree that guarantees the ultimate victory of truth, and to find relief, as the Prophet did, in the knowledge that the outcome does not depend on our persuasiveness but on the decree of the God whose forces will certainly prevail.",
  "175": "“You will see what will happen to them, and they too will see!” This verse addresses the Prophet with a statement about the future. He will see what happens to the deniers, and they too will see. The statement points forward to a coming event, a reversal or a consequence that will be visible to both the Prophet and the deniers. The mutual seeing underscores the public nature of the coming reality: both sides will witness it, and the truth of the situation will be plain to all.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its announcement of a coming visible reality. The current situation, in which the deniers appear powerful and the Prophet appears weak, is temporary. A change is coming, a reversal that will be visible to all. The Prophet will see the fate of the deniers; the deniers will see their own fate. The seeing will confirm the truth that the Prophet has proclaimed and the error that the deniers have embraced.\n\nThe phrase “what will happen to them” suggests consequence. Something is going to happen to the deniers, something that the Prophet will witness. The vagueness of the phrase leaves the specific content to God’s wisdom, but the implication is clear: the consequence will be significant, it will be visible, and it will confirm the truth of the Prophet’s message. The deniers, who now mock and reject, will experience a reality that vindicates the Prophet and exposes their error.\n\nThe mutual seeing, “you will see, and they too will see,” emphasizes the shared witness. The coming reality will not be private or hidden but public, observed by all parties. The mutual seeing ensures that there will be no room for denial or for claiming that the consequence was other than it was. Both the Prophet and the deniers will see, and the seeing will settle the matter between them, confirming the truth and exposing the error for all to witness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an announcement of a coming visible vindication and a call to trust that the truth will be confirmed in time. It invites us to recognize that the present appearance of things is not the final reality, that a reversal is coming, and that the truth, however obscured in the present, will be made visible to all. The verse challenges us to trust the coming vindication, to persevere in the truth regardless of present appearances, and to leave the deniers to the seeing that will confirm what they now deny, knowing that the truth, proclaimed faithfully, will be vindicated visibly, and that both the bearers of truth and the deniers will witness the vindication that settles the matter between them once and for all.",
  "176": "“Do they really wish to hasten Our punishment?” This verse poses a searching question to the deniers. They had been challenging the Prophet to bring on the punishment he warned about, treating the delay as proof that the warning was empty. The verse asks whether they really wish to hasten the punishment. The question exposes the folly of their challenge. They are asking for something whose reality they do not grasp and whose severity they do not appreciate.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the deniers’ folly. To hasten punishment is the height of foolishness, yet the deniers, in their mockery, were doing exactly that. They treated the punishment as a bluff, something to be called, and they dared the Prophet to bring it on. The question reveals that they have no idea what they are asking for. The punishment they mock is real, and to hasten it is to bring destruction on themselves.\n\nThe word “really” conveys astonishment at the deniers’ attitude. Do they really, genuinely, with understanding, wish to hasten the punishment? The question implies that no one who understood the punishment would wish to hasten it. The deniers’ challenge was born not of understanding but of ignorance and bravado. They did not grasp the reality of what they were asking for, and the question exposes that lack of grasp.\n\nThe question also serves as a warning. By asking whether the deniers wish to hasten the punishment, the verse reminds them that the punishment is real and that its hastening is within God’s power. The challenge that the deniers intended as mockery could, in fact, be answered, and the answer would be far more terrible than they imagine. The question turns their mockery back on them, revealing the danger of their dare.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the folly of treating God’s punishment lightly and a call to take seriously the realities we are tempted to mock. It invites us to recognize that the punishment the deniers challenged is real, that to hasten it is foolishness, and that the delay is a mercy, not an evidence of emptiness. The verse challenges us to take seriously the warnings of God, to use the time of delay for repentance rather than for mockery, and to recognize that those who dare the punishment are daring a reality whose severity they do not grasp, and that the wisest response to the delay is gratitude for the mercy it represents and urgent preparation for the day it ends.",
  "177": "“Yet when it descends upon them: how evil will that morning be for those who had been warned!” This verse describes the moment when the punishment the deniers mocked finally arrives. When it descends upon them, the morning of its arrival will be evil, terrible, for those who had been warned. The verse captures the reversal: the deniers who mocked the warning will experience its fulfillment, and the experience will be evil beyond description.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its revelation of the reality that the deniers denied. The punishment they called a bluff will descend. The morning they thought would never come will arrive. And when it arrives, it will be evil, a day of terrible consequence for those who had been warned but who refused the warning. The description confirms the reality of the punishment and the folly of the deniers’ mockery.\n\nThe word “descends” is significant. The punishment comes down, from above, from God. The descent conveys the inevitability and the overwhelming nature of the punishment. It is not something that can be resisted or escaped; it descends, with the weight and the finality of a divine decree. When it descends, the deniers will have no defense, no refuge, no escape.\n\nThe phrase “how evil will that morning be” captures the dread of the moment. The morning, usually a time of hope and new beginning, will be evil for the deniers, a time of terrible consequence. The evil of the morning measures the severity of the punishment and the completeness of the reversal. The day that the deniers thought would never come will be the worst day they have ever experienced, the day when the warning they mocked becomes the reality they endure.\n\nThe phrase “for those who had been warned” is pointed. The punishment falls specifically on those who had been warned, those who had received clear warning and had chosen to ignore or mock it. The warning had given them every opportunity to turn, and they had refused. The punishment, when it comes, falls on people who had no excuse, who had been warned thoroughly and who had rejected the warning entirely. The justice of the punishment is established by the completeness of the warning that preceded it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering description of the reality of the punishment and a warning about the fate of those who ignore clear warning. It invites us to recognize that the punishment the deniers mocked is real, that its arrival will be terrible, and that the warning we have received places us in the same position as the deniers, with the same opportunity to turn and the same risk of refusing. The verse challenges us to take the warning seriously, to turn while there is time, and to ensure that the morning of the punishment does not find us among those who had been warned but who refused the warning, knowing that the evil of that morning is reserved for those who, despite clear warning, chose to deny.",
  "178": "“And turn away from them for a while.” This verse repeats, with slight variation, the instruction given earlier to the Prophet. He is again told to turn away from the deniers for a while. The repetition underscores the importance of the instruction and provides the Prophet with a renewed authorization to disengage from the stubborn deniers. The turning away, as before, is temporary, for a season, not a permanent abandonment.\n\nThe significance of the repetition lies in its reinforcement. The instruction to turn away has already been given, but the repetition emphasizes it, ensuring that the Prophet takes it seriously and acts on it. The repetition also occurs after the announcement of the coming punishment, placing the turning away in the context of the certain consequence. Since the punishment will come, the Prophet can turn away, trusting that the consequence will accomplish what his persuasion could not.\n\nThe phrase “for a while” again indicates the temporary nature of the disengagement. The Prophet is not to turn away permanently but for a defined period. The temporariness keeps the instruction balanced: disengage from the futile effort with the deniers, but do not abandon the mission entirely. The balance reflects wisdom: persistent engagement with the implacable is futile, but the mission as a whole continues, with attention directed toward more receptive audiences.\n\nThe instruction offers the Prophet relief from the burden of the stubborn deniers. The Prophet had been carrying the weight of their rejection, the effort of repeated persuasion, the pain of their mockery. The instruction to turn away releases him from that weight, at least for a season, allowing him to rest from the futile effort and to find more fruitful fields for his message. The relief is a mercy, acknowledging the difficulty of the Prophet’s task and providing a measure of respite.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of the wisdom of disengagement when persuasion is futile and a call to trust the consequences to God. It invites us to recognize that there are times when turning away is the wise and authorized course, that persistence with the implacable may be neither required nor fruitful, and that the Prophet himself was instructed to turn away for a while. The verse challenges us to discern when disengagement is appropriate, to trust the consequences to the God who sends them, and to find relief, as the Prophet did, in the permission to turn away from those who will not be persuaded, knowing that the truth does not depend on our persuasiveness and that the God who warned will, in His time, accomplish what our efforts could not.",
  "179": "“You will see, and they too will see!” This verse repeats the statement made earlier about the mutual seeing of the coming reality. The Prophet will see, and the deniers too will see. The repetition reinforces the certainty of the coming visible reversal and the public nature of the vindication. Both parties will witness the truth confirmed and the error exposed.\n\nThe significance of the repetition lies in its emphasis. The statement has already been made, but its repetition underscores its importance and its certainty. The coming reality is so certain, and its public witness so significant, that the statement is made twice. The repetition ensures that the Prophet and the deniers both grasp the point: a visible reversal is coming, and both will see it.\n\nThe mutual seeing, “you will see, and they too will see,” again emphasizes the shared witness. The coming reality will not be private but public, observed by all parties. The mutual seeing ensures that there will be no denial of the reality when it comes. Both the Prophet and the deniers will witness it, and the witnessing will settle the matter between them, confirming the truth and exposing the error beyond any possibility of dispute.\n\nThe placement of the repetition, after the instruction to turn away and the announcement of the coming punishment, gives the statement a particular force. The Prophet has been told to turn away from the deniers and to trust that a coming reality will vindicate the truth. The repetition of the mutual seeing assures him that the vindication is certain and that he will witness it. The assurance supports the turning away: the Prophet can disengage because he knows that the vindication is coming and that he will see it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reaffirmation of the certainty of the coming visible vindication and a call to trust it. It invites us to recognize that the truth will be confirmed visibly, that the reversal is certain, and that both the bearers of truth and the deniers will witness the confirmation. The verse challenges us to trust the coming vindication, to persevere in the truth regardless of present appearances, and to find in the certainty of the mutual seeing the strength to continue, knowing that the day is coming when the truth will be seen by all, and that those who bore it faithfully and those who denied it stubbornly will both witness the reality that settles the matter between them forever.",
  "180": "“Glorified is your Lord, the Lord of Honour and Power, above what they claim!” This verse is a declaration of God’s perfection and transcendence, serving as part of the closing doxology of the chapter. Glorified is the Lord, the Lord of Honour and Power, far above what the deniers claim. The declaration exalts God above the fabrications of the deniers, affirming His true nature and repudiating the false claims that have been made about Him throughout the chapter.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its comprehensive affirmation of God’s nature. God is the Lord of Honour and Power, possessing both the dignity that commands reverence and the might that accomplishes His will. The combination of honour and power presents a complete picture of God’s greatness: He is honored in His nature and powerful in His action. The declaration affirms this true nature over against the deniers’ false claims, which have misrepresented God throughout the chapter.\n\nThe word “glorified” carries the sense of declaring God’s perfection, of affirming that He is free from every imperfection and every false attribution. In this context, the glorification specifically exalts God above the deniers’ claims: above the attribution of children, above the fabricated relationships, above every distortion that human invention has imposed on His nature. The glorification restores the true understanding of God by clearing away the false.\n\nThe phrase “above what they claim” identifies the specific object of the glorification. God is far above the claims of the deniers. Their claims do not touch Him or define Him. He is wholly other than the picture they have constructed, wholly free from the errors they have propagated. The distance between God and the deniers’ claims is infinite: He is not slightly misrepresented but vastly above every false attribution, as different from the fabricated gods as the truth is from the lie.\n\nThe placement of the declaration, near the end of the chapter, is significant. The chapter has exposed the deniers’ false claims in detail: their attribution of daughters to God, their fabricated relationships, their groundless mythology. Now, in the closing doxology, the declaration of God’s glorification gathers up the refutation and presents it in the form of praise. God is glorified above all the claims, and the glorification both honors Him and completes the refutation of the errors.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to glorify God by affirming His true nature and a model of how to close reflection on God with praise. It invites us to recognize that God is the Lord of Honour and Power, far above every false claim, and that glorifying Him includes both affirming what He is and denying what He is not. The verse challenges us to hold a reverent and accurate understanding of God, to glorify Him above the distortions that human invention imposes, and to join the declaration that the Lord of Honour and Power is far above every claim that misrepresents Him, honoring Him with the truth about His nature as the chapter closes with the praise that such truth deserves.",
  "181": "“Peace be upon the messengers.” This verse declares peace upon the messengers, all of them, as part of the closing doxology of the chapter. The greeting of peace, which has been pronounced on individual prophets throughout the chapter, is now extended to all the messengers collectively. The comprehensive greeting gathers up the individual greetings and applies them to the entire company of those whom God has sent to convey His guidance.\n\nThe significance of the comprehensive greeting lies in its inclusiveness. Throughout the chapter, peace has been pronounced on Noah, Abraham, Moses, Aaron, and Elias individually. Now, in the closing doxology, the greeting is extended to all the messengers, encompassing not only those named in this chapter but all the prophets whom God has sent throughout history. The inclusiveness reflects the unity of the prophetic brotherhood and the equal honor that God bestows on all His messengers.\n\nThe word “peace” carries its full richness: safety, wholeness, well-being, the absence of every harm. To declare peace upon the messengers is to affirm their security in God’s favor, their honored place among the blessed, and the completeness of their reward. The greeting is both a statement about the messengers’ present state, they are in peace, and a blessing upon them, an affirmation that this peace endures.\n\nThe timing of the comprehensive greeting, in the closing doxology, is significant. Having told the stories of several messengers and pronounced peace on each, the chapter now closes by extending the greeting to all. The movement from the particular to the universal reflects the unity of the prophetic mission: though the messengers differed in time and circumstance, they shared the same mission and the same devotion, and they share the same greeting of peace from their Lord.\n\nThe greeting also serves as a model for the believers. Just as God declares peace upon His messengers, so the believers are called to honor the messengers, to receive their message, and to join, in spirit, in the greeting of peace. The greeting binds the believers to the messengers in a relationship of reverence, gratitude, and emulation, as the believers honor those whom God has honored and seek to walk in the path the messengers trod.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful comprehensive greeting to all God’s messengers and a call to honor them as God has honored them. It invites us to recognize the unity of the prophetic brotherhood, to join in the greeting of peace that God pronounces upon them, and to receive their message with the reverence that the greeting implies. The verse challenges us to honor all the messengers, to learn from all their examples, and to live in a way that reflects the devotion they exemplified, knowing that the peace God declares upon the messengers is the peace to which all the faithful are called, and that the God who greeted His messengers with peace greets with the same peace all who follow them in sincere faith.",
  "182": "“And praise be to Allah, Lord of all worlds.” This verse closes the chapter with a universal declaration of praise. All praise belongs to Allah, the Lord of all worlds. The declaration gathers up the whole chapter, with its stories of the prophets, its exposure of the deniers, its affirmations of God’s nature, and its declarations of peace, and offers it all as praise to the One who is the source and the Lord of everything.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its comprehensiveness. All praise belongs to God. Not some praise, not most praise, but all praise, from every quarter, for every reason, in every form. The universality of the praise reflects the universality of God’s lordship: since He is the Lord of all worlds, all praise from all worlds belongs to Him. The declaration gathers every possible expression of gratitude, reverence, and honor and directs it to the One who deserves it all.\n\nThe phrase “Lord of all worlds” identifies the God who is praised. He is not a local deity or a tribal god but the Lord of all worlds, the Creator and Sustainer of everything that exists. The universality of His lordship matches the universality of the praise: because He is Lord of all, all praise is His due. The identification places the praise in its proper context, directing it to the only One who is great enough to deserve it all.\n\nThe closing of the chapter with praise is fitting. The chapter has displayed God’s power, His justice, His mercy, His faithfulness to His messengers, and His transcendence above the false claims of the deniers. Having contemplated all of this, the fitting response is praise, the offering of honor and gratitude to the One whose greatness the chapter has revealed. The praise gathers up the contemplation and offers it back to God as worship.\n\nThe declaration also serves as a model for the reader. Having read the chapter, having contemplated the truths it presents, the fitting response is to join in the praise. The verse invites the reader to add his or her voice to the universal chorus, to offer praise to the Lord of all worlds, and to close the engagement with the chapter as the chapter itself closes, with the declaration that all praise belongs to Allah, the Lord of everything that exists.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a universal call to praise and a fitting close to the chapter. It invites us to recognize that all praise belongs to God, that He is the Lord of all worlds, and that the fitting response to the contemplation of His greatness is the offering of praise. The verse challenges us to join the universal chorus, to direct our praise to the One who deserves it all, and to live in a continual attitude of gratitude and reverence toward the Lord of all worlds, knowing that the chapter that has displayed God’s dealings with His prophets and His deniers closes, as all reflection on God should close, with the praise that His greatness demands and that His creatures owe."
};
